{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Apicella","name":"Apicella, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Isgrò","name":"Isgrò, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Pollastro","name":"Pollastro, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Prevete","name":"Prevete, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't stop me now: How Validation Criteria Affect Checkpoint Selection and Early Stopping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:56:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-27T13:12:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:35:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Checkpoint selection is a standard component of neural network training, yet the validation criterion used to select a checkpoint is often chosen heuristically. Moreover, the same criterion may be used either only to rank checkpoints after completion of a predefined training run or also to determine when training should stop, thereby affecting both the selected checkpoint and the set of checkpoints available for selection. In this work, we systematically investigate the role of validation criteria under these two settings. We separately vary the training loss, the validation criterion, and the target evaluation metric, and compare post-hoc checkpoint selection, in which training proceeds for all predefined epochs, with patience-based early stopping, in which the validation criterion also controls training termination. We consider three Cross-Entropy, C-Loss, and PolyLoss as training losses, and accuracy, macro-F1, and Matthews correlation coefficient as target metrics. Selection quality is assessed through the relative gap between the test performance of the validation-selected checkpoint and the best-observed test performance for the same target metric over the complete predefined training run."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumadeep","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Soumadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos M.","familyName":"Correa","name":"Correa, Carlos M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Fiorilli","name":"Fiorilli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés N.","familyName":"Ruiz","name":"Ruiz, Andrés N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dante J.","familyName":"Paz","name":"Paz, Dante J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro Pérez","familyName":"Fernández","name":"Fernández, Alejandro Pérez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel G.","familyName":"Sánchez","name":"Sánchez, Ariel G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CNN+FoF: application of deep learning to the identification of dark matter haloes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-16T17:15:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-28T07:55:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:24:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a deep-learning-based approach for identifying dark matter haloes in cosmological N-body simulations. Our framework consists of a volumetric Convolutional Neural Network to classify individual simulation particles as either halo or non-halo members, followed by a highly optimised and parallelised Friends-of-Friends clustering algorithm that groups the classified halo members into distinct haloes. The training data comprise simulations generated using GADGET-4, with labels obtained with the ROCKSTAR halo finder. Our models incorporate two main halo mass definitions, $M_{200\\mathrm{b}}$ and $M_{\\text{vir}}$, with similar performance. For haloes defined by the ROCKSTAR $M_{200\\mathrm{b}}$ criterion, the classification network demonstrated stable performance across multiple simulation resolutions. For the highest resolution, it achieved over $98\\%$ across all primary performance metrics when identifying halo particles. Furthermore, the FoF algorithm yielded halo catalogues with a purity generally exceeding $99\\%$ and a stable completeness of $94\\%$ for masses above $5\\times10^{11} \\, M_\\odot$. Our pipeline recovered the centre-of-mass positions, velocities and halo masses with high fidelity, yielding a halo mass function consistent to within $5\\%$ of the reference while faithfully reconstructing the internal density profiles. The primary objective of this study is to offer a faster and scalable alternative to conventional halo finders, achieving a speed-up of approximately one order of magnitude relative to ROCKSTAR. While traditional N-body simulations remain dominated by the time required to solve gravitational dynamics, this post-processing acceleration becomes essential when coupled with fast field-level emulators, offering a promising pathway towards efficient, end-to-end forward modelling in modern simulation-based inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 13 figures, accepted in MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoqi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhaoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huayi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junichiro","familyName":"Sugihara","name":"Sugihara, Junichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuniato","familyName":"Eugenio","name":"Eugenio, Cuniato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pantic","familyName":"Michael","name":"Michael, Pantic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moju","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Moju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning End-to-End Control for Omnidirectional Aerial Motion on Overactuated Tilt-rotor Quadrotors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:13:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:26:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-18T07:54:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While reinforcement learning (RL) has been successfully applied to conventional quadrotors for agile and robust flight, whether actuator-level RL can be reliably deployed on tilt-rotor aerial robots remains an open question, as the hybrid actuation coupling brushless rotors with rotational joints introduces a substantially harder sim-to-real gap. In this work, we propose an end-to-end RL framework for omnidirectional motion control on overactuated tilt-rotor quadrotors, directly mapping target poses to joint and rotor commands. The learning framework combines actuator-level simulation with an asymmetric actor-critic architecture for 6D pose-reaching. For reliable sim-to-real transfer on the hybrid actuation, we integrate system identification with minimal yet physically grounded domain randomization. The trained policy is deployed zero-shot on real hardware and evaluated across waypoint hovering, external disturbances, payload variation and trajectory tracking, together with simulated traversal of allocation-singular configurations. The policy is compared with a state-of-the-art NMPC baseline: NMPC attains lower steady-state position error, whereas the RL policy offers a more uniform orientation error across evaluations, transitions between poses faster, and requires less onboard computation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-21T02:25:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sokea","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Sokea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leanghok","familyName":"Hour","name":"Hour, Leanghok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sanghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Aniket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hee Chul","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Hee Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moon Jip","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Moon Jip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngsun","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Youngsun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Landscape-Similarity-Guided Optimization in Divide-and-Conquer QAOA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:46:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:35:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T06:50:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:37:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:16:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-16T00:48:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-17T00:18:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Divide-and-conquer strategies mitigate hardware constraints for the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) on Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices by partitioning large interaction graphs into smaller, hardware-compatible sub-problems. However, this approach introduces a severe classical training bottleneck: a decomposition across $m$ boundary nodes generates $2^m$ distinct sub-problems that typically require independent optimization. In this work, we demonstrate that across diverse synthetic and real-world interaction graphs, the variational landscapes of these reduced QAOA instances actually exhibit a robust universality. Adapting the replica-overlap framework of spin-glass physics, we define a landscape-overlap order parameter $q$ to quantify geometric correlations between energy landscapes, revealing a sharp landscape-similarity transition as graph connectivity is tuned. Exploiting this, we introduce Doubly Optimized QAOA (DO-QAOA), an adaptive pipeline that collapses the sub-problems from $2^m$ distinct sub-problems into $K=\\mathcal{O}(1)$ effective landscape classes. By performing optimization on a single representative sub-problem and dynamically transferring parameters to remaining sub-problems, DO-QAOA lowers runtime and quantum measurement overhead by orders of magnitude while maintaining a competitive Approximation Ratio Gap (ARG)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-17T02:43:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marwa R.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Marwa R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naima","familyName":"Kaabouch","name":"Kaabouch, Naima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Physics-Informed Neuro-Fuzzy Framework for Quantum Error Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T16:19:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T22:05:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:13:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T02:33:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-12T18:39:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:47:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As quantum processors scale beyond 100 qubits, distinguishing software bugs from stochastic hardware noise becomes a critical diagnostic challenge. We present a neuro-fuzzy framework that addresses this attribution problem by combining Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference Systems (ANFIS) with physics-grounded feature engineering. We introduce the Bhattacharyya veto, a hard constraint calibrated to the empirically characterized noise floor of the device, which classifies as buggy any output distribution whose divergence from the ideal exceeds what the device's noise produces under normal operation. The framework is validated on two IBM Heron r2 processors. On ibm_fez, across 105 circuits spanning 17 algorithm families, it attains 89.5% accuracy (Wilson 95% CI [82.2, 94.0]) with no abstentions; every residual error is a bug whose ideal computational-basis distribution is identical to the intended circuit's, or whose divergence lies below the device's noise floor. On a held-out suite of 30 deeper circuits (ISA depth to 100) executed on ibm_kingston, with all thresholds frozen, it attains 96.7% effective and 93.3% strict accuracy. We resolve key ambiguities - such as distinguishing correct Grover amplification from bug-induced collapse - and identify fundamental limits of single-basis diagnostics, including a Z-basis blind spot where phase-only errors remain statistically invisible. The extracted rule base is analyzed rather than asserted: the learned rules reproduce the physics of the veto, of the Grover boundary, and of depth-dependent noise without any of them being hard-coded. This work establishes an interpretable diagnostic layer that prevents error mitigation from being applied to logically flawed circuits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:45:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilin","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Yilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Abend","name":"Abend, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduard","familyName":"Hovy","name":"Hovy, Eduard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lea","familyName":"Frermann","name":"Frermann, Lea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring Pragmatic Influence in Large Language Model Instructions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T06:52:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T10:44:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T00:40:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is not only what we ask large language models (LLMs) to do that matters, but also how we ask them. Phrases like ``This is urgent'' or ``As your supervisor'' can shift model behavior without altering task content. We study this effect as pragmatic framing, contextual cues that shape directive interpretation rather than task specification. While prior work exploits such cues for prompt optimization or probes them as security vulnerabilities, pragmatic framing itself has received comparatively little attention as a target of controlled measurement in instruction following. To support its systematic study as a measurable property, we introduce a framework that combines three components: directive-framing decomposition separating framing context from task specification; a taxonomy organizing 400 instantiations of framing into 13 strategies across 4 mechanism clusters; and priority-based measurement that quantifies influence through observable shifts in directive prioritization. Evaluating five open-weight LLMs across different families and scales, we find that pragmatic framing produces systematic shifts in directive prioritization, and the effectiveness ranking of different strategies proves highly consistent across models. This reveals that susceptibility to pragmatic framing is a structured behavioral property of instruction-tuned systems. Measuring this susceptibility is a prerequisite for any deliberate response to it, and this work provides the framework to do so."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the Conference on Language Modeling (COLM 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-14T02:19:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21777","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21777","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21777","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katja","familyName":"Kossira","name":"Kossira, Katja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yunxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jürgen","familyName":"Seiler","name":"Seiler, Jürgen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Kaup","name":"Kaup, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Object Segmentation Mask Selection Using Specular Reflections"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:52:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:43:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T11:06:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:46:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/vcip67698.2025.11396903","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Specular reflections pose a significant challenge for object segmentation, as their sharp intensity transitions often mislead both conventional algorithms and deep learning based methods. However, as the specular reflection must lie on the surface of the object, this fact can be exploited to improve the segmentation masks. By identifying the largest region containing the reflection as the object, we derive a more accurate object mask without requiring specialized training data or model adaption. We evaluate our method on both synthetic and real world images and compare it against established and state-of-the-art techniques including Otsu thresholding, YOLO, and SAM2. Compared to the best performing baseline SAM2, our approach achieves up to 26.7% improvement in IoU, 22.3% in DSC, and 9.7% in pixel accuracy. Qualitative evaluations on real world images further confirm the robustness and generalizability of the proposed approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21777","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Heinlein","name":"Heinlein, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Taraz","name":"Taraz, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Error of Deep Operator Networks Is the Sum of Its Parts: Branch-Trunk and Mode Error Decompositions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65M22, 68T07, 15A18, 35Q53","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:52:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T06:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:18:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Operator learning has the potential to strongly impact scientific computing by learning solution operators for differential equations, potentially accelerating multi-query tasks such as design optimization and uncertainty quantification by orders of magnitude. Despite proven universal approximation properties, deep operator networks (DeepONets) often exhibit limited accuracy and generalization in practice, which hinders their adoption. Understanding these limitations is therefore crucial for further advancing the approach.\n This work analyzes performance limitations of the classical DeepONet architecture. It is shown that the approximation error is dominated by the branch network when the internal dimension is sufficiently large, and that the learned trunk basis can often be replaced by classical basis functions without a significant impact on performance.\n To investigate this further, a modified DeepONet is constructed in which the trunk network is replaced by the left singular vectors of the training solution matrix. This modification yields several key insights. First, for examples involving the KdV and Burgers equations, a spectral bias in the branch network is observed, with coefficients of dominant, low-frequency modes learned more effectively. Second, through the interplay of the singular-value weighting and the optimizer's neglect of small modes, the branch error is dominated by modes with large and intermediate singular values. Third, using a shared branch network for all mode coefficients, as in the standard architecture, improves generalization of small modes compared to a stacked architecture in which coefficients are computed separately. Finally, detrimental coupling between modes in parameter space is identified, which weakens with increasing network width."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:53:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiafeng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huakun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Huakun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianmin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TiMi: Empower Time Series Transformers with Multimodal Mixture of Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:51:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T03:22:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:25:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal time series forecasting has garnered significant attention for its potential to provide more accurate predictions than traditional single-modality models by leveraging rich information inherent in other modalities. However, due to fundamental challenges in modality alignment, existing methods often struggle to effectively incorporate multimodal data into predictions, particularly textual information that has a causal influence on time series fluctuations, such as emergency reports and policy announcements. In this paper, we reflect on the role of textual information in numerical forecasting and propose Time series transformers with Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts, TiMi, to unleash the causal reasoning capabilities of LLMs. Concretely, TiMi utilizes LLMs to generate inferences on future developments, which serve as guidance for time series forecasting. To seamlessly integrate both exogenous factors and time series into predictions, we introduce a Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts (MMoE) module as a lightweight plug-in to empower Transformer-based time series models for multimodal forecasting, eliminating the need for explicit representation-level alignment. Experimentally, our proposed TiMi demonstrates consistent state-of-the-art performance on sixteen real-world multimodal forecasting benchmarks, outperforming advanced baselines while offering both strong adaptability and interpretability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:26:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Branislav","familyName":"Kveton","name":"Kveton, Branislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samyadeep","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Samyadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhojyoti","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhojyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Leyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Dernoncourt","name":"Dernoncourt, Franck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungchul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sungchul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyun","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Seunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puneet","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Puneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyung","familyName":"Kil","name":"Kil, Jihyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuxiang","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jiuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nedim","familyName":"Lipka","name":"Lipka, Nedim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan A.","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Ryan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Derr","name":"Derr, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Based Personalized Generation for Users with Sparse Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T01:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T21:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:10:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) personalization holds great promise for tailoring responses by leveraging personal context and history. However, real-world users usually possess sparse interaction histories with limited personal context, such as cold-start users in social platforms and newly registered customers in online E-commerce platforms, compromising the LLM-based personalized generation. To address this challenge, we introduce GraSPer (Graph-based Sparse Personalized Reasoning), a novel framework for enhancing personalized text generation under sparse context. GraSPer first augments user context by predicting items that the user would likely interact with in the future. With reasoning alignment, it then generates texts for these interactions to enrich the augmented context. In the end, it generates personalized outputs conditioned on both the real and synthetic histories, ensuring alignment with user style and preferences. Extensive experiments on three benchmark personalized generation datasets show that GraSPer achieves significant performance gain, substantially improving personalization in sparse user context settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:59:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Junxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengfei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Tengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanglin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhanglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yunfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiliang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daimeng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Daimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global-Local Dual Perception for MLLMs in High-Resolution Text-Rich Image Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:38:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T02:29:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text Image Machine Translation (TIMT) aims to translate text embedded in images in the source-language into target-language, requiring synergistic integration of visual perception and linguistic understanding. Existing TIMT methods, whether cascaded pipelines or end-to-end multimodal large language models (MLLMs),struggle with high-resolution text-rich images due to cluttered layouts, diverse fonts, and non-textual distractions, resulting in text omission, semantic drift, and contextual inconsistency. To address these challenges, we propose GLoTran, a global-local dual visual perception framework for MLLM-based TIMT. GLoTran integrates a low-resolution global image with multi-scale region-level text image slices under an instruction-guided alignment strategy, conditioning MLLMs to maintain scene-level contextual consistency while faithfully capturing fine-grained textual details. Moreover, to realize this dual-perception paradigm, we construct GLoD, a large-scale text-rich TIMT dataset comprising 510K high-resolution global-local image-text pairs covering diverse real-world scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that GLoTran substantially improves translation completeness and accuracy over state-of-the-art MLLMs, offering a new paradigm for fine-grained TIMT under high-resolution and text-rich conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:07:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21806","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21806","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21806","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxue","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhou","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yanzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunrong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Chunrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Agent Frameworks Fall Short: Examining Functional Challenges and Usability Concerns"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T16:17:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:54:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T07:48:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:33:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly built on agent frameworks that provide reusable abstractions for workflow orchestration, state management, tool integration, and execution control. However, the quality of this infrastructure layer remains insufficiently understood, particularly its functionality challenges and usability concerns, as existing studies have mainly examined traditional deep learning (DL) frameworks or model-level agent failures. Therefore, we conduct an empirical study of 5,669 bug reports and 809 feature requests from five mainstream agent frameworks: AutoGen, CrewAI, LangChain, LangGraph, and MetaGPT. We construct a four-dimensional taxonomy covering 22 root causes, seven symptoms, 11 motivations, and six requirements, and map them to the five-stage agent lifecycle. Across the four RQs, results show an execution centered quality pattern shaped by semantic interface boundaries. Reported bugs mainly manifest as Incorrect Functionality (76.00%) and involve more API, configuration, parsing, and serialization related causes than DL framework bugs, while their associations remain sparse and stage specific. Feature requests mainly target Feature Enhancement (49.07%) and reveal structured needs for Orchestration Expressiveness, Development Delivery, Model Adaptation, and Tool Ecosystem. These findings call for quality assurance beyond crash based and tensor level testing, with emphasis on API sequences, structured LLM outputs, serialization boundaries, execution traces, and execution centered maintenance, offering empirical guidance for reliable and usable agent framework infrastructure."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21806","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Fingscheidt","name":"Fingscheidt, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Karabutov","name":"Karabutov, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panqi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Panqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Alshina","name":"Alshina, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörn","familyName":"Ostermann","name":"Ostermann, Jörn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Optimizing Image Codecs for VMAF NEG: Analysis, Issues, and a Robust Loss Proposal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:08:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T13:46:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T08:40:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The VMAF (video multi-method assessment fusion) metric for image and video coding recently gained more and more popularity as it is supposed to have a high correlation with human perception. This makes training and particularly fine-tuning of machine-learned codecs on this metric interesting. However, VMAF is shown to be attackable in a way that, e.g., unsharpening an image can lead to a gain in VMAF quality while decreasing the quality in human perception. A particular version of VMAF called VMAF NEG has been designed to be more robust against such attacks and therefore it should be more useful for fine-tuning of codecs. In this paper, our contributions are threefold. First, we identify and analyze the still existing vulnerability of VMAF NEG towards attacks, particulary towards the attack that consists in employing VMAF NEG for image codec fine-tuning. Second, to benefit from VMAF NEG's high correlation with human perception, we propose a robust loss including VMAF NEG for fine-tuning either the encoder or the decoder. Third, we support our quantitative objective results by providing perceptive impressions of some image examples."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5+1 Pages, 3 tabels and 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:17:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jitindra","familyName":"Fartiyal","name":"Fartiyal, Jitindra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Freire","name":"Freire, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergei K.","familyName":"Turitsyn","name":"Turitsyn, Sergei K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergei G.","familyName":"Solovski","name":"Solovski, Sergei G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PatchDenoiser: Parameter-efficient multi-scale patch learning and fusion denoiser for Low-dose CT imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T11:59:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:45:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-29T12:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:45:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-dose CT images are essential for reducing radiation exposure in cancer screening, pediatric imaging, and longitudinal monitoring protocols, but their quality is often degraded by noise from low-dose acquisition, patient motion, or scanner limitations, affecting both clinical interpretation and downstream analysis. Traditional filtering approaches often over-smooth and lose fine anatomical details, while deep learning methods, including CNNs, GANs, and transformers, may struggle to preserve such details or require large, computationally expensive models, limiting clinical practicality. We propose PatchDenoiser, a lightweight, energy-efficient multi-scale patch-based denoising framework. It decomposes denoising into local texture extraction and global context aggregation, fused via a spatially aware patch fusion strategy. This design enables effective noise suppression while preserving fine structural and anatomical details. PatchDenoiser is ultra-lightweight, with far fewer parameters and lower computational complexity than CNN, GAN, and transformer based denoisers. On the 2016 Mayo Low-Dose CT dataset, PatchDenoiser consistently outperforms state-of-the-art CNN- and GAN-based methods in PSNR and SSIM. It is robust to variations in slice thickness, reconstruction kernels, and HU windows, generalizes across scanners without fine-tuning, and reduces parameters by ~9x and energy consumption per inference by ~27x compared with conventional CNN denoisers. PatchDenoiser thus provides a practical, scalable, and computationally efficient solution for medical image denoising, balancing performance, robustness, and clinical deployability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:22:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Kramer","name":"Kramer, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward H.","familyName":"Kennedy","name":"Kennedy, Edward H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac M.","familyName":"Opper","name":"Opper, Isaac M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Causal Inference with High-Dimensional Treatments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:53:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-28T15:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-29T01:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we consider causal inference in various high-dimensional treatment settings, including for single multi-valued treatments and vector treatments with binary or continuous components, when the number of treatments can be comparable to or even larger than the number of observations. These settings bring unique challenges: first, the treatment effects of interest are represented by a high-dimensional vector rather than a scalar; second, positivity violations are often unavoidable; and third, estimation can be based on a smaller effective sample size. We first discuss fundamental limits of estimating effects here, showing that consistent estimation is impossible without further assumptions. We go on to propose novel doubly robust estimators for mean potential outcomes of a high-dimensional single multi-valued treatment. We analyze the proposed estimators under sparsity assumptions, giving finite-sample risk bounds and showing that consistent estimation is possible under these conditions. Moreover, we derive minimax lower bounds in a sparse and structure-agnostic model to characterize optimal rates of convergence and show our risk bounds are unimprovable. We then generalize our proposed estimators as a sparse pseudo-outcome regression framework with constrained regression estimators and error guarantees under sparsity, allowing estimation of generic functionals and different types of high-dimensional treatments. We apply the framework to derive estimators of the mean potential outcomes for high-dimensional vector treatments. Finally, we illustrate the proposed methods through a simulation and an empirical application."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-29T02:29:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21947","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21947","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21947","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohan","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Sohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish Mahendran","familyName":"Kurapath","name":"Kurapath, Ashish Mahendran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tejas","familyName":"Melkote","name":"Melkote, Tejas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Algorithmic Blindness in Large Language Models: A Calibration Study of Performance Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:32:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T14:33:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:15:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-05T09:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-07T00:50:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-25T22:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:39:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable breadth of knowledge, yet their ability to reason about computational processes remains poorly understood. Closing this gap matters for practitioners who rely on LLMs to guide algorithm selection and deployment. We address this limitation using causal discovery as a testbed and evaluate eight frontier LLMs against ground truth derived from algorithm executions. We find systematic, near-total failure across models. The predicted ranges are far wider than true confidence intervals yet still fail to contain the true algorithmic mean in most cases. Most models perform worse than random guessing. The best model's marginal improvement points to benchmark memorization rather than principled reasoning. We term this failure algorithmic blindness and argue it reflects a fundamental gap between declarative knowledge about algorithms and calibrated procedural prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at https://github.com/sohv/algorithmic-blindness"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21947","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21783","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21783","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21783","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beatrice","familyName":"Luciani","name":"Luciani, Beatrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex van den","familyName":"Berg","name":"Berg, Alex van den","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matti","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Matti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre L.","familyName":"Ratschat","name":"Ratschat, Alexandre L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Marchal-Crespo","name":"Marchal-Crespo, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Therapist-Robot-Patient Physical Interaction is Worth a Thousand Words: Enabling Intuitive Therapist Guidance via Remote Haptic Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:04:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:19:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41598-026-63191-x","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic systems can enhance the amount and repeatability of physically guided motor training. Yet their real-world adoption is limited, partly due to non-intuitive trainer/therapist-trainee/patient interactions. To address this gap, we present a haptic teleoperation system for trainers to remotely guide and monitor the movements of a trainee wearing an arm exoskeleton. The trainer can physically interact with the exoskeleton through a commercial handheld haptic device via virtual contact points at the exoskeleton's elbow and wrist, allowing intuitive guidance. Thirty-two participants tested the system in a trainer-trainee paradigm, comparing our haptic demonstration system with conventional visual demonstration in guiding trainees in executing arm poses. Quantitative analyses showed that haptic demonstration significantly reduced movement completion time and improved smoothness, while speech analysis using large language models for automated transcription and categorization of verbal commands revealed fewer verbal instructions. The haptic demonstration did not result in higher reported mental and physical effort by trainers compared to the visual demonstration, while trainers reported greater competence and trainees lower physical demand. These findings support the feasibility of our proposed interface for effective remote human-robot physical interaction. Future work should assess its usability and efficacy for clinical populations in restoring clinicians' sense of agency during robot-assisted therapy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 5 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21783","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:52:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seokwon","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Seokwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngbin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Youngbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyuk","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Seunghyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungbeom","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seungbeom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MoonJeong","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, MoonJeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Routing by Reaching: Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:44:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T12:23:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-20T05:12:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) learn to sample diverse candidates in proportion to a reward function, making them well-suited for scientific discovery, where exploring multiple promising solutions is crucial. Further extending GFlowNets to multi-objective settings has attracted growing interest as real-world applications often involve multiple, conflicting objectives. However, existing approaches require joint training for each combination of objectives, meaning that any change in the objective set necessitates retraining from scratch. We propose a framework that composes pre-trained GFlowNets at inference time, enabling rapid adaptation without fine-tuning or retraining. Importantly, our framework is flexible, capable of handling diverse reward combinations ranging from linear scalarization to complex nonlinear operators, which are often handled separately in previous literature. We prove that our method exactly recovers the target distribution for linear scalarization, and quantify the approximation quality for nonlinear operators through a distortion factor. Experiments on a synthetic 2D grid and real-world molecule generation tasks demonstrate that our approach achieves performance comparable to baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Appears in the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:47:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ningyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Welleck","name":"Welleck, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection for LLM Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:54:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-18T04:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:21:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a central post-training paradigm for large language models (LLMs), but its performance is highly sensitive to the quality of training problems. This sensitivity stems from the non-stationarity of RL: rollouts are generated by an evolving policy, and learning is shaped by exploration and reward feedback, unlike supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with fixed trajectories. As a result, prior work often relies on manual curation or simple heuristic filters (e.g., accuracy), which can admit incorrect or low-utility problems. We propose GradAlign, a gradient-aligned data selection method for LLM reinforcement learning that uses a small, trusted validation set to prioritize training problems whose policy gradients align with validation gradients, yielding an adaptive curriculum. We evaluate GradAlign across three challenging data regimes: unreliable reward signals, distribution imbalance, and low-utility training corpus, showing that GradAlign consistently outperforms existing baselines, underscoring the importance of directional gradient signals in navigating non-stationary policy optimization and yielding more stable training and improved final performance. We release our implementation at https://github.com/StigLidu/GradAlign"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages. Accepted by COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:34Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:47:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wilson","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wilson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine E","familyName":"Apgar","name":"Apgar, Catherine E","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lee A","familyName":"Bernstein","name":"Bernstein, Lee A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"YunHsuan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, YunHsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan B","familyName":"McIntosh","name":"McIntosh, Alan B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitri G","familyName":"Medvedev","name":"Medvedev, Dmitri G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ellen M","familyName":"OBrien","name":"OBrien, Ellen M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christiaan E","familyName":"Vermeulen","name":"Vermeulen, Christiaan E","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew S","familyName":"Voyles","name":"Voyles, Andrew S","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan T","familyName":"Morrell","name":"Morrell, Jonathan T","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using Neural Networks to Accelerate TALYS-2.0 Nuclear Reaction Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-13T14:56:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.physletb.2026.140722","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts to improve the predictability of TALYS-2.0 calculated charged-particle residual product cross sections have focused on adjusting parameters related to the optical model potential and pre-equilibrium process. Although adjusted TALYS-2.0 outputs show marked improvements in agreement with experimental data over the default parameters, the procedure is generally time-consuming due to the need for sequential TALYS-2.0 calculations. Since the models and model parameters must be defined and constrained prior to adjustment, we show in this work that an artificial neural network can serve as a surrogate model to successfully predict TALYS-2.0 outputs within this domain of input parameters. No practical differences were observed in the trained model's performance between uniform random, Latin hypercube and Sobol sequence sampling for generating the training datasets. Once validated, trained neural network models were used to adjust TALYS-2.0 nuclear model parameters, where a multi-parameter fitting procedure was not only feasible but optimal for this process. The neural network approach is \u0026gt;1000x faster at generating residual product cross sections than using TALYS-2.0 directly, and a high-fidelity surrogate model could be implemented with about 1500 TALYS-2.0 files to achieve adjusted cross sections comparable to the previous publication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures, 10 supplementary figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:21Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:17:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Br.AI.K"],"givenName":"Jean-Philippe","familyName":"Garnier","name":"Garnier, Jean-Philippe","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A General Equilibrium Theory of Orchestrated AI Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T13:21:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-13T10:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T01:20:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a general equilibrium theory for systems of large language model (LLM) agents operating under centralized orchestration. The framework is a production economy in the sense of Arrow-Debreu (1954), extended to infinite-dimensional commodity spaces following Bewley (1972). Each LLM agent is modeled as a firm whose production set Y a $\\subset$ H = L 2 ([0, T ], R R ) represents the feasible metric trajectories determined by its frozen model weights. The orchestrator is the consumer, choosing a routing policy over the agent DAG to maximize system welfare subject to a budget constraint evaluated at functional prices p $\\in$ H A . These prices-elements of the Hilbert dual of the commodity space-assign a shadow value to each metric of each agent at each instant. We prove, via Brouwer's theorem applied to a finitedimensional approximation V K $\\subset$ H, that every such economy admits at least one general equilibrium (p * , y * , $π$ * ). A functional Walras' law holds as a theorem: the value of functional excess demand is zero for all prices, as a consequence of the consumer's budget constraint-not by construction. We further establish Pareto optimality (First Welfare Theorem), decentralizability of Pareto optima (Second Welfare Theorem), and uniqueness with geometric convergence under a contraction condition (Banach). The orchestration dynamics constitute a Walrasian t{â}tonnement that converges globally under the contraction condition, unlike classical t{â}tonnement (Scarf, 1960). The framework admits a DSGE interpretation with SLO parameters as policy rates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T04:01:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqiao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kaiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyi","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Chenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renliang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Renliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yanqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARLArena: A Unified Framework for Stable Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:22:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T23:51:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:58:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-04T04:31:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:06:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic reinforcement learning (ARL) has rapidly gained attention as a promising paradigm for training agents to solve complex, multi-step interactive tasks. Despite encouraging early results, ARL remains highly unstable, often leading to training collapse. This instability limits scalability to larger environments and longer interaction horizons, and constrains systematic exploration of algorithmic design choices. In this paper, we first propose ARLArena, a stable training recipe and systematic analysis framework that examines training stability in a controlled and reproducible setting. ARLArena first constructs a clean and standardized testbed. Then, we decompose policy gradient into four core design dimensions and assess the performance and stability of each dimension. Through this fine-grained analysis, we distill a unified perspective on ARL and propose SAMPO, a stable agentic policy optimization method designed to mitigate the dominant sources of instability in ARL. Empirically, SAMPO achieves consistently stable training and strong performance across diverse agentic tasks. Overall, this study provides a unifying policy gradient perspective for ARL and offers practical guidance for building stable and reproducible LLM-based agent training pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T05:03:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Ghiasvand","name":"Ghiasvand, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haniyeh Ehsani","familyName":"Oskouie","name":"Oskouie, Haniyeh Ehsani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahnoosh","familyName":"Alizadeh","name":"Alizadeh, Mahnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramtin","familyName":"Pedarsani","name":"Pedarsani, Ramtin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMLoP: Multi-Modal Low-Rank Prompting for Efficient Vision-Language Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:00:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-01T05:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt learning has become a dominant paradigm for adapting vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP to downstream tasks without modifying pretrained weights. While extending prompts to both vision and text encoders across multiple transformer layers significantly boosts performance, it dramatically increases the number of trainable parameters, with state-of-the-art methods requiring millions of parameters and abandoning the parameter efficiency that makes prompt tuning attractive. In this work, we propose MMLoP (Multi-Modal Low-Rank Prompting), a framework that achieves deep multi-modal prompting with only 11.5K trainable parameters, comparable to early text-only methods like CoOp. MMLoP parameterizes vision and text prompts at each transformer layer through a low-rank factorization that constrains prompts to a compact subspace, providing parameter efficiency while motivating the need for our complementary regularization components. To further close the accuracy gap with state-of-the-art methods, we introduce three complementary components: a self-regulating consistency loss that anchors prompted representations to frozen zero-shot CLIP features at both the feature and logit levels, a uniform drift correction that removes the global embedding shift induced by prompt tuning to preserve class-discriminative structure, and a shared up-projection that couples vision and text prompts through a common low-rank factor to enforce cross-modal alignment. Extensive experiments across three benchmarks and 11 diverse datasets demonstrate that MMLoP achieves a highly favorable accuracy-efficiency tradeoff, outperforming the majority of existing methods including those with orders of magnitude more parameters, while achieving a harmonic mean of 79.70\\% on base-to-novel generalization. Code is available at https://github.com/sajjad-ucsb/MMLoP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:13:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazi Samin Yasar","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Kazi Samin Yasar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tanbir","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Md Tanbir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamim","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Tamim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajwad","familyName":"Abrar","name":"Abrar, Ajwad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rafid","familyName":"Haque","name":"Haque, Md Rafid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MixSarc: A Bangla-English Code-Mixed Corpus for Implicit Meaning Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:12:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-27T15:30:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:39:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bangla-English code-mixing is widespread across South Asian social media, yet resources for implicit meaning identification in this setting remain scarce. Existing sentiment and sarcasm models largely focus on monolingual English or high-resource languages and struggle with transliteration variation, cultural references, and intra-sentential language switching. To address this gap, we introduce MixSarc, the first publicly available Bangla-English code-mixed corpus for implicit meaning identification. The dataset contains 9,087 manually annotated sentences labeled for humor, sarcasm, offensiveness, and vulgarity. We construct the corpus through targeted social media collection, systematic filtering, and multi-annotator validation. We benchmark transformer-based models and evaluate zero-shot large language models under structured prompting. Results show strong performance on humor detection but substantial degradation on sarcasm, offense, and vulgarity due to class imbalance and pragmatic complexity. Zero-shot models achieve competitive micro-F1 scores but low exact match accuracy. Further analysis reveals that over 42\\% of negative sentiment instances in an external dataset exhibit sarcastic characteristics. MixSarc provides a foundational resource for culturally aware NLP and supports more reliable multi-label modeling in code-mixed environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:11:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Linhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elias","familyName":"Raffoul","name":"Raffoul, Elias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diagnosis-Driven Co-planning of Network Reinforcement and BESS for Distribution Grid with High Penetration of Electric Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:45:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-27T04:47:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While the rapid proliferation of electric vehicles (EVs) accelerates net-zero goals, uncoordinated charging activities impose severe operational challenges on distribution grids, including exacerbated peak loads, thermal overloading, and voltage violations. To overcome the computational intractability of jointly optimizing grid infrastructure reinforcements and Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) installations, this paper proposes a novel three-stage Diagnosis-Driven Co-Planning (DDCP) framework. The methodology integrates a Violation Detection and Quantification (VDQ) model to systematically identify system breaches, and a Violation Mitigation-Based Planning (VMBP) model for optimal BESS allocation. Specifically, Stage I of the DDCP framework diagnoses critical bottleneck lines that render standalone BESS solutions infeasible; Stage II executes targeted physical upgrades exclusively on these bottlenecks; and Stage III finalizes the optimal BESS deployment on the updated network topology. Furthermore, this study quantifies the EV hosting capacity thresholds before and after BESS integration across varying EV adoption rates and base voltages. Finally, a comprehensive comparative analysis evaluates four mitigation approaches: the VDQ-driven cable upgrade (VCU) model, the VMBP model, system-wide voltage uprating, and the proposed DDCP framework. The results demonstrate that the DDCP framework not only resolves the complex joint-optimization hurdle but also achieves superior techno-economic performance in addressing high-EV-penetration challenges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:11:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathalie C.","familyName":"Pinheiro","name":"Pinheiro, Nathalie C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Donghu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah P.","familyName":"Menke","name":"Menke, Hannah P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket C.","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Aniket C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire E.","familyName":"Heaney","name":"Heaney, Claire E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed H.","familyName":"ElSheikh","name":"ElSheikh, Ahmed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher C.","familyName":"Pain","name":"Pain, Christopher C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate models for Rock-Fluid Interaction: A Grid-Size-Invariant Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T20:13:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00366-026-02353-x","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modelling rock-fluid interaction requires solving a set of partial differential equations (PDEs) to predict the flow behaviour and the reactions of the fluid with the rock on the interfaces. Conventional high-fidelity numerical models require a high resolution to obtain reliable results, resulting in huge computational expense. This restricts the applicability of these models for multi-query problems, such as uncertainty quantification and optimisation, which require running numerous scenarios. As a cheaper alternative to high-fidelity models, this work develops eight surrogate models for predicting the fluid flow in porous media. Four of these are reduced-order models (ROM) based on one neural network for compression and another for prediction. The other four are single neural networks with the property of grid-size invariance; a term which we use to refer to image-to-image models that are capable of inferring on computational domains that are larger than those used during training. In addition to the novel grid-size-invariant framework for surrogate models, we compare the predictive performance of UNet and UNet++ architectures, and demonstrate that UNet++ outperforms UNet for surrogate models. Furthermore, we show that the grid-size-invariant approach is a reliable way to reduce memory consumption during training, resulting in good correlation between predicted and ground-truth values and outperforming the ROMs analysed. The application analysed is particularly challenging because fluid-induced rock dissolution results in a non-static solid field and, consequently, it cannot be used to help in adjustments of the future prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":2,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:19:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jindan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jindan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhaoxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tayfun","familyName":"Gokmen","name":"Gokmen, Tayfun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Symmetric Point Tracking: Tackling Non-ideal Reference in Analog In-memory Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T20:22:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:09:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Analog in-memory computing (AIMC) performs computation directly within resistive crossbar arrays, offering an energy-efficient platform to scale large vision and language models. However, non-ideal analog device properties make the training on AIMC devices challenging. In particular, its update asymmetry can induce a systematic drift of weight updates towards a device-specific symmetric point (SP), which typically does not align with the optimum of the training objective. To mitigate this bias, most existing works assume the SP is known and pre-calibrate it to zero before training by setting the reference point as the SP. Nevertheless, calibrating AIMC devices requires costly pulse updates, and residual calibration error can directly degrade training performance. In this work, we present the first theoretical characterization of the pulse complexity of SP calibration and the resulting estimation error. We further propose a dynamic SP estimation method that tracks the SP during model training, and establishes its convergence guarantees. In addition, we develop an enhanced variant based on chopping and filtering techniques from digital signal processing. Numerical experiments demonstrate both the efficiency and effectiveness of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:30:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Curriculum Model-based Reinforcement Learning for Shape Control of Deformable Linear Objects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:44:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T05:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Precise shape control of Deformable Linear Objects (DLOs) is crucial in robotic applications such as industrial and medical fields. However, existing methods face challenges in handling complex large deformation tasks, especially those involving opposite curvatures, and lack efficiency and precision. To address this, we propose a two-stage framework combining Reinforcement Learning (RL) and online visual servoing. In the large-deformation stage, a model-based reinforcement learning approach using an ensemble of dynamics models is introduced to significantly improve sample efficiency. Additionally, we design a self-curriculum goal generation mechanism that dynamically selects intermediate-difficulty goals with high diversity through imagined evaluations, thereby optimizing the policy learning process. In the small-deformation stage, a Jacobian-based visual servo controller is deployed to ensure high-precision convergence. Simulation results show that the proposed method enables efficient policy learning and significantly outperforms mainstream baselines in shape control success rate and precision. Furthermore, the framework effectively transfers the policy trained in simulation to real-world tasks with zero-shot adaptation. It successfully completes all 30 cases with diverse initial and target shapes across DLOs of different sizes and materials. The project website is available at: https://anonymous.4open.science/w/sc-mbrl-dlo-EB48/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IROS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:42:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21877","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21877","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21877","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Laiti","name":"Laiti, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Talon","name":"Talon, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacopo","familyName":"Staiano","name":"Staiano, Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Ricci","name":"Ricci, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How to Take a Memorable Picture? Empowering Users with Actionable Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:02:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T15:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:36:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image memorability, i.e., how likely an image is to be remembered, has traditionally been studied in computer vision either as a passive prediction task, with models regressing a scalar score, or with generative methods altering the visual input to boost the image likelihood of being remembered. Yet, none of these paradigms supports users at capture time, when the crucial question is how to improve a photo memorability. We introduce the task of Memorability Feedback (MemFeed), where an automated model should provide actionable, human-interpretable guidance to users with the goal to enhance an image future recall. We also present MemCoach, the first approach designed to provide concrete suggestions in natural language for memorability improvement (e.g., \"emphasize facial expression,\" \"bring the subject forward\"). Our method, based on Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), is training-free and employs a teacher-student steering strategy, aligning the model internal activations toward more memorable patterns learned from a teacher model progressing along least-to-most memorable samples. To enable systematic evaluation on this novel task, we further introduce MemBench, a new benchmark featuring sequence-aligned photoshoots with annotated memorability scores. Our experiments, considering multiple MLLMs, demonstrate the effectiveness of MemCoach, showing consistently improved performance over several zero-shot models. The results indicate that memorability can not only be predicted but also taught and instructed, shifting the focus from mere prediction to actionable feedback for human creators."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted @ CVPR 2026. Project page: https://laitifranz.github.io/MemCoach/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21877","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:42:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"MacAvaney","name":"MacAvaney, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Dalton","name":"Dalton, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Text Ranking in Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T07:08:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep research has emerged as an important task that aims to address hard queries that need extensive open-web exploration. To tackle it, most prior work equips large language model (LLM)-based agents with opaque web search APIs, enabling agents to iteratively issue search queries, retrieve external evidence, and reason over it. Despite search's essential role in deep research, black-box web search APIs leave the behaviour of established text ranking methods in deep research largely unclear. To fill this gap, we reproduce key findings and best practices for text ranking methods in deep research. We examine their effectiveness from three perspectives: (i) retrieval units (documents vs. passages), (ii) pipeline configurations (different retrievers, re-rankers, and re-ranking depths), and (iii) query characteristics (the mismatch between agent-issued queries and the training queries of text rankers). We perform experiments on BrowseComp-Plus, a deep research dataset with a fixed corpus, evaluating 2 open-source agents, 5 retrievers, and 3 re-rankers. We find that agent-issued queries typically follow web-search-style syntax (e.g., quoted exact matches), favouring lexical, learned sparse, and multi-vector retrievers; passage-level units are more efficient under limited context windows, and avoid the difficulties of document length normalisation in lexical retrieval; re-ranking is highly effective. We further propose a query-to-question (Q2Q) method that translates agent-issued queries into natural language questions, significantly reducing the query mismatch."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 49th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval (SIGIR 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:42:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Gengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramana Rao","familyName":"Kompella","name":"Kompella, Ramana Rao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaowen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA Knows Its Limits: Adaptive Execution Horizons for Robot Policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:48:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-20T22:55:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:38:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Action chunking has recently emerged as a standard practice in flow-based Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. However, the effect and choice of the execution horizon - the number of actions to be executed from each predicted chunk - remains underexplored. In this work, we first show that varying the execution horizon leads to substantial performance deviations, with performance initially improving and then declining as the horizon increases. To uncover the reasons, we analyze the cross- and self-attention weights in flow-based VLAs and reveal two key phenomena: (i) intra-chunk actions attend invariantly to vision-language tokens, limiting adaptability to environmental changes; and (ii) the initial and terminal action tokens serve as stable anchors, forming latent centers around which intermediate actions are organized. Motivated by these insights, we interpret action self-attention weights as a proxy for the model's predictive limit and propose AutoHorizon, the first test-time method that dynamically estimates the execution horizon for each predicted action chunk to adapt to changing perceptual conditions. Across simulated and real-world robotic manipulation tasks, AutoHorizon is performant, incurs negligible computational overhead, and generalizes across diverse tasks and flow-based models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ECCV 2026. Project page at https://hatchetproject.github.io/autohorizon/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:41:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Siahkoohi","name":"Siahkoohi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Hyunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional neural control variates for variance reduction in Bayesian inverse problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:40:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T04:59:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:28:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bayesian inference for inverse problems involves computing expectations under posterior distributions--e.g., posterior means, variances, or predictive quantities--typically via Monte Carlo (MC) estimation. When the quantity of interest varies significantly under the posterior, accurate estimates demand many samples--a cost often prohibitive for partial differential equation-constrained problems. To address this challenge, we introduce conditional neural control variates, a modular method that learns amortized control variates from joint model-data samples to reduce the variance of MC estimators. To scale to high-dimensional problems, we leverage Stein's identity to design an architecture based on an ensemble of hierarchical coupling layers with tractable Jacobian trace computation. Training requires: (i) samples from the joint distribution of unknown parameters and observed data; and (ii) the posterior score function, which can be computed from physics-based likelihood evaluations, neural operator surrogates, or learned generative models such as conditional normalizing flows. Once trained, the control variates generalize across observations without retraining. We validate our approach on stylized and partial differential equation-constrained Darcy flow inverse problems, outperforming classical Stein control variates and achieving substantial variance reduction, even when the analytical score is replaced by a learned surrogate."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:41:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21218","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21218","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21218","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Banayeeanzade","name":"Banayeeanzade, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingchuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deqing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Deqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spencer","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Spencer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin","familyName":"Babinsky","name":"Babinsky, Erin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Praneeth","familyName":"Karimireddy","name":"Karimireddy, Sai Praneeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EPSVec: Efficient and Private Synthetic Data Generation via Dataset Vectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T00:04:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-20T15:23:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-quality data is essential for modern machine learning, yet many valuable corpora are sensitive and cannot be freely shared. Synthetic data offers a practical substitute for downstream development, and large language models (LLMs) have emerged as powerful engines for generating it. However, existing private text generation methods are severely inefficient: they are data-intensive, computationally slow, and often require large private corpora or batch sizes to achieve usable quality. We introduce EPSVec, a differentially-private lightweight alternative that steers LLM generation using *dataset vectors*--directions in activation space that capture the distributional gap between private data and public priors. EPSVec extracts and sanitizes steering vectors just once and then performs standard decoding. This decouples the privacy budget from generation, enabling arbitrarily many synthetic samples without additional privacy cost and yielding strong fidelity even in low-data regimes. Furthermore, we enhance our method by utilizing pretrained (base) models and introducing fixed-shot prompting to boost generation diversity and fidelity. Our experiments demonstrate that EPSVec outperforms existing baselines in distributional alignment and downstream utility, particularly in low-data regimes, while significantly reducing computational overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026. Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21218","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:41:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22159","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22159","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22159","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoran","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DeRui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, DeRui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CASR: A Robust Cyclic Framework for Arbitrary Large-Scale Super-Resolution with Distribution Alignment and Self-Similarity Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:05:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-17T04:24:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:24:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T09:39:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:53:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T05:03:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Arbitrary-Scale SR (ASISR) remains fundamentally limited by cross-scale distribution shift: once the inference scale leaves the training range, noise, blur, and artifacts accumulate sharply. We revisit this challenge from a cross-scale distribution transition perspective and propose CASR, a simple yet highly efficient cyclic SR framework that reformulates ultra-magnification as a sequence of in-distribution scale transitions. This design ensures stable inference at arbitrary scales while requiring only a single model. CASR tackles two major bottlenecks: distribution drift across iterations and patch-wise diffusion inconsistencies. The proposed SSAM module aligns structural distributions via superpixel aggregation, preventing error accumulation, while SARM module restores high-frequency textures by enforcing correlation-guided consistency and preserving self-similarity structure through correlation alignment. Despite using only a single model, our approach significantly reduces distribution drift, preserves long-range texture consistency, and achieves superior generalization even at extreme magnification."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22159","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:24:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21331","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21331","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21331","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nelson","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nelson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William R.","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, William R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebecca","familyName":"Kramer-Bottiglio","name":"Kramer-Bottiglio, Rebecca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostas","familyName":"Bekris","name":"Bekris, Kostas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mridul","familyName":"Aanjaneya","name":"Aanjaneya, Mridul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CableRobotGraphSim: A Graph Neural Network for Modeling Partially Observable Cable-Driven Robot Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-09T16:18:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T01:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"General-purpose simulators have accelerated the development of robots. Traditional simulators based on first-principles, however, typically require full-state observability or depend on parameter search for system identification. This work presents \\texttt{CableRobotGraphSim}, a novel Graph Neural Network (GNN) model for cable-driven robots that aims to address shortcomings of prior simulation solutions. By representing cable-driven robots as graphs, with the rigid-bodies as nodes and the cables and contacts as edges, this model can quickly and accurately match the properties of other simulation models and real robots, while ingesting only partially observable inputs. Accompanying the GNN model is a sim-and-real co-training procedure that promotes generalization and robustness to noisy real data. This model is further integrated with a Model Predictive Path Integral (MPPI) controller for closed-loop navigation, which showcases the model's speed and accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21331","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:33:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21949","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21949","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21949","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinchao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yinchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Shikh-Bahaei","name":"Shikh-Bahaei, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy Efficient Federated Learning with Hyperdimensional Computing over Wireless Communication Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:33:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T16:45:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T02:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we investigate a problem of minimizing total energy consumption for secure federated learning (FL) over wireless edge networks. To address the high computational cost and privacy challenges in conventional FL with neural networks (NN) for resource-constrained users, we propose a novel FL with hyperdimensional computing and differential privacy (FL-HDC-DP) framework. In the considered model, each edge user employs hyperdimensional computing (HDC) for local training, which replaces complex neural updates with simple hypervector operations, and applies differential privacy (DP) noise to protect transmitted model information. We optimize the total energy of computation and communication under both latency and privacy constraints. We formulate the problem as an optimization that minimizes the total energy of all users by jointly allocating HDC dimension, transmission time, system bandwidth, transmit power, and CPU frequency. To solve this problem, a sigmoid-variant function is proposed to characterize the relationship between the HDC dimension and the convergence rounds required to reach a target accuracy. Based on this model, we develop two alternating optimization algorithms, where closed-form expressions for time, frequency, bandwidth, and power allocations are derived at each iteration. Since the iterative algorithm requires a feasible initialization, we construct a feasibility problem and obtain feasible initial resource parameters by solving a per round transmission time minimization problem. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed FL-HDC-DP framework achieves up to 83.3% total energy reduction compared with the baseline, while attaining about 90% accuracy in approximately 3.5X fewer communication rounds than the NN baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21949","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21889","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21889","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21889","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Otto","familyName":"Nyberg","name":"Nyberg, Otto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fausto","familyName":"Carcassi","name":"Carcassi, Fausto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Tugnoli","name":"Tugnoli, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Cinà","name":"Cinà, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"2-Step Agent: A Framework for the Interaction of a Decision Maker with AI Decision Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:11:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:50:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T13:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:57:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T18:14:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:06:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictions from ML models support human decision making in several fields, including high-stakes ones such as healthcare and the judiciary. Yet, we still lack a clear understanding of how decision makers learn from ML-based decision support (ML-DS). In this paper, we introduce a general computational framework, the 2-Step Agent, to capture this process. As a prediction from an ML model contains information about the training data, a prediction can also be used for inference. Our framework models (i) how a prediction for a new observation affects the beliefs of a rational Bayesian agent, and (ii) how this change in beliefs affects the estimation of causal effect, the downstream decision, and the subsequent outcome. In addition to the framework itself, we make three contributions. First, for the linear Gaussian setting, we derive a tractable solution for the challenging Bayesian inference problem we introduced, i.e. one in which the agent infers from an ML prediction. Second, we experimentally identify conditions under which ML-DS is beneficial. Third, we show that a single misaligned prior belief can be sufficient for ML-DS to lead to worse downstream outcomes compared to no decision support even when the ML model is well-specified and the agent is perfectly rational. Hence, even under ideal conditions, ML-DS can do more harm than good."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21889","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21788","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21788","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21788","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Scaling of LLM Training with Flexible Context Parallelism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T12:37:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:44:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling long-context capabilities is crucial for Large Language Models (LLMs). However, real-world data contain a large number of sequences with heterogeneous lengths. Existing training libraries for LLMs rely on static parallelism strategies, which suffer from severe load imbalance, redundant communication, and suboptimal hardware utilization under data heterogeneity. In this work, we propose Flexible Context Parallelism (FCP), an efficient parallelism strategy that adaptively reconfigures communication groups and context parallelism degrees during LLM training. We generalize more flexible non-power-of-two parallelism degrees and develop a polynomial-time algorithm to generate near-optimal parallelism strategies with only millisecond-level overhead per training batch. FCP is able to maintain high hardware efficiency even under extreme data heterogeneity. Experimental results demonstrate that FCP significantly outperforms Megatron-LM and DeepSpeed in both LLM and MLLM training, achieving up to 1.46x speedup in average throughput while maintaining near-linear scaling efficiency across large-scale clusters. For extremely unbalanced batches, FCP even achieves 2.24x speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21788","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Canonaco","name":"Canonaco, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Pozanco","name":"Pozanco, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Borrajo","name":"Borrajo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic Partial Grounding via LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:13:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T13:23:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:58:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Grounding is a critical step in classical planning, yet it often becomes a computational bottleneck due to the exponential growth in grounded actions and atoms as task size increases. Recent advances in partial grounding have addressed this challenge by incrementally grounding only the most promising operators, guided by predictive models. However, these approaches primarily rely on relational features or learned embeddings and do not leverage the textual and structural cues present in PDDL descriptions. We propose SPG-LLM, which uses LLMs to analyze the domain and problem files to heuristically identify potentially irrelevant objects, actions, and predicates prior to grounding, significantly reducing the size of the grounded task. Across seven hard-to-ground benchmarks, SPG-LLM achieves faster grounding-often by orders of magnitude-while delivering comparable or better plan costs in some domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:07:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walter Hernandez","familyName":"Cruz","name":"Cruz, Walter Hernandez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Devine","name":"Devine, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Vadgama","name":"Vadgama, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Tasca","name":"Tasca, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DLT-Corpus: A Large-Scale Text Collection for the Distributed Ledger Technology Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T22:55:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:16:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3770855.3817553","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce DLT-Corpus, the largest domain-specific text collection for Distributed Ledger Technology (DLT) research to date: 2.98 billion tokens from 22.12 million documents spanning scientific literature (37,440 publications), United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO) patents (49,023 filings), and social media (22 million posts). Existing Natural Language Processing (NLP) resources for DLT focus narrowly on cryptocurrency price prediction and smart contracts, leaving domain-specific language underexplored despite the sector's ~$3 trillion market capitalization and rapid technological evolution.\n We demonstrate DLT-Corpus' utility by analyzing patterns of technology emergence and market-innovation correlations. Findings reveal that technologies first appear in our scientific literature subset before reaching patents and social media, following traditional technology transfer patterns. While social media sentiment remains overwhelmingly bullish even during crypto winters, scientific and patent activity grows less tied to short-term sentiment, tracking overall market expansion in a virtuous cycle in which research precedes and enables economic growth that, in turn, funds further innovation.\n We release the DLT-Corpus and companion artifacts: LedgerBERT (+23% over BERT-base on DLT-specific Named Entity Recognition (NER) task), a sentiment analysis dataset of 23,301 crypto news headlines and descriptions, tools, and code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining V.2 (KDD '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:29Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T03:01:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huai","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Huai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hesheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hesheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeatherCity: Urban Scene Reconstruction with Controllable Multi-Weather Transformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:44:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T17:10:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T01:23:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Editable high-fidelity 4D scenes are crucial for autonomous driving, as they can be applied to end-to-end training and closed-loop simulation. However, existing reconstruction methods are primarily limited to replicating observed scenes and lack the capability for diverse weather simulation. While image-level weather editing methods tend to introduce scene artifacts and offer poor controllability over the weather effects. To address these limitations, we propose \\textbf{WeatherCity}, a novel framework for 4D urban scene reconstruction and weather editing. Specifically, we leverage a text-guided image editing model to achieve flexible editing of image weather backgrounds. To tackle the challenge of multi-weather modeling, we introduce a novel weather Gaussian representation based on shared scene features and dedicated weather-specific decoders. This representation is further enhanced with a content consistency optimization, ensuring coherent modeling across different weather conditions. Additionally, we design a physics-driven model that simulates dynamic weather effects through particles and motion patterns. Extensive experiments on multiple datasets and various scenes demonstrate that WeatherCity achieves flexible controllability, high fidelity, and temporal consistency in 4D reconstruction and weather editing. Our framework not only enables fine-grained control over weather conditions (e.g., light rain and heavy snow) but also supports object-level manipulation within the scene. Codes are released at https://github.com/IRMVLab/WeatherCity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:58:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxiu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huaxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Baolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUI-Libra: Training Native GUI Agents to Reason and Act with Action-aware Supervision and Partially Verifiable RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T21:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-source native GUI agents still lag behind closed-source systems on long-horizon navigation tasks. This gap stems from two limitations: a shortage of high-quality, action-aligned reasoning data, and the direct adoption of generic post-training pipelines that overlook the unique challenges of GUI agents. We identify two fundamental issues in these pipelines: (i) standard SFT with CoT reasoning often hurts grounding, and (ii) step-wise RLVR-tyle training faces partial verifiability, where multiple actions can be correct but only a single demonstrated action is used for verification. This makes offline step-wise metrics weak predictors of online task success. In this work, we present GUI-Libra, a tailored training recipe that addresses these challenges. First, to mitigate the scarcity of action-aligned reasoning data, we introduce a data construction and filtering pipeline and release a curated 81K GUI reasoning dataset. Second, to reconcile reasoning with grounding, we propose action-aware SFT that mixes reasoning-then-action and direct-action data and reweights tokens to emphasize action and grounding. Third, to stabilize RL under partial verifiability, we identify the overlooked importance of KL regularization in RLVR and show that a KL trust region is critical for improving offline-to-online predictability; we further introduce success-adaptive scaling to downweight unreliable negative gradients. Across diverse web and mobile benchmarks, GUI-Libra consistently improves both step-wise accuracy and end-to-end task completion. Our results suggest that carefully designed post-training and data curation can unlock significantly stronger task-solving capabilities without costly online data collection. We release our dataset, code, and models to facilitate further research on data-efficient post-training for reasoning-capable GUI agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"57 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:40:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doyoung","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Doyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinsoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lohendran","familyName":"Baskaran","name":"Baskaran, Lohendran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Axial-Centric Cross-Plane Attention for 3D Medical Image Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:00:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T05:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:33:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Abridged: Clinicians commonly interpret 3D medical images by examining multiple anatomical planes rather than relying on volumetric views. In clinical CT workflows, the axial plane often serves as the primary diagnostic reference, while the auxiliary planes provide complementary spatial context. However, many existing 3D deep learning approaches either process volumetric data holistically or assign equal importance to all planes, failing to reflect this asymmetric, axial-centric interpretation strategy. To address this, we propose an axial-centric cross-plane attention architecture for 3D medical image classification that models asymmetric dependencies between anatomical planes. The architecture employs large-scale axial CT images pretrained MedDINOv3 as a frozen feature extractor for axial, coronal, and sagittal planes. RICA blocks and intra-plane transformer encoders capture plane-specific positional and contextual information, while axial-centric cross-plane transformer encoders selectively condition axial representations on complementary auxiliary representations. Experiments on six datasets from the MedMNIST3D benchmark show that the proposed method consistently outperforms existing 3D and multi-plane models in ACC and AUC. A lightweight variant, AC-Tiny, achieves competitive performance with substantially fewer trainable parameters, suggesting that architectural design contributes more to performance gains than increased model scale. Ablation studies further validate the importance of axial-centric querying, QKV allocation, directional cross-plane fusion, residual-free cross-attention, and classification head design. Slice-level Grad-CAM visualizations demonstrate that the model identifies diagnostically relevant regions across all planes. These findings highlight the value of aligning architectural design with clinical interpretation workflows for robust 3D medical image analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to BMVC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:40:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21792","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21792","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21792","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Rubin","name":"Rubin, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"p-Hacking Inflates Type I Error Rates in the Error Statistical Approach but not in the Formal Inference Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Other Statistics (stat.OT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:17:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T08:54:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:36:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T13:40:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"p-hacking occurs when researchers conduct multiple significance tests (e.g., p1;H0,1 and p2;H0,2) and then selectively report tests that yield desirable (usually significant) results (e.g., p2 \u0026lt; 0.05;H0,2) without correcting for multiple testing (e.g., 0.05/2 = 0.025). In the present article, I consider p-hacking in the context of two philosophies of significance testing - the error statistical approach and the formal inference approach. I argue that although p-hacking inflates Type I error rates in the error statistical approach, it does not inflate them in the formal inference approach. Specifically, in the error statistical approach, the \"actual\" familywise error rate (e.g., 1 - [1 - 0.05]2 = 0.098 for two independent tests) is relevant because it covers both the reported and unreported tests in the \"actual\" test procedure (i.e., p1;H0,1 and p2;H0,2). In this approach, Type I error rate inflation occurs because the \"actual\" error rate (0.098) is higher than the nominal error rate (0.05). In contrast, in the formal inference approach, the \"actual\" familywise error rate is irrelevant because (a) the researcher does not report a statistical inference about the corresponding intersection null hypothesis (i.e., H0,1 \u0026amp; H0,2), and (b) the \"actual\" familywise error rate does not license inferences about the reported individual hypotheses (i.e., H0,2). Instead, in the formal inference approach, only the nominal error rate is relevant, and a comparison with the \"actual\" error rate is inappropriate. Implications for conceptualizing, demonstrating, and reducing p-hacking are discussed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21792","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:34:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Schote","name":"Schote, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Kolbitsch","name":"Kolbitsch, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostas","familyName":"Papafitsoros","name":"Papafitsoros, Kostas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Kofler","name":"Kofler, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning spatially adaptive sparsity level maps for arbitrary convolutional dictionaries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:13:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T14:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T02:15:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State-of-the-art learned reconstruction methods often rely on black-box modules that, despite their strong performance, raise questions about their interpretability and robustness. Here, we build on a recently proposed image reconstruction method, which is based on embedding data-driven information into a model-based convolutional dictionary regularization via neural network-inferred spatially adaptive sparsity level maps. By means of improved network design and dedicated training strategies, we extend the method to achieve filter-permutation invariance as well as the possibility to change the convolutional dictionary at inference time. We apply our method to low-field MRI and compare it to several other recent deep learning-based methods, also on in vivo data, where the benefit of using a different dictionary is demonstrated. We further assess the method's robustness when tested on in- and out-of-distribution data. When tested on the latter, the proposed method suffers less from the data distribution shift compared to the other learned methods, which we attribute to its reduced reliance on training data due to its underlying model-based reconstruction component."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted for publication at ICIP 2026; differs from previous versions after a bugfix in one of the used packages; corresponds to the final camera-ready version submitted to the conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youguang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Youguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Biros","name":"Biros, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proximal-IMH: Proximal Posterior Proposals for Independent Metropolis-Hastings with Approximate Operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:58:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-17T19:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the problem of sampling from a posterior distribution arising in Bayesian inverse problems in science, engineering, and imaging. Our method belongs to the family of independence Metropolis-Hastings (IMH) sampling algorithms, which are common in Bayesian inference. Relying on the existence of an approximate posterior distribution that is cheaper to sample from but may have significant bias, we introduce Proximal-IMH, a scheme that removes this bias by correcting samples from the approximate posterior through an auxiliary optimization problem. This yields a local adjustment that trades off adherence to the exact model against stability around the approximate reference point. For idealized settings, we prove that the proximal correction tightens the match between approximate and exact posteriors, thereby improving acceptance rates and mixing. The method applies to both linear and nonlinear input-output operators and is particularly suitable for inverse problems where exact posterior sampling is too expensive. We present numerical experiments including multimodal and data-driven priors with nonlinear input-output operators. The results show that Proximal-IMH reliably outperforms existing IMH variants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:56:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeonje","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hyeonje","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongsoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jeongsoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay-Yoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jay-Yoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolMATH: A Diagnostic Benchmark for Long-Horizon Tool Use under Systematic Tool-Catalog Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T09:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T08:26:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:47:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce \\ToolMATH, a math-grounded diagnostic benchmark for evaluating long-horizon tool use under controllable tool-catalog conditions. \\ToolMATH converts stepwise MATH solutions into reusable Python tools with natural-language descriptions and typed schemas, and pairs each problem with a tool environment requiring sequential tool use, intermediate-output reuse, and logically connected tool-call chains. \\ToolMATH controls tool availability and catalog difficulty by constructing gold tools and graded distractors with varying similarity to gold tools. \\ToolMATH also incorporates behavior-conditioned metrics, enabling diagnostic evaluation beyond final accuracy. Building on these measurements, \\ToolMATH emphasizes three evaluation axes: (1) \\emph{Adaptability} measures how much Gold-only success is retained when gold tools are replaced entirely by distractors; (2) \\emph{Robustness} measures stability under adding distractors as a noise; and (3) \\emph{Tool Connectivity} measures whether models preserve accuracy over long executed tool-call chains. Furthermore, trace-level failure analyses characterize how models fail under each tool-catalog condition. Together, these diagnostics reveal distinct model profiles: reliable tool use, tool avoidance, adaptive substitution, and impacts of unreliable tool catalogs. Overall, \\ToolMATH provides a controlled testbed for evaluating how language models adapt to changing tool availability, remain robust to distractors, and maintain correctness across long-horizon tool-use trajectories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to NeurIPS Evaluation \u0026amp; Dataset Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:56:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21545","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21545","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21545","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yequan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yequan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Liyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MUON+: Towards More Effective Muon via One Additional Normalization Step for LLM Pre-training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:04:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T17:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:57:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:38:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Muon has recently emerged as a strong optimizer for large language model pre-training, orthogonalizing the momentum matrix via Newton--Schulz polar iterations. A natural intuition is that polar iterations, by flattening the singular spectrum to all ones, should also eliminate column- and row-wise norm imbalance in the update. We show that this is not true in practice: practical polar steps can substantially amplify the imbalance. We term this the post-polar imbalanced update problem, and prove that such imbalance tightens the second-order term in a blockwise descent analysis, weakening Muon's per-step descent guarantee. Motivated by this analysis, we propose Muon+, a one-line fix that inserts a single normalization step after polar orthogonalization. Muon+ adds no optimizer state. Across pre-training experiments on GPT and LLaMA models from 60M to 7B parameters, spanning both compute-optimal budgets and extended token-to-parameter ratios up to approximately 200, Muon+ consistently outperforms Muon in terms of training and validation perplexity, leading to significant overall pre-training speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21545","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:39:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renyuan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Renyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengping","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shengping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuezhou","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tacmap: Bridging the Tactile Sim-to-Real Gap via Geometry-Consistent Penetration Depth Map"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:40:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T14:33:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:11:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Based Tactile Sensors (VBTS) are essential for achieving dexterous robotic manipulation, yet the tactile sim-to-real gap remains a fundamental bottleneck. Current tactile simulations suffer from a persistent dilemma: simplified geometric projections lack physical authenticity, while high-fidelity Finite Element Methods (FEM) are too computationally prohibitive for large-scale reinforcement learning. In this work, we present Tacmap, a high-fidelity, computationally efficient tactile simulation framework anchored in volumetric penetration depth. Our key insight is to bridge the tactile sim-to-real gap by unifying both domains through a shared deform map representation. Specifically, we compute 3D intersection volumes as depth maps in simulation, while in the real world, we employ an automated data-collection rig to learn a robust mapping from raw tactile images to ground-truth depth maps. By aligning simulation and real-world in this unified geometric space, Tacmap minimizes domain shift while maintaining physical consistency. Quantitative evaluations across diverse contact scenarios demonstrate that Tacmap's deform maps closely mirror real-world measurements. Moreover, we validate the utility of Tacmap through an in-hand rotation task, where a policy trained exclusively in simulation achieves zero-shot transfer to a physical robot."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T03:01:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingcheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowen","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Haowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chuanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhulin","familyName":"An","name":"An, Zhulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songhua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Songhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MultiAnimate: Pose-Guided Image Animation Made Extensible"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:26:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T07:24:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:48:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pose-guided human image animation aims to synthesize realistic videos of a reference character driven by a sequence of poses. While diffusion-based methods have achieved remarkable success, most existing approaches are limited to single-character animation. We observe that naively extending these methods to multi-character scenarios often leads to identity confusion and implausible occlusions between characters. To address these challenges, in this paper, we propose an extensible multi-character image animation framework built upon modern Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) for video generation. At its core, our framework introduces two novel components-Identifier Assigner and Identifier Adapter - which collaboratively capture per-person positional cues and inter-person spatial relationships. This mask-driven scheme, along with a scalable training strategy, not only enhances flexibility but also enables generalization to scenarios with more characters than those seen during training. Remarkably, trained on only a two-character dataset, our model generalizes to multi-character animation while maintaining compatibility with single-character cases. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach achieves state-of-the-art performance in multi-character image animation, surpassing existing diffusion-based baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026 Accepted. Project page at https://hyc001.github.io/MultiAnimate/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:12:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth S. Z.","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Elizabeth S. Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adil","familyName":"Soubki","name":"Soubki, Adil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miles","familyName":"Cranmer","name":"Cranmer, Miles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SymTorch: Symbolic Distillation of Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:17:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:03:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T09:57:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:58:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"What mathematical functions do neural network components learn? Symbolic distillation addresses this question by expressing neural network components with interpretable, closed-form mathematical expressions that expose the functional structure learned during training. We develop symbolic distillation as a systematic, architecture-agnostic methodology, and release our approach as the open-source SymTorch package - a PySR-powered library built natively for the PyTorch ecosystem. Applying this methodology across diverse architectures, we find that SymTorch is successful in the automated discovery of physical laws. Specifically, our approach (1) recovers pairwise interaction forces from graph neural networks trained on empirical $n$-body observations, (2) distills the exact closed-form PDE/ODE solutions of multiple physical systems, including the value of constants, from physics-informed neural networks trained on sparse data, and (3) uncovers the chaotic dynamics of the Lorenz system from high-dimensional data, ultimately outperforming the base neural network on downstream prediction tasks. We further demonstrate the utility of our framework for model interpretability by providing an optimized implementation of SLIME - a symbolic extension to the LIME explainability method. SLIME consistently outperforms LIME across predictive metrics across eight popular classification and regression benchmarks, while still providing an interpretable local symbolic model. Lastly, we investigate replacing transformer MLP layers with symbolic surrogates: replacing 1-7 layers with symbolic approximations yields 2-19\\% throughput improvements and up to 18.7\\% VRAM reduction, with the resulting hybrid models lying on the Pareto front of throughput versus perplexity among open-source LLMs of comparable scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:12:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21858","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21858","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21858","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dezhi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhengzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiliang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Qiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changpeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Changpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProactiveMobile: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Boosting Proactive Intelligence on Mobile Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:32:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T13:46:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:46:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-28T08:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:33:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-08T09:18:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:47:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made significant progress in mobile agent development, yet their capabilities are predominantly confined to a reactive paradigm, where they merely execute explicit user commands. The emerging paradigm of proactive intelligence, where agents autonomously anticipate needs and initiate actions, represents the next frontier for mobile agents. However, its development is critically bottlenecked by the lack of benchmarks that can address real-world complexity and enable objective, executable evaluation. To overcome these challenges, we introduce ProactiveMobile, a comprehensive benchmark designed to systematically advance research in this domain. ProactiveMobile formalizes the proactive task as inferring latent user intent across four dimensions of on-device contextual signals and generating an executable function sequence from a comprehensive function pool of 63 APIs. The benchmark features over 3,660 instances of 14 scenarios that embrace real-world complexity through multi-answer annotations. To ensure quality, a team of 30 experts conducts a final audit of the benchmark, verifying factual accuracy, logical consistency, and action feasibility, and correcting any non-compliant entries. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our fine-tuned Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct achieves a success rate of 19.15%, outperforming o1 (15.71%) and GPT-5 (7.39%). This result indicates that proactivity is a critical competency widely lacking in current MLLMs, yet it is learnable, emphasizing the importance of the proposed benchmark for proactivity evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21858","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:47:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang-Duo","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Fang-Duo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei-Yueh","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fei-Yueh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsueh-Wei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Hsueh-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Jaw","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wei-Jaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Chung","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao-Chung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIDI-Informed Singing Accompaniment Generation in a Compositional Song Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T06:43:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:59:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T13:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While end-to-end lyrics-to-song models offer convenience for casual users, professional songwriters require score-to-song systems that allow them to retain authorship over the core melody. However, existing score-to-song methods are limited to short-form snippets and fail to maintain coherence in long-form generation, particularly during vocal-silent sections like intros and bridges. To address this long-form bottleneck, we propose MIDI-informed singing accompaniment generation (MIDI-SAG). Unlike conventional audio-only models, MIDI-SAG utilizes symbolic timing and chord information derived from the vocal MIDI to provide a stable musical roadmap. By incorporating structure planning, which defines temporal boundaries and semantic labels, our framework facilitates consistent generation across both vocal and non-vocal sections. We demonstrate the feasibility of this compositional pipeline by leveraging specialized pre-trained modules, enabling data-efficient training on a single GPU. Our experiments show the potential of this approach for both professional score-to-song and general lyrics-to-song tasks. While an early exploration, MIDI-SAG suggests a promising direction for structured, long-form music synthesis. Audio demos are available, and the code will be open-sourced at https://composerflow.github.io/web_revealed/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:18:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21720","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21720","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21720","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Silvi","name":"Silvi, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ponrawee","familyName":"Prasertsom","name":"Prasertsom, Ponrawee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Culbertson","name":"Culbertson, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devdatt","familyName":"Dubhashi","name":"Dubhashi, Devdatt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moa","familyName":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Moa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenny","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Kenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the relationship between regularity and learnability in recursive numeral systems using Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T08:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:35:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human recursive numeral systems (i.e., counting systems such as English base-10 numerals), like many other grammatical systems, are highly regular. Following prior work that relates cross-linguistic tendencies to biases in learning, we ask whether regular systems are common because regularity facilitates learning. Adopting methods from the Reinforcement Learning literature, we confirm that highly regular human(-like) systems are easier to learn than unattested but possible irregular systems. This asymmetry emerges under the natural assumption that recursive numeral systems are designed for generalisation from limited data to represent all integers exactly. We also find that the influence of regularity on learnability is absent for unnatural, highly irregular systems, whose learnability is influenced instead by signal length, suggesting that different pressures may influence learnability differently in different parts of the space of possible numeral systems. Our results contribute to the body of work linking learnability to cross-linguistic prevalence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21720","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:24:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Germán T.","familyName":"Eizaguirre","name":"Eizaguirre, Germán T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Tissen","name":"Tissen, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Sánchez-Artigas","name":"Sánchez-Artigas, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Both Ends Count! Just How Good are LLM Agents at \"Text-to-Big SQL\"?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:12:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T14:47:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:49:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T14:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-13T13:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:35:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3805621.3807640","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-SQL and Big Data are both extensively benchmarked fields, yet there is limited research that evaluates them jointly. In the real world, Text-to-SQL systems are often embedded with Big Data workflows, such as large-scale data processing or interactive data analytics. We refer to this as ``Text-to-Big SQL''. However, existing text-to-SQL benchmarks remain narrowly scoped and overlook the cost and performance implications that arise at scale. For instance, translation errors that are minor on small datasets lead to substantial cost and latency overheads as data scales, a relevant issue completely ignored by text-to-SQL metrics.\n In this paper, we overcome this overlooked challenge by introducing novel and representative metrics for evaluating Text-to-Big SQL. Our study focuses on production-level LLM agents, a database-agnostic system adaptable to diverse user needs. Via an extensive evaluation of frontier models, we show that text-to-SQL metrics are insufficient for Big Data. In contrast, our proposed text-to-Big SQL metrics accurately reflect execution efficiency, cost, and the impact of data scale. For example, GPT-4o compensates for roughly 7% lower accuracy than the top-performing later-generation models with up to a 12.16x speedup, while GPT-5.2 is more than twice as cost-effective as Gemini 3 Pro at large input scales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:24:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21750","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21750","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21750","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleena","familyName":"Siji","name":"Siji, Aleena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Mohammad Karimi","familyName":"Mamaghan","name":"Mamaghan, Amir Mohammad Karimi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdinand","familyName":"Kapl","name":"Kapl, Ferdinand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Höppe","name":"Höppe, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanouil","familyName":"Angelis","name":"Angelis, Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Dittadi","name":"Dittadi, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maurice","familyName":"Brenner","name":"Brenner, Maurice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heinzinger","name":"Heinzinger, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl Henrik","familyName":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Karl Henrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitlin","familyName":"Maile","name":"Maile, Kaitlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"von Oswald","name":"von Oswald, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Words to Amino Acids: Does the Curse of Depth Persist?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:06:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-24T15:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-27T00:45:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protein language models (PLMs) have become widely adopted as general-purpose models, demonstrating strong performance in protein engineering and de novo design. Like large language models (LLMs), they are typically trained as deep transformers with next-token or masked-token prediction objectives on massive sequence corpora and are scaled by increasing model depth. Recent work on autoregressive LLMs has identified the Curse of Depth: many later layers contribute little to the final output predictions. These findings naturally raise the question of whether a similar depth inefficiency also appears in PLMs, where many widely used models are not autoregressive, and some are multimodal, accepting both protein sequence and structure as input. In this work, we present a depth analysis of seven popular PLM families across model scales, spanning autoregressive, masked, and diffusion objectives, and quantify how layer contributions evolve with depth using a unified set of probing-, perturbation-, and downstream-evaluation measurements. Across models, we observe consistent depth-dependent patterns that extend prior findings on LLMs: a large fraction of task-relevant computation is concentrated in a subset of layers, while the remaining layers mainly provide incremental refinement of the final prediction. These trends persist beyond sequence-only settings and also appear in multimodal PLMs. Taken together, our results suggest that depth inefficiency is a common feature of modern PLMs, motivating future work on more depth-efficient architectures and training methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21750","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-27T01:53:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayden","familyName":"Higgins","name":"Higgins, Ayden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jackknife Inference for Fixed Effects Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:33:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-22T07:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:34:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops a general method of inference for fixed effects models which is (i) automatic, (ii) computationally inexpensive, (iii) tuning parameter-free, and (iv) highly model agnostic. Specifically, we show how to combine a collection of subsample estimators into a jackknife $t$-statistic, from which hypothesis tests, confidence intervals, and $p$-values are readily obtained."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:26:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21394","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21394","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21394","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehran","familyName":"Kafai","name":"Kafai, Mehran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Habas","name":"Habas, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MemoPhishAgent: Memory-Augmented Multi-Modal LLM Agent for Phishing URL Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:17:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T19:48:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:08:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional phishing website detection relies on static heuristics or reference lists, which lag behind rapidly evolving attacks. While recent systems incorporate large language models (LLMs), they are still prompt-based, deterministic pipelines that underutilize reasoning capability. We present MemoPhishAgent (MPA), a memory-augmented multi-modal LLM agent that dynamically orchestrates phishing-specific tools and leverages episodic memories of past reasoning trajectories to guide decisions on recurring and novel threats. On two public datasets, MPA outperforms three state-of-the-art (SOTA) baselines, improving recall by 13.6%. To better reflect realistic, user-facing phishing detection performance, we further evaluate MPA on a benchmark of real-world suspicious URLs actively crawled from five social media platforms, where it improves recall by 20%. Detailed analysis shows episodic memory contributes up to 27% recall gain without introducing additional computational overhead. The ablation study confirms the necessity of the agent-based approach compared to prompt-based baselines and validates the effectiveness of our tool design. Finally, MPA is deployed in production, processing 60K targeted high-risk URLs weekly, and achieving 91.44% recall, providing proactive protection for millions of customers. Together, our results show that combining multi-modal reasoning with episodic memory yields robust phishing detection in realistic user-exposure settings. Our implementation is available at https://github.com/XuanChen-xc/MemoPhishAgent.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Industry Track Camera Ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21394","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:26:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuancheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haihua","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ImpRIF: Stronger Implicit Reasoning Leads to Better Complex Instruction Following"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-04T07:50:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T12:48:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As applications of large language models (LLMs) become increasingly complex, the demand for robust complex instruction following capabilities is growing accordingly. We argue that a thorough understanding of the instruction itself, especially the latent reasoning structure embedded between the lines, is crucial for improving instruction following. Therefore we target complex instructions that involve implicit reasoning, intricate logical relations, and multi-constraint dependencies. We propose ImpRIF, a method to enhance LLMs' understanding of implicit reasoning instructions, thereby improving its ability to follow complex instructions. We formalize such instructions as verifiable reasoning graphs, enabling programmatic verification and graph-driven chain-of-thought reasoning. Based on this formulation, we synthesize large-scale single- and multi-turn data, propose fine-tuning with graph reasoning, and apply reinforcement learning to explicitly train models to reason along the graph. On five complex instruction following benchmarks, our models substantially outperform their base models. These results demonstrate that enhancing implicit reasoning capabilities can significantly improve complex instruction following."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:05Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:59:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xudong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachuan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jiachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne","familyName":"Frey-Marti","name":"Frey-Marti, Marianne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MEDSYN: Benchmarking Multi-EviDence SYNthesis in Complex Clinical Cases for Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:33:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T05:14:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:23:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:38:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:15:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have shown great potential in medical applications, yet existing benchmarks inadequately capture real-world clinical complexity. We introduce MEDSYN, a multilingual, multimodal benchmark of highly complex clinical cases with up to 7 distinct visual clinical evidence (CE) types per case. Mirroring clinical workflow, we evaluate 18 MLLMs on differential diagnosis (DDx) generation and final diagnosis (FDx) selection. While top models often match or even outperform human experts on DDx generation, all MLLMs exhibit a much larger DDx--FDx performance gap compared to expert clinicians, indicating a failure mode in synthesis of heterogeneous CE types. Ablations attribute this failure to (i) overreliance on less discriminative textual CE ($\\it{e.g.}$, medical history) and (ii) a cross-modal CE utilization gap. We introduce Evidence Sensitivity to quantify the latter and show that a smaller gap correlates with higher diagnostic accuracy. Finally, we demonstrate how it can be used to guide interventions to improve model performance. We will open-source our benchmark and code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:12:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangcong","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Fangcong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, Howard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Danqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DySCO: Dynamic Attention-Scaling Decoding for Long-Context Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T15:31:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:58:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning over long contexts is a crucial capability for language models (LMs). Although recent models support increasingly long context windows, their accuracy often deteriorates as input length grows. In practice, models often struggle to keep attention aligned with the most relevant context throughout decoding. In this work, we propose DYSCO, a novel decoding algorithm for improving long-context reasoning. DYSCO leverages retrieval heads--a subset of attention heads specialized for longcontext retrieval--to identify task-relevant tokens at each decoding step and explicitly up-weight them. By doing so, DYSCO dynamically adjusts attention during generation to better utilize relevant context. The method is training-free and can be applied directly to any off-the-shelf LMs. Across multiple instruction-tuned and reasoning models, DYSCO consistently improves performance on challenging long-context reasoning benchmarks, yielding relative gains of up to 25% on MRCR and LongBenchV2 at 128K context length with modest additional compute. Further analysis highlights the importance of both dynamic attention rescaling and retrievalhead guided selection for the effectiveness of the method, while providing interpretability insights into decoding-time attention behavior. Our code is available at https://github.com/princeton-pli/DySCO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:21:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhineet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Abhineet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Rozeboom","name":"Rozeboom, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilanjan","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Nilanjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tokenizing Semantic Segmentation with Run Length Encoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:44:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T22:50:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:57:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T04:39:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a new unified approach to semantic segmentation in both images and videos by using language modeling to output the masks as sequences of discrete tokens. We use run length encoding (RLE) to discretize the segmentation masks, and adapt the Pix2Seq framework to learn autoregressive models to output these tokens. We propose novel tokenization strategies to compress the lengths of the token sequences to make it practicable to extend this approach to videos. We also show how instance information can be incorporated into the tokenization process to perform panoptic segmentation. We evaluate our models on two domain-specific datasets to demonstrate their competitiveness with the state of the art in certain scenarios, in spite of being severely bottlenecked by our limited computational resources. We supplement these analyses by proposing several promising approaches to foster future competitiveness in general-purpose applications, and facilitate this by making our code and models publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and models available at: https://github.com/abhineet123/p2s-video"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:23:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21922","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21922","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21922","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Rizzi","name":"Rizzi, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Persistent Brownian Motions in Confluent Tissues"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Tissues and Organs (q-bio.TO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:00:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:52:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/g6l8-wbt1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biological tissues are active materials whose non-equilibrium dynamics emerge from distinct cellular force-generating mechanisms. Using a two-dimensional active foam model, we compare the effects of traction forces and junctional tension fluctuations on confluent tissue dynamics. While these two modes of activity produce qualitatively different cell shapes, rearrangement statistics, and spatiotemporal correlations in fluid states, we find that the long-time cellular motion universally converges to persistent Brownian dynamics. This universal feature contrasts with the non-universal correlations between cell geometry, rearrangement rate, and fluidity, which depend sensitively on the underlying modes of active force. Our results demonstrate that persistent Brownian motion provides a minimal framework for describing tissue dynamics, while distinct active forces leave identifiable structural and dynamical signatures, thereby enabling inference of the dominant active force in fluid state tissues."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21922","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:25:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yushen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Unsupervised and Sparsely-Supervised 3D Object Detection by Semantic Pseudo-Labeling and Prototype Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:26:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T08:42:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:57:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D object detection is essential for autonomous driving and robotic perception, yet its reliance on large-scale manually annotated data limits scalability and adaptability. To reduce annotation dependency, unsupervised and sparsely-supervised paradigms have emerged. However, they face intertwined challenges: low-quality pseudo-labels, unstable feature mining, and a lack of a unified training framework. This paper proposes SPL, a unified training framework for both unsupervised and sparsely-supervised 3D object detection via \\underline{S}emantic \\underline{P}seudo-labeling and prototype \\underline{L}earning. SPL first generates high-quality pseudo-labels by integrating image semantics, point cloud geometry, and temporal cues, producing both 3D bounding boxes for dense objects and 3D point labels for sparse ones. These pseudo-labels are not used directly but as probabilistic priors within a novel, multi-stage prototype learning strategy. This strategy stabilizes feature representation learning through memory-based initialization and momentum-based prototype updating, effectively mining features from both labeled and unlabeled data. Extensive experiments on KITTI and nuScenes datasets demonstrate that SPL significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both settings. Our work provides a robust and generalizable solution for learning 3D object detectors with minimal or no manual annotations. Our code is available at https://github.com/TossherO/SPL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:25:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binesh","familyName":"Sadanandan","name":"Sadanandan, Binesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vahid","familyName":"Behzadan","name":"Behzadan, Vahid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PSF-Med: Measuring and Explaining Paraphrase Sensitivity in Medical Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:03:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T20:52:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:13:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical Vision Language Models (VLMs) can change their answers when clinicians rephrase the same question, a failure mode that threatens deployment safety. We introduce PSF-Med, a benchmark of 26,850 chest X-ray questions paired with 92,856 meaning-preserving paraphrases across MIMIC-CXR, PadChest, and VinDr-CXR, spanning clinical populations in the US, Spain, and Vietnam. Every paraphrase is validated by an LLM judge using a bidirectional clinical entailment rubric, with 91.6% cross-family agreement. Across nine VLMs, including general-purpose models, we find flip rates from 3% to 37%. However, low flip rate does not imply visual grounding: text-only baselines show that some models stay consistent even when the image is removed, suggesting they rely on language priors. To study mechanisms in one model, we apply GemmaScope 2 Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) to MedGemma 4B and analyze FlipBank, a curated set of 158 flip cases. We identify a sparse feature at layer 17 that correlates with prompt framing and predicts decision margin shifts. In causal patching, removing this feature's contribution recovers 45% of the yes-minus-no logit margin on average and fully reverses 15% of flips. Acting on this finding, we show that clamping the identified feature at inference reduces flip rates by 31% relative with only a 1.3 percentage-point accuracy cost, while also decreasing text-prior reliance. These results suggest that flip rate alone is not enough; robustness evaluations should test both paraphrase stability and image reliance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:25:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin Song","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jin Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditFlow: Benchmarking and Optimizing Code Edit Recommendation Systems via Reconstruction of Developer Flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:02:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T01:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:14:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T02:21:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:19:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3798249","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) for code editing have achieved remarkable progress, yet recent empirical studies reveal a fundamental disconnect between technical accuracy and developer productivity. Despite their strong benchmark performance, developers complete tasks 19% slower when using AI assistance, with over 68.81% of recommendations disrupting their mental flow. This misalignment stems from the use of static commit snapshots that lack temporal information, causing models to optimize for end results rather than the incremental, context-sensitive steps that align with developers' natural reasoning process.\n To bridge this gap, we present EditFlow, which benchmarks and optimizes subsequent code edit recommendation systems through the reconstruction of developer editing flows. EditFlow addresses three key challenges. First, collecting edit-order data that reflects developers' flow is inherently difficult: manual annotation introduces prohibitive overhead, while development logs capture only single trajectories instead of all plausible editing flows. Second, benchmarking recommendation performance against developers' ongoing editing flow requires a digital-twin-like simulation that can faithfully simulate the editing process. Third, existing heterogeneous systems vary drastically in scale and architecture, posing challenges for developing a unified optimization strategy that endows all models with mental-flow awareness regardless of design or capability.\n ......"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to the Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages (OOPSLA 2026). (Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 10, OOPSLA1)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:24:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21655","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21655","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21655","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijiang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anxiang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Anxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jianqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CCCaption: Dual-Reward Reinforcement Learning for Complete and Correct Image Captioning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:34:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-28T14:51:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image captioning remains a fundamental task for vision language understanding, yet ground-truth supervision still relies predominantly on human-annotated references. Because human annotations reflect subjective preferences and expertise, ground-truth captions are often incomplete or even incorrect, which in turn limits caption models. We argue that caption quality should be assessed by two objective aspects: completeness (does the caption cover all salient visual facts?) and correctness (are the descriptions true with respect to the image?). To this end, we introduce CCCaption: a dual-reward reinforcement learning framework with a dedicated fine-tuning corpus that explicitly optimizes these properties to generate \\textbf{C}omplete and \\textbf{C}orrect \\textbf{Captions}. For completeness, we use diverse LVLMs to disentangle the image into a set of visual queries, and reward captions that answer more of these queries, with a dynamic query sampling strategy to improve training efficiency. For correctness, we penalize captions that contain hallucinations by validating the authenticity of sub-caption queries, which are derived from the caption decomposition. Our symmetric dual-reward optimization jointly maximizes completeness and correctness, guiding models toward captions that better satisfy these objective criteria. Extensive experiments across standard captioning benchmarks show consistent improvements, offering a principled path to training caption models beyond human-annotation imitation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accept by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21655","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:10:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I-Hsiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, I-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tse-Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tse-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chien","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Yu-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jen-Chien","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jen-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Ting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVisRAG: Causality-Aware Vision-Based Retrieval-Augmented Generation under Visual Degradations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:27:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T07:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:36:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-08T06:31:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-26T08:59:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:39:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (VisRAG) leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to jointly retrieve relevant visual documents and generate grounded answers based on multimodal evidence. However, existing VisRAG models degrade in performance when visual inputs suffer from distortions such as blur, noise, low light, or shadow, where semantic and degradation factors become entangled within pretrained visual encoders, leading to errors in both retrieval and generation stages. To address this limitation, we introduce RobustVisRAG, a causality-guided dual-path framework that improves VisRAG robustness while preserving efficiency and zero-shot generalization. RobustVisRAG uses a non-causal path to capture degradation signals through unidirectional attention and a causal path to learn purified semantics guided by these signals. Together with the proposed Non-Causal Distortion Modeling and Causal Semantic Alignment objectives, the framework enforces a clear separation between semantics and degradations, enabling stable retrieval and generation under challenging visual conditions. To evaluate robustness under realistic conditions, we introduce the Distortion-VisRAG dataset, a large-scale benchmark containing both synthetic and real-world degraded documents across seven domains, with 12 synthetic and 5 real distortion types that comprehensively reflect practical visual degradations. Experimental results show that RobustVisRAG improves retrieval, generation, and end-to-end performance by 7.35%, 6.35%, and 12.40%, respectively, on real-world degradations, while maintaining comparable accuracy on clean inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR2026; Project Page: https://robustvisrag.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:25:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Kaltheuner","name":"Kaltheuner, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Dröge","name":"Dröge, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Plack","name":"Plack, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Stotko","name":"Stotko, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reinhard","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Reinhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neu-PiG: Neural Preconditioned Grids for Fast Dynamic Surface Reconstruction on Long Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:59:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:10:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T08:31:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:38:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Temporally consistent surface reconstruction of dynamic 3D objects from unstructured point cloud data remains challenging, especially for very long sequences. Existing methods either optimize deformations incrementally, risking drift and requiring long runtimes, or rely on complex learned models that demand category-specific training. We present Neu-PiG, a fast deformation optimization method based on a novel preconditioned latent-grid encoding that distributes spatial features parameterized on the position and normal direction of a keyframe surface. Our method encodes entire deformations across all time steps at various spatial scales into a multi-resolution latent grid, parameterized by the position and normal direction of a reference surface from a single keyframe. This latent representation is then augmented for time modulation and decoded into per-frame 6-DoF deformations via a lightweight multilayer perceptron (MLP). To achieve high-fidelity, drift-free surface reconstructions in seconds, we employ Sobolev preconditioning during gradient-based training of the latent space, completely avoiding the need for any explicit correspondences or further priors. Experiments across diverse human and animal datasets demonstrate that Neu-PiG outperforms state-the-art approaches, offering both superior accuracy and scalability to long sequences while running at least 60x faster than existing training-free methods and achieving inference speeds on the same order as heavy pretrained models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2026, Code: https://github.com/vc-bonn/neu-pig"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:34:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onur C.","familyName":"Koyun","name":"Koyun, Onur C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammet Fatih","familyName":"Demir","name":"Demir, Muhammet Fatih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Metin Nafi","familyName":"Gurcan","name":"Gurcan, Metin Nafi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Momentum Memory for Knowledge Distillation in Computational Pathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:51:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T14:47:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T20:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:12:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal learning that integrates genomics and histopathology has shown strong potential in cancer diagnosis, yet its clinical translation is hindered by the limited availability of paired histology-genomics data. Knowledge distillation (KD) offers a practical solution by transferring genomic supervision into histopathology models, enabling accurate inference using histology alone. However, existing KD methods rely on batch-local alignment, which introduces instability due to limited within-batch comparisons and ultimately degrades performance.\n To address these limitations, we propose Momentum Memory Knowledge Distillation (MoMKD), a cross-modal distillation framework driven by a momentum-updated memory. This memory aggregates genomic and histopathology information across batches, effectively enlarging the supervisory context available to each mini-batch. Furthermore, we decouple the gradients of the genomics and histology branches, preventing genomic signals from dominating histology feature learning during training and eliminating the modality-gap issue at inference time.\n Extensive experiments on the TCGA-BRCA benchmark (HER2, PR, and ODX classification tasks) and an independent in-house testing dataset demonstrate that MoMKD consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MIL and multimodal KD baselines, delivering strong performance and generalization under histology-only inference. Overall, MoMKD establishes a robust and generalizable knowledge distillation paradigm for computational pathology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026. Code: https://github.com/CAIR-LAB-WFUSM/MoMKD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:36:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyi","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yudong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Juyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Easy3E: Feed-Forward 3D Asset Editing via Rectified Voxel Flow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:15:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:18:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-22T07:26:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:59:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing 3D editing methods rely on computationally intensive scene-by-scene iterative optimization and suffer from multi-view inconsistency. We propose an effective and feed-forward 3D editing framework based on the TRELLIS generative backbone, capable of modifying 3D models from a single editing view. Our framework addresses two key issues: adapting training-free 2D editing to structured 3D representations, and overcoming the bottleneck of appearance fidelity in compressed 3D features. To ensure geometric consistency, we introduce Voxel FlowEdit, an edit-driven flow in the sparse voxel latent space that achieves globally consistent 3D deformation in a single pass. To restore high-fidelity details, we develop a normal-guided single to multi-view generation module as an external appearance prior, successfully recovering high-frequency textures. Experiments demonstrate that our method enables fast, globally consistent, and high-fidelity 3D model editing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2026, Project Page: https://ustc3dv.github.io/Easy3E/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:24:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Rui","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"QiangQiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, QiangQiang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Song","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Song","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Lin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yifan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Decheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Decheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Linchuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Linchuan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Rubing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rubing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Guanghua","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guanghua","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Jianchen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jianchen","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AngelSlim: A more accessible, comprehensive, and efficient toolkit for large model compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-07T07:02:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:50:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:24:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T12:35:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:53:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This technical report introduces AngelSlim, a comprehensive and versatile toolkit for large model compression developed by the Tencent Hunyuan team. By consolidating cutting-edge algorithms, including quantization, speculative decoding, token pruning, and distillation. AngelSlim provides a unified pipeline that streamlines the transition from model compression to industrial-scale deployment. To facilitate efficient acceleration, we integrate state-of-the-art FP8 and INT8 Post-Training Quantization (PTQ) algorithms alongside pioneering research in ultra-low-bit regimes, featuring HY-1.8B-int2 as the first industrially viable 2-bit large model. Beyond quantization, we propose a training-aligned speculative decoding framework compatible with multimodal architectures and modern inference engines, achieving 1.8x to 2.0x throughput gains without compromising output correctness. Furthermore, we develop a training-free sparse attention framework that reduces Time-to-First-Token (TTFT) in long-context scenarios by decoupling sparse kernels from model architectures through a hybrid of static patterns and dynamic token selection. For multimodal models, AngelSlim incorporates specialized pruning strategies, namely IDPruner for optimizing vision tokens via Maximal Marginal Relevance and Samp for adaptive audio token merging and pruning. By integrating these compression strategies from low-level implementations, AngelSlim enables algorithm-focused research and tool-assisted deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:24:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Galozy","name":"Galozy, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Structural Non-Preservation of Epistemic Behaviour under Policy Transformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-20T01:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:20:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) agents under partial observability often condition actions on internally accumulated information such as memory or inferred latent context. We formalise such information-conditioned interaction patterns as behavioural dependency: variation in action selection with respect to internal information under fixed observations. This induces a probe-relative notion of $ε$-behavioural equivalence and a within-policy behavioural distance that quantifies probe sensitivity. We establish three structural results. First, the set of policies exhibiting non-trivial behavioural dependency is not closed under convex aggregation. Second, behavioural distance contracts under convex combination. Third, we prove a sufficient local condition under which gradient ascent on a skewed mixture objective decreases behavioural distance when a dominant-mode gradient aligns with the direction of steepest contraction. Minimal bandit and partially observable gridworld experiments provide controlled witnesses of these mechanisms. In the examined settings, behavioural distance decreases under convex aggregation and under continued optimisation with skewed latent priors, and in these experiments it precedes degradation under latent prior shift. These results identify structural conditions under which probe-conditioned behavioural separation is not preserved under common policy transformations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures. Under review at RLC 2026. Fixed references due to copy-paste errors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heejin","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Heejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Architecture Determines Reasoning Quality: A Variable Isolation Study on the Car Wash Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:40:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T16:28:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T01:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models consistently fail the \"car wash problem,\" a viral reasoning benchmark requiring implicit physical constraint inference. We present a variable isolation study (n=20 per condition, 6 conditions, 120 total trials) examining which prompt architecture layers in a production system enable correct reasoning. Using Claude 3.5 Sonnet with controlled hyperparameters (temperature 0.7, top_p 1.0), we find that the STAR (Situation-Task-Action-Result) reasoning framework alone raises accuracy from 0% to 85% (p=0.001, Fisher's exact test, odds ratio 13.22). Adding user profile context via vector database retrieval provides a further 10 percentage point gain, while RAG context contributes an additional 5 percentage points, achieving 100% accuracy in the full-stack condition. These results suggest that structured reasoning scaffolds -- specifically, forced goal articulation before inference -- matter substantially more than context injection for implicit constraint reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:42:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunggi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chunggi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayato","familyName":"Saiki","name":"Saiki, Hayato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tica","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eiji","familyName":"Ikeda","name":"Ikeda, Eiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhu-Tian","name":"Zhu-Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanspeter","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Hanspeter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViSTAR: Virtual Skill Training with Augmented Reality with 3D Avatars and LLM coaching agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:26:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T15:08:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:20:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ViSTAR, a Virtual Skill Training system in AR that supports self-guided basketball skill practice, with feedback on balance, posture, and timing. From a formative study with basketball players and coaches, the system addresses three challenges: understanding skills, identifying errors, and correcting mistakes. ViSTAR follows the Behavioral Skills Training (BST) framework-instruction, modeling, rehearsal, and feedback. It provides feedback through visual overlays, rhythm and timing cues, and an AI-powered coaching agent using 3D motion reconstruction. We generate verbal feedback by analyzing spatio-temporal joint data and mapping features to natural-language coaching cues via a Large Language Model (LLM). A key novelty is this feedback generation: motion features become concise coaching insights. In two studies (N=16), participants generally preferred our AI-generated feedback to coach feedback and reported that ViSTAR helped them notice posture and balance issues and refine movements beyond self-observation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:59:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21818","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21818","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21818","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guibin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dixuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangping","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiangping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youqiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Youqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengcong","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Zhengcong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Debang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaofeng","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Chaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikun","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Yikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tuanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingshan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Mingshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqiang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhe","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yuzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoxuan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Baoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binjie","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Binjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunjie","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yunjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jujie","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaojie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingchen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyue","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xuanyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuangzhuang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhuangzhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaifei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binglu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Binglu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhui","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Tianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max W. Y.","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Max W. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Hung","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chien-Hung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SkyReels-V4: Multi-modal Video-Audio Generation, Inpainting and Editing model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:47:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T06:24:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:27:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T09:20:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:59:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"SkyReels V4 is a unified multi modal video foundation model for joint video audio generation, inpainting, and editing. The model adopts a dual stream Multimodal Diffusion Transformer (MMDiT) architecture, where one branch synthesizes video and the other generates temporally aligned audio, while sharing a powerful text encoder based on the Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLM). SkyReels V4 accepts rich multi modal instructions, including text, images, video clips, masks, and audio references. By combining the MLLMs multi modal instruction following capability with in context learning in the video branch MMDiT, the model can inject fine grained visual guidance under complex conditioning, while the audio branch MMDiT simultaneously leverages audio references to guide sound generation. On the video side, we adopt a channel concatenation formulation that unifies a wide range of inpainting style tasks, such as image to video, video extension, and video editing under a single interface, and naturally extends to vision referenced inpainting and editing via multi modal prompts. SkyReels V4 supports up to 1080p resolution, 32 FPS, and 15 second duration, enabling high fidelity, multi shot, cinema level video generation with synchronized audio. To make such high resolution, long-duration generation computationally feasible, we introduce an efficiency strategy: Joint generation of low resolution full sequences and high-resolution keyframes, followed by dedicated super-resolution and frame interpolation models. To our knowledge, SkyReels V4 is the first video foundation model that simultaneously supports multi-modal input, joint video audio generation, and a unified treatment of generation, inpainting, and editing, while maintaining strong efficiency and quality at cinematic resolutions and durations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21818","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:59:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Bokser","name":"Bokser, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Gonzalez","name":"Gonzalez, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Preston","name":"Preston, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Bahner","name":"Bahner, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annika","familyName":"Wollschläger","name":"Wollschläger, Annika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arianna","familyName":"Ilvonen","name":"Ilvonen, Arianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asa","familyName":"Eckert-Erdheim","name":"Eckert-Erdheim, Asa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwin","familyName":"Khadke","name":"Khadke, Ashwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilal","familyName":"Hammoud","name":"Hammoud, Bilal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dean","familyName":"Molinaro","name":"Molinaro, Dean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Jenelten","name":"Jenelten, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Mayne","name":"Mayne, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howie","familyName":"Choset","name":"Choset, Howie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Bogoslavskyi","name":"Bogoslavskyi, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itic","familyName":"Tinman","name":"Tinman, Itic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Tigue","name":"Tigue, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Preisig","name":"Preisig, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenny","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Kenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kim","familyName":"Ang","name":"Ang, Kim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liana","familyName":"Margolese","name":"Margolese, Liana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Frias","name":"Frias, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Drews","name":"Drews, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Boggavarapu","name":"Boggavarapu, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick","familyName":"Burnham","name":"Burnham, Rick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Zapolsky","name":"Zapolsky, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangbae","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangbae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Biddlestone","name":"Biddlestone, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Mayorga","name":"Mayorga, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shamel","familyName":"Fahmi","name":"Fahmi, Shamel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surya","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"McCollum","name":"McCollum, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Velin","familyName":"Dimitrov","name":"Dimitrov, Velin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Moyne","name":"Moyne, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Ming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yu-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farbod","familyName":"Farshidian","name":"Farshidian, Farbod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Hutter","name":"Hutter, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Perry","name":"Perry, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Al","familyName":"Rizzi","name":"Rizzi, Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabe","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Gabe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"System Design of the Ultra Mobility Vehicle: A Driving, Balancing, and Jumping Bicycle Robot"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:05:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T18:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:59:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T16:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-18T01:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trials cyclists and mountain bike riders can hop, jump, balance, and drive on one or both wheels. This versatility allows them to achieve speed and energy-efficiency on smooth terrain and agility over rough terrain. Inspired by these athletes, we present the design and control of a robotic platform, Ultra Mobility Vehicle (UMV), which combines a bicycle and a reaction mass to move dynamically with minimal actuated degrees of freedom. We employ a simulation-driven design optimization process to synthesize a spatial linkage topology with a focus on vertical jump height and momentum-based balancing on a single wheel contact. Using a constrained Reinforcement Learning (RL) framework, we demonstrate zero-shot transfer of diverse athletic behaviors, including track-stands, jumps, wheelies, rear wheel hopping, and front flips. This 23.5 kg robot is capable of high speeds (8 m/s) and jumping on and over large obstacles (1 m tall, or 130% of the robot's nominal height)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 Pages, 11 figures, 3 movies, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:53:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hexin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan Zhong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xuan Zhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan Clint","familyName":"Legasto","name":"Legasto, Alan Clint","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingquan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Mingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhe","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Shih","name":"Shih, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overview of the CXR-LT 2026 Challenge: Multi-Center Long-Tailed and Zero Shot Chest X-ray Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T03:39:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:31:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chest X-ray (CXR) interpretation is hindered by the long-tailed distribution of pathologies and the open-world nature of clinical environments. Existing benchmarks often rely on closed-set classes from single institutions, failing to capture the prevalence of rare diseases or the appearance of novel findings. To address this, we present the CXR-LT 2026 challenge. This third iteration of the benchmark introduces a multi-center dataset comprising over 145,000 images from PadChest and NIH Chest X-ray datasets. The challenge defines two core tasks: (1) Robust Multi-Label Classification on 30 known classes and (2) Open-World Generalization to 6 unseen (out-of-distribution) rare disease classes. We report the results of the top-performing teams, evaluating them via mean Average Precision (mAP), AUROC, and F1-score. The winning solutions achieved an mAP of 0.5854 on Task 1 and 0.4315 on Task 2, demonstrating that large-scale vision-language pre-training significantly mitigates the performance drop typically associated with zero-shot diagnosis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:39Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:53:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21435","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21435","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21435","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqiong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bobo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinkai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangtai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangtai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Furu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuicheng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shuicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synergizing Understanding and Generation with Interleaved Analyzing-Drafting Thinking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:26:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T16:12:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T01:19:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unified Vision-Language Models (UVLMs) aim to advance multimodal learning by supporting both understanding and generation within a single framework. However, existing approaches largely focus on architectural unification while overlooking the need for explicit interaction between the two capabilities during task solving. As a result, current models treat understanding and generation as parallel skills rather than synergistic processes. To achieve real synergy, we introduce the interleaved Analyzing-Drafting problem-solving loop (AD-Loop), a new think paradigm that dynamically alternates between analytic and drafting operations. By interleaving textual thoughts with visual thoughts, AD-Loop enables models to iteratively refine both comprehension and outputs, fostering genuine synergy. To train this mechanism, we design a two-stage strategy: supervised learning on interleaved thought data to initialize alternation, followed by reinforcement learning to promote adaptive and autonomous control. Extensive experiments demonstrate that AD-Loop consistently improves performance across standard benchmarks for both understanding and generation, with strong transferability to various UVLMs architectures. Visual analyses further validate the effectiveness of implicit visual thoughts. These results highlight AD-Loop as a principled and broadly applicable strategy for synergizing comprehension and creation. The project page is at https://sqwu.top/AD-Loop."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 17 figures, 6 tables, ICLR conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21435","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:53:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Libing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Chunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Send Less, Perceive More: Masked Quantized Point Cloud Communication for Loss-Tolerant Collaborative Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-15T11:42:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:16:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Collaborative perception allows connected vehicles to overcome occlusions and limited viewpoints by sharing sensory information. However, existing approaches struggle to achieve high accuracy under strict bandwidth constraints and remain highly vulnerable to random transmission packet loss. We introduce QPoint2Comm, a quantized point-cloud communication framework that dramatically reduces bandwidth while preserving high-fidelity 3D information. Instead of transmitting intermediate features, QPoint2Comm directly communicates quantized point-cloud indices using a shared codebook, enabling efficient reconstruction with lower bandwidth than feature-based methods. To ensure robustness to possible communication packet loss, we employ a masked training strategy that simulates random packet loss, allowing the model to maintain strong performance even under severe transmission failures. In addition, a cascade attention fusion module is proposed to enhance multi-vehicle information integration. Extensive experiments on both simulated and real-world datasets demonstrate that QPoint2Comm sets a new state of the art in accuracy, communication efficiency, and resilience to packet loss."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T05:05:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21262","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21262","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21262","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasha","familyName":"Robinson","name":"Robinson, Sasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katherine M.","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Katherine M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Sucholutsky","name":"Sucholutsky, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelsey R.","familyName":"Allen","name":"Allen, Kelsey R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Under the Influence: Quantifying Persuasion and Vigilance in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T04:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T06:37:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:28:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T15:24:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T02:27:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With increasing integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into areas of high-stakes human decision-making, it is important to understand the risks they introduce as advisors. To be useful advisors, LLMs must sift through large amounts of content, written with both benevolent and malicious intent, and then use this information to convince a user to take a specific action. This involves two social capacities: vigilance (the ability to determine which information to use, and which to discard) and persuasion (synthesizing the available evidence to make a convincing argument). While existing work has investigated these capacities in isolation, there has been little prior investigation of how these capacities may be linked. Here, we use a simple multi-turn puzzle-solving game, Sokoban, to study LLMs' abilities to persuade and be rationally vigilant towards other LLM agents. We find that puzzle-solving performance, persuasive capability, and vigilance are dissociable capacities in LLMs. Performing well on the game does not automatically mean a model can detect when it is being misled, even if the possibility of deception is explicitly mentioned. However, LLMs do consistently modulate their token use, using fewer tokens to reason when advice is benevolent and more when it is malicious, even if they are still persuaded to take actions leading them to failure. To our knowledge, our work presents the first investigation of the relationship between persuasion, vigilance, and task performance in LLMs, and suggests that monitoring all three independently will be critical for future work in AI safety."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21262","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T05:05:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Pauls","name":"Pauls, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karsten","familyName":"Schrödter","name":"Schrödter, Karsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Ligensa","name":"Ligensa, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berkant","familyName":"Turan","name":"Turan, Berkant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Zimmer","name":"Zimmer, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sassan","familyName":"Saatchi","name":"Saatchi, Sassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Pokutta","name":"Pokutta, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Ciais","name":"Ciais, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Gieseke","name":"Gieseke, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ECHOSAT: Estimating Canopy Height Over Space And Time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-12T10:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-13T00:39:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forest monitoring is critical for climate change mitigation. However, existing global tree height maps provide only static snapshots and do not capture temporal forest dynamics, which are essential for accurate carbon accounting. We introduce ECHOSAT, a global and temporally consistent tree height map at 10 m resolution spanning multiple years. To this end, we resort to multi-sensor satellite data to train a specialized vision transformer model, which performs pixel-level temporal regression. A self-supervised growth loss regularizes the predictions to follow growth curves that are in line with natural tree development, including gradual height increases over time, but also abrupt declines due to forest loss events such as fires. Our experimental evaluation shows that our model improves state-of-the-art accuracies in the context of single-year predictions. We also provide the first global-scale height map that accurately quantifies tree growth and disturbances over time. We expect ECHOSAT to advance global efforts in carbon monitoring and disturbance assessment. The maps can be accessed at https://github.com/ai4forest/echosat."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 12 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-13T02:24:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21900","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21900","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21900","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jingbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huakang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huakang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haohe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haohe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binshen","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Binshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmoOmni: Bridging Emotional Understanding and Expression in Omni-Modal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:30:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T15:47:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:48:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evolution of Omni-Modal Large Language Models~(Omni-LLMs) has revolutionized human--computer interaction, enabling unified audio-visual perception and speech response. However, existing Omni-LLMs struggle with complex real-world scenarios, often leading to superficial understanding and contextually mismatched emotional responses. This issue is further intensified by Omni-LLM's Thinker-Talker architectures, which are implicitly connected through hidden states, leading to the loss of emotional details. In this work, we present EmoOmni, a unified framework for accurate understanding and expression in multimodal emotional dialogue. At its core, we introduce the emotional Chain-of-Thought~(E-CoT), which enforces a reasoning from fine-grained multimodal perception to textual response. Moreover, we explicitly treat E-CoT as high-level emotional instructions that guide the talker, enabling accurate emotional expression. Complementing the model, we construct EmoOmniPipe to obtain the real-world annotated dialogue data and establish a benchmark, EmoOmniEval, to facilitate systematic assessment of multimodal emotional dialogue task. Experiments show that EmoOmni-7B achieves comparable performance with Qwen3Omni-30B-A3B-Thinking under the same talker."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21900","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21772","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21772","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21772","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peize","familyName":"He","name":"He, Peize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniWhisper: Efficient Continual Multi-task Training for Robust Universal Audio Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:47:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:43:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T16:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:23:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A universal audio representation should capture fine-grained speech cues and high-level semantics for environmental sounds and music in a single encoder. Existing encoders often excel in one domain but degrade in others. We propose UniWhisper, an efficient continual multi-task training framework that casts heterogeneous audio tasks into a unified instruction and answer format. This enables standard next-token training without task-specific heads and losses. We train it on 38k hours of public audio and assess the encoder using shallow MLP probes and k-nearest neighbors (kNN) on 20 tasks spanning speech, environmental sound, and music. UniWhisper reaches normalized weighted averages of 0.81 with MLP probes and 0.61 with kNN, compared to 0.64 and 0.46 for Whisper, while retaining strong speech performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21772","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21599","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21599","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21599","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weisheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Tan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangfan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuetong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Iterative Closed-Loop Motion Synthesis for Scaling the Capabilities of Humanoid Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:52:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T13:51:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:12:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physics-based humanoid control relies on training with motion datasets that have diverse data distributions. However, the fixed difficulty distribution of datasets limits the performance ceiling of the trained control policies. Additionally, the method of acquiring high-quality data through professional motion capture systems is constrained by costs, making it difficult to achieve large-scale scalability. To address these issues, we propose a closed-loop automated motion data generation and iterative framework. It can generate high-quality motion data with rich action semantics, including martial arts, dance, combat, sports, gymnastics, and more. Furthermore, our framework enables difficulty iteration of policies and data through physical metrics and objective evaluations, allowing the trained tracker to break through its original difficulty limits. On the PHC single-primitive tracker, using only approximately 1/10 of the AMASS dataset size, the average failure rate on the test set (2201 clips) is reduced by 45% compared to the baseline. Finally, we conduct comprehensive ablation and comparative experiments to highlight the rationality and advantages of our framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21599","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yongchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangquan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guangquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"See It, Say It, Sorted: An Iterative Training-Free Framework for Visually-Grounded Multimodal Reasoning in LVLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:13:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:18:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T07:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:34:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent large vision-language models (LVLMs) have demonstrated impressive reasoning ability by generating long chain-of-thought (CoT) responses. However, CoT reasoning in multimodal contexts is highly vulnerable to visual hallucination propagation: once an intermediate reasoning step becomes inconsistent with the visual evidence, subsequent steps-even if logically valid-can still lead to incorrect final answers. Existing solutions attempt to mitigate this issue by training models to \"think with images\" via reinforcement learning (RL). While effective, these methods are costly, model-specific, and difficult to generalize across architectures. Differently, we present a lightweight method that bypasses RL training and provides an iterative, training-free, plug-and-play framework for visually-grounded multimodal reasoning. Our key idea is to supervise each reasoning step at test time with visual evidence, ensuring that every decoded token is justified by corresponding visual cues. Concretely, we construct a textual visual-evidence pool that guides the model's reasoning generation. When existing evidence is insufficient, a visual decider module dynamically extracts additional relevant evidence from the image based on the ongoing reasoning context, expanding the pool until the model achieves sufficient visual certainty to terminate reasoning and produce the final answer. Extensive experiments on multiple LVLM backbones and benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. Our method achieves 16.5%-29.5% improvements on TreeBench and 13.7% RH-AUC gains on RH-Bench, substantially reducing hallucination rates while improving reasoning accuracy without additional training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Tamayo","name":"Tamayo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iñaki","familyName":"Lacunza","name":"Lacunza, Iñaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paula","familyName":"Rivera-Hidalgo","name":"Rivera-Hidalgo, Paula","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Severino","familyName":"Da Dalt","name":"Da Dalt, Severino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Aula-Blasco","name":"Aula-Blasco, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aitor","familyName":"Gonzalez-Agirre","name":"Gonzalez-Agirre, Aitor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta","familyName":"Villegas","name":"Villegas, Marta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MrBERT: Modern Multilingual Encoders via Vocabulary, Domain, and Dimensional Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:19:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T08:52:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:36:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MrBERT, a family of 150M-300M parameter encoders built on the ModernBERT architecture and pre-trained on 35 languages and code. Through targeted adaptation, this model family achieves state-of-the-art results on Catalan- and Spanish-specific tasks, while establishing robust performance across specialized biomedical and legal domains. To bridge the gap between research and production, we incorporate Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL), enabling flexible vector sizing that significantly reduces inference and storage costs. Ultimately, the MrBERT family demonstrates that modern encoder architectures can be optimized for both localized linguistic excellence and efficient, high-stakes domain specialization. We open source the complete model family on Huggingface."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 14 tables and 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21273","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21273","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21273","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"GuoHua","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, GuoHua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StoryTailor:A Zero-Shot Pipeline for Action-Rich Multi-Subject Visual Narratives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:07:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T08:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:32:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating multi-frame, action-rich visual narratives without fine-tuning faces a threefold tension: action text faithfulness, subject identity fidelity, and cross-frame background continuity. We propose StoryTailor, a zero-shot pipeline that runs on a single RTX 4090 (24 GB) and produces temporally coherent, identity-preserving image sequences from a long narrative prompt, per-subject references, and grounding boxes. Three synergistic modules drive the system: Gaussian-Centered Attention (GCA) to dynamically focus on each subject core and ease grounding-box overlaps; Action-Boost Singular Value Reweighting (AB-SVR) to amplify action-related directions in the text embedding space; and Selective Forgetting Cache (SFC) that retains transferable background cues, forgets nonessential history, and selectively surfaces retained cues to build cross-scene semantic ties. Compared with baseline methods, experiments show that CLIP-T improves by up to 10-15%, with DreamSim lower than strong baselines, while CLIP-I stays in a visually acceptable, competitive range. With matched resolution and steps on a 24 GB GPU, inference is faster than FluxKontext. Qualitatively, StoryTailor delivers expressive interactions and evolving yet stable scenes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages,19 figures,accepted by CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21273","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:13:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengsi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Chengsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyong","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaoyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure Semantic Communications via AI Defenses: Fundamentals, Solutions, and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T08:33:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication (SemCom) redefines wireless communication from reproducing symbols to transmitting task-relevant semantics. However, this AI-native architecture also introduces new vulnerabilities, as semantic failures may arise from adversarial perturbations to models, corrupted training data, desynchronized priors, or misaligned inference even when lower-layer transmission reliability and cryptographic protection remain intact. This survey provides a defense-centered and system-oriented synthesis of security in SemCom via AI defense. We analyze AI-centric threat models by consolidating existing studies and organizing attack surfaces across model-level, channel-realizable, knowledge-based, and networked inference vectors. Building on this foundation, we present a structured taxonomy of defense strategies organized by where semantic integrity can be compromised in SemCom systems despite correct symbol delivery, spanning semantic encoding, wireless transmission, knowledge integrity, and coordination among multiple agents. These categories correspond to distinct security failure modes, including representation fragility, channel-realizable manipulation, semantic prior poisoning or desynchronization, and adversarial propagation through distributed inference. We also examine security utility operating envelopes that capture tradeoffs among semantic fidelity, robustness, latency, and energy under realistic constraints, survey evaluation frameworks and representative applications, and identify open challenges in cross-layer composition and deployment-time certification. Overall, this survey offers a unified system-level perspective that enables readers to understand major threat and defense mechanisms in AI-native SemCom systems and to leverage emerging security techniques in the design and deployment of robust SemCom architectures for next-generation intelligent networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunyou","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qunyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengbo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Pengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marina","familyName":"Zapater","name":"Zapater, Marina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Atienza","name":"Atienza, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SigmaQuant: Hardware-Aware Heterogeneous Quantization Method for Edge DNN Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T14:17:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:55:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) are essential for performing advanced tasks on edge or mobile devices, yet their deployment is often hindered by severe resource constraints, including limited memory, energy, and computational power. While uniform quantization provides a straightforward approach to compress model and reduce hardware requirement, it fails to fully leverage the varying robustness across layers, and often lead to accuracy degradation or suboptimal resource usage, particularly at low bitwidths. In contrast, heterogeneous quantization, which allocates different bitwidths to individual layers, can mitigate these drawbacks. Nonetheless, current heterogeneous quantization methods either needs huge brute-force design space search or lacks the adaptability to meet different hardware conditions, such as memory size, energy budget, and latency requirement. Filling these gaps, this work introduces \\textbf{\\textit{SigmaQuant}}, an adaptive layer-wise heterogeneous quantization framework designed to efficiently balance accuracy and resource usage for varied edge environments without exhaustive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21831","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21831","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21831","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimberly T.","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Kimberly T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Gausen","name":"Gausen, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magda","familyName":"Dubois","name":"Dubois, Magda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mona","familyName":"Murad","name":"Murad, Mona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bessie","familyName":"O'Dell","name":"O'Dell, Bessie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadine","familyName":"Staes-Polet","name":"Staes-Polet, Nadine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Summerfield","name":"Summerfield, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Strait","name":"Strait, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multi-Turn Framework for Evaluating AI Misuse in Fraud and Cybercrime Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:01:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:47:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T17:14:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T02:03:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI is increasingly being used to assist fraud and cybercrime. However, it is unclear the extent to which current large language models can provide useful information for complex criminal activity. Working with law enforcement and policy experts, we developed multi-turn evaluations for three fraud and cybercrime scenarios (romance scams, CEO impersonation, and identity theft). Our evaluations focus on text-to-text interactions. In each scenario, we evaluate whether models provide actionable assistance beyond information typically available on the web, as assessed by domain experts. We do so in ways designed to resemble real-world misuse, such as breaking down requests for fraud into a sequence of seemingly benign queries.\n We found that (1) current large language models provide minimal actionable information for fraud and cybercrime without the use of advanced jailbreaking techniques, (2) model safeguards have significant impact on the provision of information, with the two open-weight large language models fine-tuned to remove safety guardrails providing the most actionable and useful responses, and (3) decomposing requests into benign-seeming queries elicited more assistance than explicitly malicious framing or basic system-level jailbreaks. Overall, the results suggest that current text-generation models provide relatively minimal uplift for fraud and cybercrime through information provision, without extensive effort to circumvent safeguards. This work contributes a reproducible, expert-grounded framework for tracking how these risks may evolve with time as models grow more capable and adversaries adapt."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21831","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21646","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21646","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21646","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yexing","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yexing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youcheng","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Youcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yichong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Multilingual Multimodal Machine Translation with Speech-Text Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:19:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:53:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:17:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have achieved notable success in enhancing translation performance by integrating multimodal information. However, existing research primarily focuses on image-guided methods, whose applicability is constrained by the scarcity of multilingual image-text pairs. The speech modality overcomes this limitation due to its natural alignment with text and the abundance of existing speech datasets, which enable scalable language coverage. In this paper, we propose a Speech-guided Machine Translation (SMT) framework that integrates speech and text as fused inputs into an MLLM to improve translation quality. To mitigate reliance on low-resource data, we introduce a Self-Evolution Mechanism. The core components of this framework include a text-to-speech model, responsible for generating synthetic speech, and an MLLM capable of classifying synthetic speech samples and iteratively optimizing itself using positive samples. Experimental results demonstrate that our framework surpasses all existing methods on the Multi30K multimodal machine translation benchmark, achieving new state-of-the-art results. Furthermore, on general machine translation datasets, particularly the FLORES-200, it achieves average state-of-the-art performance in 108 translation directions. Ablation studies on CoVoST-2 confirms that differences between synthetic and authentic speech have negligible impact on translation quality. The code and models are released at https://github.com/yxduir/LLM-SRT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21646","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longze","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Longze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ancheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Alinejad-Rokny","name":"Alinejad-Rokny, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RuCL: Stratified Rubric-Based Curriculum Learning for Multimodal Large Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:46:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:05:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a prevailing paradigm for enhancing reasoning in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). However, relying solely on outcome supervision risks reward hacking, where models learn spurious reasoning patterns to satisfy final answer checks. While recent rubric-based approaches offer fine-grained supervision signals, they suffer from high computational costs of instance-level generation and inefficient training dynamics caused by treating all rubrics as equally learnable. In this paper, we propose Stratified Rubric-based Curriculum Learning (RuCL), a novel framework that reformulates curriculum learning by shifting the focus from data selection to reward design. RuCL generates generalized rubrics for broad applicability and stratifies them based on the model's competence. By dynamically adjusting rubric weights during training, RuCL guides the model from mastering foundational perception to tackling advanced logical reasoning. Extensive experiments on various visual reasoning benchmarks show that RuCL yields a remarkable +7.83% average improvement over the Qwen2.5-VL-7B model, achieving a state-of-the-art accuracy of 60.06%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gholamreza","familyName":"Haffari","name":"Haffari, Gholamreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minxian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Minxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajkumar","familyName":"Buyya","name":"Buyya, Rajkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel N.","familyName":"Toosi","name":"Toosi, Adel N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Layer Scheduling for MoE-Based LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:42:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of tasks, but serving them efficiently at scale remains a critical challenge due to their substantial computational and latency demands. While most existing inference frameworks rely on simple scheduling strategies such as First-Come-First-Serve (FCFS) at the engine level and Round-Robin (RR) at the scheduler or coordinator level, they often fail to fully utilize system resources and may suffer from issues such as head-of-line blocking and load imbalance. Recent advances in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models have also introduced new challenges in scheduling arising from expert parallelism and routing complexity. This research proposes a multi-layer scheduling framework tailored for MoE-based LLM serving. It targets scheduling at three levels: request-level, enginelevel, and expert-level. At the request level, we explore algorithms such as Shortest-Job-First (SJF) and priority-aware aging to improve throughput and reduce latency. At the engine level, we design load-aware dispatching strategies that account for the current prefix token load, KV cache utilization, and user stickiness to achieve better resource matching. At the expert level, we focus on alleviating expert hotspots and strategically placing inter-layer expert dependencies to balance load and improve routing efficiency. Extensive experimental results from more than 100 experiments conducted under diverse workload distributions show that our approach consistently outperforms the state-of-theart inference framework vLLM, achieving up to 17.8% reduction in Time To First Token (TTFT) latency and 13.3% reduction in Time-Per-Output-Token (TPOT) latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"van der Laan","name":"van der Laan, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Researcher's Guide to Empirical Risk Minimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:26:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:18:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T16:28:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T02:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This guide provides a reference for high-probability regret bounds in empirical risk minimization (ERM). The presentation is modular: we begin with intuition and general proof strategies, then state broadly applicable guarantees under high-level conditions and provide tools for verifying them for specific losses and function classes. We emphasize that many ERM rate derivations can be organized around a three-step recipe -- a basic inequality, a uniform local concentration bound, and a fixed-point argument -- which yields regret bounds in terms of a critical radius, defined via localized Rademacher complexity, under a mild Bernstein-type variance-risk condition. To make these bounds concrete, we upper bound the critical radius using local maximal inequalities and metric-entropy integrals, thereby recovering familiar rates for VC-subgraph, Sobolev/Hölder, and bounded-variation classes.\n We also study ERM with nuisance components -- including weighted ERM and Neyman-orthogonal losses -- as they arise in causal inference, missing data, and domain adaptation. Following the orthogonal statistical learning framework, we highlight that these problems often admit regret-transfer bounds linking regret under an estimated loss to population regret under the target loss. These bounds typically decompose the regret into (i) statistical error under the estimated loss and (ii) approximation error due to nuisance estimation. Under sample splitting or cross-fitting, the first term can be controlled using standard fixed-loss ERM regret bounds, while the second depends only on nuisance-estimation accuracy. As a novel contribution, we also treat the in-sample regime, in which the nuisances and the ERM are fit on the same data, deriving regret bounds and showing that fast oracle rates remain attainable under suitable smoothness and Donsker-type conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version 2; minor edits and clarifications, expanded references, extended Section 2 (high-probability bounds)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:47Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenny Tsu Wei","familyName":"Choo","name":"Choo, Kenny Tsu Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Taxonomy of Human--MLLM Interaction in Early-Stage Sketch-Based Design Ideation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:18:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:18:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T15:39:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:17:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772363.3798524","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are increasingly integrated into early-stage design tools, it is important to understand how designers collaborate with AI during ideation. In a user study with 12 participants, we analysed sketch-based design interactions with an MLLM-powered system using automatically recorded interaction logs and post-task interviews. Based on how creative responsibility was allocated between humans and the AI, we predefined four interaction modes: Human-Only, Human-Lead, AI-Lead, and Co-Evolution, and analysed how these modes manifested during sketch-based design ideation. Our results show that designers rarely rely on a single mode; instead, human-led and AI-led roles are frequently interwoven and shift across ideation instances. These findings provide an empirical basis for future work to investigate why designers shift roles with AI and how interactive systems can better support such dynamic collaboration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at CHI 2026 Posters"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peixia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peixia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongdong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pulak","familyName":"Purkait","name":"Purkait, Pulak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Shadow Generation and Relighting via Light-Geometry Interaction Maps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:47:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:31:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Light-Geometry Interaction (LGI) maps, a novel representation that encodes light-aware occlusion from monocular depth. Unlike ray tracing, which requires full 3D reconstruction, LGI captures essential light-shadow interactions reliably and accurately, computed from off-the-shelf 2.5D depth map predictions. LGI explicitly ties illumination direction to geometry, providing a physics-inspired prior that constrains generative models. Without such prior, these models often produce floating shadows, inconsistent illumination, and implausible shadow geometry. Building on this representation, we propose a unified pipeline for joint shadow generation and relighting - unlike prior methods that treat them as disjoint tasks - capturing the intrinsic coupling of illumination and shadowing essential for modeling indirect effects. By embedding LGI into a bridge-matching generative backbone, we reduce ambiguity and enforce physically consistent light-shadow reasoning. To enable effective training, we curated the first large-scale benchmark dataset for joint shadow and relighting, covering reflections, transparency, and complex interreflections. Experiments show significant gains in realism and consistency across synthetic and real images. LGI thus bridges geometry-inspired rendering with generative modeling, enabling efficient, physically consistent shadow generation and relighting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tangsang","familyName":"Chongbang","name":"Chongbang, Tangsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranesh Pyara","familyName":"Shrestha","name":"Shrestha, Pranesh Pyara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amrit","familyName":"Sarki","name":"Sarki, Amrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anku","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Anku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Structural Noise in Low-Resource S2TT: An Optimized Cascaded Nepali-English Pipeline with Punctuation Restoration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T12:30:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cascaded speech-to-text translation (S2TT) systems for low-resource languages can suffer from structural noise, particularly the loss of punctuation during the Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) phase. This research investigates the impact of such noise on Nepali-to-English translation and proposes an optimized pipeline to mitigate quality degradation. We first establish highly proficient ASR and NMT components: a Wav2Vec2-XLS-R-300m model achieved a state-of-the-art 2.72% CER on OpenSLR-54, and a multi-stage fine-tuned MarianMT model reached a 28.32 BLEU score on the FLORES-200 benchmark. We empirically investigate the influence of punctuation loss, demonstrating that unpunctuated ASR output significantly degrades translation quality, causing a massive 20.7% relative BLEU drop on the FLORES benchmark. To overcome this, we propose and evaluate an intermediate Punctuation Restoration Module (PRM). The final S2TT pipeline was tested across three configurations on a custom dataset. The optimal configuration, which applied the PRM directly to ASR output, achieved a 4.90 BLEU point gain over the direct ASR-to-NMT baseline (BLEU 36.38 vs. 31.48). This improvement was validated by human assessment, which confirmed the optimized pipeline's superior Adequacy (3.673) and Fluency (3.804) with inter-rater reliability (Krippendorff's $α {\\geq}$ 0.723). This work validates that targeted punctuation restoration is the most effective intervention for mitigating structural noise in the Nepali S2TT pipeline. It establishes an optimized baseline and demonstrates a critical architectural insight for developing cascaded speech translation systems for similar low-resource languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures, 12 tables, Transactions on Asian and Low-Resource Language Information Processing (Under Review)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21333","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21333","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21333","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Pittaluga","name":"Pittaluga, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaid","familyName":"Tasneem","name":"Tasneem, Zaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manmohan","familyName":"Chandraker","name":"Chandraker, Manmohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HorizonForge: Driving Scene Editing with Any Trajectories and Any Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T00:13:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:55:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Controllable driving scene generation is critical for realistic and scalable autonomous driving simulation, yet existing approaches struggle to jointly achieve photorealism and precise control. We introduce HorizonForge, a unified framework that reconstructs scenes as editable Gaussian Splats and Meshes, enabling fine-grained 3D manipulation and language-driven vehicle insertion. Edits are rendered through a noise-aware video diffusion process that enforces spatial and temporal consistency, producing diverse scene variations in a single feed-forward pass without per-trajectory optimization. To standardize evaluation, we further propose HorizonSuite, a comprehensive benchmark spanning ego- and agent-level editing tasks such as trajectory modifications and object manipulation. Extensive experiments show that Gaussian-Mesh representation delivers substantially higher fidelity than alternative 3D representations, and that temporal priors from video diffusion are essential for coherent synthesis. Combining these findings, HorizonForge establishes a simple yet powerful paradigm for photorealistic, controllable driving simulation, achieving an 83.4% user-preference gain and a 25.19% FID improvement over the second best state-of-the-art method. Project page: https://horizonforge.github.io/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21333","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengrui","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Chengrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Lanzetti","name":"Lanzetti, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Mazumdar","name":"Mazumdar, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Generalizable Collaborative Agents via Strategic Risk Aversion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T04:56:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:23:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many emerging agentic paradigms require agents to collaborate with one another (or people) to achieve shared goals. Unfortunately, existing approaches to learning policies for such collaborative problems produce brittle solutions that fail when paired with new partners. We attribute these failures to a combination of free-riding during training and a lack of strategic robustness. To address these problems, we study the concept of strategic risk aversion and interpret it as a principled inductive bias for generalizable cooperation with unseen partners. While strategically risk-averse players are robust to deviations in their partner's behavior by design, we show that, in collaborative games, they also (1) can have better equilibrium outcomes than those at classical game-theoretic concepts like Nash, and (2) exhibit less or no free-riding. Inspired by these insights, we develop a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) algorithm that integrates strategic risk aversion into standard policy optimization methods. Our empirical results across collaborative benchmarks (including an LLM collaboration task) validate our theory and demonstrate that our approach consistently achieves reliable collaboration with heterogeneous and previously unseen partners across collaborative tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21402","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21402","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21402","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyoung","familyName":"Jun","name":"Jun, Jinyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Won-Dong","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Won-Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghudeep","familyName":"Gadde","name":"Gadde, Raghudeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungbeom","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jungbeom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlowFixer: Towards Detail-Preserving Subject-Driven Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:16:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T23:26:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:10:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present FlowFixer, a refinement framework for subject-driven generation (SDG) that restores fine details lost during generation caused by changes in scale and perspective of a subject. FlowFixer proposes direct image-to-image translation from visual references, avoiding ambiguities in language prompts. To enable image-to-image training, we introduce a one-step denoising scheme to generate self-supervised training data, which automatically removes high-frequency details while preserving global structure, effectively simulating real-world SDG errors. We further propose a keypoint matching-based metric to properly assess fidelity in details beyond semantic similarities usually measured by CLIP or DINO. Experimental results demonstrate that FlowFixer outperforms state-of-the-art SDG methods in both qualitative and quantitative evaluations, setting a new benchmark for high-fidelity subject-driven generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21402","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alina","familyName":"Devkota","name":"Devkota, Alina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Thrasher","name":"Thrasher, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Adjeroh","name":"Adjeroh, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binod","familyName":"Bhattarai","name":"Bhattarai, Binod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashnna K.","familyName":"Gyawali","name":"Gyawali, Prashnna K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedVG: Gradient-Guided Aggregation for Enhanced Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:05:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T19:08:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:02:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training across multiple clients without sharing their private data. However, data heterogeneity across clients leads to client drift, which degrades the overall generalization performance of the model. This effect is further compounded by overemphasis on poorly performing clients. To address this problem, we propose FedVG, a novel gradient-based federated aggregation framework that leverages a global validation set to guide the optimization process. Such a global validation set can be established using readily available public datasets, ensuring accessibility and consistency across clients without compromising privacy. In contrast to conventional approaches that prioritize client dataset volume, FedVG assesses the generalization ability of client models by measuring the magnitude of validation gradients across layers. Specifically, we compute layerwise gradient norms to derive a client-specific score that reflects how much each client needs to adjust for improved generalization on the global validation set, thereby enabling more informed and adaptive federated aggregation. Extensive experiments on both natural and medical image benchmarking datasets, across diverse model architectures, demonstrate that FedVG consistently improves performance, particularly in highly heterogeneous settings. Moreover, FedVG is modular and can be seamlessly integrated with various state-of-the-art FL algorithms, often further improving their results. Our code is available at https://github.com/alinadevkota/FedVG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026 (Findings Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihoon","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Jihoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Kagan","name":"Kagan, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raffi","familyName":"Khatchadourian","name":"Khatchadourian, Raffi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonbin","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Wonbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Lopez-Lira","name":"Lopez-Lira, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoontae","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Yoontae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Levy","name":"Levy, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yongjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanyeol","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Chanyeol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Forecasting Future Language: Context Design for Mention Markets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Finance (q-fin.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-04T12:43:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T06:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:27:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mention markets, a type of prediction market in which contracts resolve based on whether a specified keyword is mentioned during a future public event, require accurate probabilistic forecasts of keyword-mention outcomes. While recent work shows that large language models (LLMs) can generate forecasts competitive with human forecasters, it remains unclear how input context should be designed to support accurate prediction. In this paper, we study this question through experiments on earnings-call mention markets, which require forecasting whether a company will mention a specified keyword during its upcoming call. We run controlled comparisons varying (i) which contextual information is provided (news and/or prior earnings-call transcripts) and (ii) how \\textit{market probability}, (i.e., prediction market contract price) is used. We introduce Market-Conditioned Prompting (MCP), which explicitly treats the market-implied probability as a prior and instructs the LLM to update this prior using textual evidence, rather than re-predicting the base rate from scratch. In our experiments, we find three insights: (1) richer context consistently improves forecasting performance; (2) market-conditioned prompting (MCP), which treats the market probability as a prior and updates it using textual evidence, yields better-calibrated forecasts; and (3) a mixture of the market probability and MCP (MixMCP) outperforms the market baseline. By dampening the LLM's posterior update with the market prior, MixMCP yields more robust predictions than either the market or the LLM alone."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgy","familyName":"Savva","name":"Savva, Georgy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Michel","name":"Michel, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daohan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Daohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suppakit","familyName":"Waiwitlikhit","name":"Waiwitlikhit, Suppakit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Meehan","name":"Meehan, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhairya","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Dhairya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivats","familyName":"Poddar","name":"Poddar, Srivats","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saining","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Saining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solaris: Building a Multiplayer Video World Model in Minecraft"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:59:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:10:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing action-conditioned video generation models (video world models) are limited to single-agent perspectives, failing to capture the multi-agent interactions of real-world environments. We introduce Solaris, a multiplayer video world model that simulates consistent multi-view observations. To enable this, we develop a multiplayer data system designed for robust, continuous, and automated data collection on video games such as Minecraft. Unlike prior platforms built for single-player settings, our system supports coordinated multi-agent interaction and synchronized videos + actions capture. Using this system, we collect 12.64 million multiplayer frames and propose an evaluation framework for multiplayer movement, memory, grounding, building, and view consistency. We train Solaris using a staged pipeline that progressively transitions from single-player to multiplayer modeling, combining bidirectional, causal, and Self Forcing training. In the final stage, we introduce Checkpointed Self Forcing, a memory-efficient Self Forcing variant that enables a longer-horizon teacher. Results show our architecture and training design outperform existing baselines. Through open-sourcing our system and models, we hope to lay the groundwork for a new generation of multi-agent world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project website: https://solaris-wm.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew A. H.","familyName":"Walker","name":"Walker, Matthew A. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junayd Ul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Junayd Ul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keith T.","familyName":"Butler","name":"Butler, Keith T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discovering new photovoltaics using optimal transport theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:44:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T16:43:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Searching by chemical and structural analogy is one of the most commonly used and successful approaches to materials discovery. However, formulating this task for algorithmic implementation raises the question of how we define similar materials. Methods have been proposed for searching materials space using vectors based on chemical composition and functional fragments in the material. Descriptors for structural similarity have also been proposed. However, the question of how to incorporate and balance structural and compositional similarity measures in a single metric remains open. Here, we adapt methods developed for calculating distances between undirected graphs and apply them to crystalline materials similarity. The Fused Gromov-Wasserstein (FGW) metric uses optimal transport theory to map between two graphs considering a balance of the graph structure and the information present at the nodes of the graph (atoms in crystals). We apply the method to exploring new photovoltaic materials. We demonstrate that FGW is competitive with embeddings from an equivariant graph neural network, trained on $\u0026gt; 10^6$ materials, despite minimal training. We then apply FGW to a discovery campaign to identify materials from the Materials Project database that have not previously been explored as photovoltaics, but have similarities to known high-efficiency materials. After validating predictions with hybrid density functional theory, we identify seven previously unexplored high-efficiency photovoltaic absorber candidates, including Cs$_5$Sb$_8$, which is found to have a predicted SLME of $\u0026gt; 30\\%$ and to be thermodynamically stable. The FGW approach demonstrates the power of strong inductive biases for developing metrics for materials exploration with minimal training data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinda","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jinda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Multi-Modal LLMs Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Group Normalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:52:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T06:20:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) have significantly advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models. Extending these methods to multimodal settings, however, faces a critical challenge: the instability of std-based normalization, which is easily distorted by extreme samples with nearly positive or negative rewards. Unlike pure-text LLMs, multimodal models are particularly sensitive to such distortions, as both perceptual and reasoning errors influence their responses. To address this, we characterize each sample by its difficulty, defined through perceptual complexity (measured via visual entropy) and reasoning uncertainty (captured by model confidence). Building on this characterization, we propose difficulty-aware group normalization (Durian), which re-groups samples by difficulty levels and shares the std within each group. Our approach preserves GRPO's intra-group distinctions while eliminating sensitivity to extreme cases, yielding significant performance gains across multiple multimodal reasoning benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:05Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoya","familyName":"Kawabe","name":"Kawabe, Tomoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rin","familyName":"Takano","name":"Takano, Rin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical LLM-Based Multi-Agent Framework with Prompt Optimization for Multi-Robot Task Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:08:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:28:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-robot task planning requires decomposing natural-language instructions into executable actions for heterogeneous robot teams. Conventional Planning Domain Definition Language (PDDL) planners provide rigorous guarantees but struggle to handle ambiguous or long-horizon missions, while large language models (LLMs) can interpret instructions and propose plans but may hallucinate or produce infeasible actions. We present a hierarchical multi-agent LLM-based planner with prompt optimization: an upper layer decomposes tasks and assigns them to lower-layer agents, which generate PDDL problems solved by a classical planner. When plans fail, the system applies TextGrad-inspired textual-gradient updates to optimize each agent's prompt and thereby improve planning accuracy. In addition, meta-prompts are learned and shared across agents within the same layer, enabling efficient prompt optimization in multi-agent settings. On the MAT-THOR benchmark, our planner achieves success rates of 0.95 on compound tasks, 0.84 on complex tasks, and 0.60 on vague tasks, improving over the previous state-of-the-art LaMMA-P by 2, 7, and 15 percentage points respectively. An ablation study shows that the hierarchical structure, prompt optimization, and meta-prompt sharing contribute roughly +59, +37, and +4 percentage points to the overall success rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA) 2026. 8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sweta","familyName":"Karlekar","name":"Karlekar, Sweta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carolina","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Carolina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magnus","familyName":"Saebo","name":"Saebo, Magnus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Beltran-Velez","name":"Beltran-Velez, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Bowlan","name":"Bowlan, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Kucer","name":"Kucer, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Blei","name":"Blei, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Duel-Evolve: Reward-Free Test-Time Scaling via LLM Self-Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:16:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:47:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many applications seek to optimize LLM outputs at test time by iteratively proposing, scoring, and refining candidates over a discrete output space. Existing methods use a calibrated scalar evaluator for the target objective to guide search, but for many tasks such scores are unavailable, too sparse, or unreliable. Pairwise comparisons, by contrast, are often easier to elicit, still provide useful signal on improvement directions, and can be obtained from the LLM itself without external supervision. Building on this observation, we introduce Duel-Evolve, an evolutionary optimization algorithm that replaces external scalar rewards with pairwise preferences elicited from the same LLM used to generate candidates. Duel-Evolve aggregates these noisy candidate comparisons via a Bayesian Bradley-Terry model, yielding uncertainty-aware estimates of candidate quality. These quality estimates guide allocation of the comparison budget toward plausible optima using Double Thompson Sampling, as well as selection of high-quality parents to generate improved candidates. We evaluate Duel-Evolve on MathBench, where it achieves 20 percentage points higher accuracy over existing methods and baselines, and on LiveCodeBench, where it improves over comparable iterative methods by over 12 percentage points. Notably, the method requires no reward model, no ground-truth labels during search, and no hand-crafted scoring function. Results show that pairwise self-preferences provide strong optimization signal for test-time improvement over large, discrete output spaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongtong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinmin","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Yinmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rilin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Rilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shangyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunfeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DualPath: Breaking the Storage Bandwidth Bottleneck in Agentic LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:04:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The performance of multi-turn, agentic LLM inference is increasingly dominated by KV-Cache storage I/O rather than computation. In prevalent disaggregated architectures, loading the massive KV-Cache from external storage creates a fundamental imbalance: storage NICs on prefill engines become bandwidth-saturated, while those on decoding engines remain idle. This asymmetry severely constrains overall system throughput.\n We present DualPath, an inference system that breaks this bottleneck by introducing dual-path KV-Cache loading. Beyond the traditional storage-to-prefill path, DualPath enables a novel storage-to-decode path, in which the KV-Cache is loaded into decoding engines and then efficiently transferred to prefill engines via RDMA over the compute network. DualPath combines this optimized data path -- which inherently avoids network congestion and avoids interference with latency-critical model execution communications -- with a global scheduler that dynamically balances load across prefill and decode engines.\n Our evaluation on three models with production agentic workloads demonstrates that DualPath improves offline inference throughput by up to 1.87$\\times$ on our in-house inference system. It can also improve online serving throughput by an average factor of 1.96$\\times$ without violating SLO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna","familyName":"Yukhymenko","name":"Yukhymenko, Hanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Alexandrov","name":"Alexandrov, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Vechev","name":"Vechev, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recovered in Translation: Efficient Pipeline for Automated Translation of Benchmarks and Datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:58:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reliability of multilingual Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation is currently compromised by the inconsistent quality of translated benchmarks. Existing resources often suffer from semantic drift and context loss, which can lead to misleading performance metrics. In this work, we present a fully automated framework designed to address these challenges by enabling scalable, high-quality translation of datasets and benchmarks. We demonstrate that adapting test-time compute scaling strategies, specifically Universal Self-Improvement (USI) and our proposed multi-round ranking method, T-RANK, allows for significantly higher quality outputs compared to traditional pipelines. Our framework ensures that benchmarks preserve their original task structure and linguistic nuances during localization. We apply this approach to translate popular benchmarks and datasets into eight Eastern and Southern European languages (Ukrainian, Bulgarian, Slovak, Romanian, Lithuanian, Estonian, Turkish, Greek). Evaluations using both reference-based metrics and LLM-as-a-judge show that our translations surpass existing resources, resulting in more accurate downstream model assessment. We release both the framework and the improved benchmarks to facilitate robust and reproducible multilingual AI development."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:16:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22200","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22200","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22200","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cole","familyName":"Simmons","name":"Simmons, Cole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard Diehl","familyName":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Richard Diehl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Jurafsky","name":"Jurafsky, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SumTablets: A Transliteration Dataset of Sumerian Tablets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:50:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:10:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2024.ml4al-1.20","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sumerian transliteration is a conventional system for representing a scholar's interpretation of a tablet in the Latin script. Thanks to visionary digital Assyriology projects such as ETCSL, CDLI, and Oracc, a large number of Sumerian transliterations have been published online, and these data are well-structured for a variety of search and analysis tasks. However, the absence of a comprehensive, accessible dataset pairing transliterations with a digital representation of the tablet's cuneiform glyphs has prevented the application of modern Natural Language Processing (NLP) methods to the task of Sumerian transliteration.\n To address this gap, we present SumTablets, a dataset pairing Unicode representations of 91,606 Sumerian cuneiform tablets (totaling 6,970,407 glyphs) with the associated transliterations published by Oracc. We construct SumTablets by first preprocessing and standardizing the Oracc transliterations before mapping each reading back to the Unicode representation of the source glyph. Further, we retain parallel structural information (e.g., surfaces, newlines, broken segments) through the use of special tokens. We release SumTablets as a Hugging Face Dataset (CC BY 4.0) and open source data preparation code via GitHub.\n Additionally, we leverage SumTablets to implement and evaluate two transliteration baselines: (1) weighted sampling from a glyph's possible readings, and (2) fine-tuning an autoregressive language model. Our fine-tuned language model achieves an average transliteration character-level F-score (chrF) of 97.55, demonstrating the immediate potential of transformer-based transliteration models in allowing experts to rapidly verify generated transliterations rather than manually transliterating tablets one-by-one."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages with 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22200","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:21Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:16:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melody","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Melody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Hewitt","name":"Hewitt, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Parametric Knowledge Access in Reasoning Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study reasoning for accessing world knowledge stored in a language model's parameters. For example, recalling that Canberra is Australia's capital may benefit from thinking through major cities and the concept of purpose-built capitals. While reasoning language models are trained via reinforcement learning to produce reasoning traces on tasks such as mathematics, they may not reason well for accessing their own world knowledge. We first find that models do not generate their best world knowledge reasoning by default: adding a simple \"think step-by-step\" cue demonstrates statistically significant improvement in knowledge recall but not math. Motivated by this, we propose training models to reason over their parametric knowledge using world-knowledge question answering as a verifiable reward. After reinforcement learning on TriviaQA (+9.9%), performance also improves on Natural Questions, HotpotQA, SimpleQA, and StrategyQA by 4.2%, 2.1%, 0.6%, and 3.0%, respectively. Reasoning models are under-optimized for parametric knowledge access, but can be easily trained to reason better."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annabel","familyName":"Goldman","name":"Goldman, Annabel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jovy","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jovy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clarissa","familyName":"Shieh","name":"Shieh, Clarissa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mia","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Mia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Kay","name":"Kay, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fumeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fumeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Codesigning Ripplet: an LLM-Assisted Assessment Authoring System Grounded in a Conceptual Model of Teachers' Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:30:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3790418","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Assessments are critical in education, but creating them can be difficult. To address this challenge in a grounded way, we partnered with 13 teachers in a seven-month codesign process. We developed a conceptual model that characterizes the iterative dual process where teachers develop assessments while simultaneously refining requirements. To enact this model in practice, we built Ripplet, a web-based tool with multilevel reusable interactions to support assessment authoring. The extended codesign revealed that Ripplet enabled teachers to create formative assessments they would not have otherwise made, shifted their practices from generation to curation, and helped them reflect more on assessment quality. In a user study with 15 additional teachers, compared to their current practices, teachers felt the results were more worth their effort and that assessment quality improved."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 2026 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:16:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dwaipayan","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Dwaipayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mandar","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Mandar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiCQA : A Lightweight Complex Question Answering System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:28:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over the last twenty years, significant progress has been made in designing and implementing Question Answering (QA) systems. However, addressing complex questions, the answers to which are spread across multiple documents, remains a challenging problem. Recent QA systems that are designed to handle complex questions work either on the basis of knowledge graphs, or utilise contem- porary neural models that are expensive to train, in terms of both computational resources and the volume of training data required. In this paper, we present LiCQA, an unsupervised question answer- ing model that works primarily on the basis of corpus evidence. We empirically compare the effectiveness and efficiency of LiCQA with two recently presented QA systems, which are based on different underlying principles. The results of our experiments show that LiCQA significantly outperforms these two state-of-the-art systems on benchmark data with noteworthy reduction in latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minqiu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luanzheng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Luanzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan R.","familyName":"Tallent","name":"Tallent, Nathan R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kento","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Kento","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMTailor: A Layer-wise Tailoring Tool for Efficient Checkpointing of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:05:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3731599.3767515","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Checkpointing is essential for fault tolerance in training large language models (LLMs). However, existing methods, regardless of their I/O strategies, periodically store the entire model and optimizer states, incurring substantial storage overhead and resource contention. Recent studies reveal that updates across LLM layers are highly non-uniform. Across training steps, some layers may undergo more significant changes, while others remain relatively stable or even unchanged. This suggests that selectively checkpointing only layers with significant updates could reduce overhead without harming training. Implementing such selective strategies requires fine-grained control over both weights and optimizer states, which no current tool provides. To address this gap, we propose \\texttt{LLMTailor}, a checkpoint-merging framework that filters and assembles layers from different checkpoints to form a composite checkpoint. Our evaluation indicates that LLMTailor can work with different selective checkpointing strategies and effectively reduce checkpoint size (e.g., 4.3 times smaller for Llama3.1-8B) and checkpoint time (e.g., 2.8 times faster for Qwen2.5-7B) while maintaining model quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures, accepted at PDSW'25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Pielage","name":"Pielage, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ole","familyName":"Hätscher","name":"Hätscher, Ole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitja","familyName":"Back","name":"Back, Mitja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Marschall","name":"Marschall, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Risse","name":"Risse, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Personality Adaptation in Large Language Models via State Machines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; J.4; K.3.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:05:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The inability of Large Language Models (LLMs) to modulate their personality expression in response to evolving dialogue dynamics hinders their performance in complex, interactive contexts. We propose a model-agnostic framework for dynamic personality simulation that employs state machines to represent latent personality states, where transition probabilities are dynamically adapted to the conversational context. Part of our architecture is a modular pipeline for continuous personality scoring that evaluates dialogues along latent axes while remaining agnostic to the specific personality models, their dimensions, transition mechanisms, or LLMs used. These scores function as dynamic state variables that systematically reconfigure the system prompt, steering behavioral alignment throughout the interaction.We evaluate this framework by operationalizing the Interpersonal Circumplex (IPC) in a medical education setting. Results demonstrate that the system successfully adapts its personality state to user inputs, but also influences user behavior, thereby facilitating de-escalation training. Notably, the scoring pipeline maintains comparable precision even when utilizing lightweight, fine-tuned classifiers instead of large-scale LLMs. This work demonstrates the feasibility of modular, personality-adaptive architectures for education, customer support, and broader human-computer interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 5 figures, submitted to ICPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peizhong","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Peizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ness","familyName":"Shroff","name":"Shroff, Ness","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Provable Last-Iterate Convergence for Multi-Objective Safe LLM Alignment via Optimistic Primal-Dual"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:54:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) plays a significant role in aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with human preferences. While RLHF with expected reward constraints can be formulated as a primal-dual optimization problem, standard primal-dual methods only guarantee convergence with a distributional policy where the saddle-point problem is in convex-concave form. Moreover, standard primal-dual methods may exhibit instability or divergence in the last iterate under policy parameterization in practical applications. In this work, we propose a universal primal-dual framework for safe RLHF that unifies a broad class of existing alignment algorithms, including safe-RLHF, one-shot, and multi-shot based methods. Building on this framework, we introduce an optimistic primal-dual (OPD) algorithm that incorporates predictive updates for both primal and dual variables to stabilize saddle-point dynamics. We establish last-iterate convergence guarantees for the proposed method, covering both exact policy optimization in the distributional space and convergence to a neighborhood of the optimal solution whose gap is related to approximation error and bias under parameterized policies. Our analysis reveals that optimism plays a crucial role in mitigating oscillations inherent to constrained alignment objectives, thereby closing a key theoretical gap between constrained RL and practical RLHF."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyam Kumar","familyName":"Navneet","name":"Navneet, Satyam Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Chandra","name":"Chandra, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When AI Writes, Whose Voice Remains? Quantifying Cultural Marker Erasure Across World English Varieties in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772363.3799085","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to ``professionalize'' workplace communication, often at the cost of linguistic identity. We introduce \"Cultural Ghosting\", the systematic erasure of linguistic markers unique to non-native English varieties during text processing. Through analysis of 22,350 LLM outputs generated from 1,490 culturally marked texts (Indian, Singaporean,\u0026amp; Nigerian English) processed by five models under three prompt conditions, we quantify this phenomenon using two novel metrics: Identity Erasure Rate (IER) \u0026amp; Semantic Preservation Score (SPS). Across all prompts, we find an overall IER of 10.26%, with model-level variation from 3.5% to 20.5% (5.9x range). Crucially, we identify a Semantic Preservation Paradox: models maintain high semantic similarity (mean SPS = 0.748) while systematically erasing cultural markers. Pragmatic markers (politeness conventions) are 1.9x more vulnerable than lexical markers (71.5% vs. 37.1% erasure). Our experiments demonstrate that explicit cultural-preservation prompts reduce erasure by 29% without sacrificing semantic quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runpeng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Runpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinchao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoLan: Mitigating Object Hallucinations in Large Vision-Language Models via Dynamic Suppression of Language Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:50:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Object hallucination is a critical issue in Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), where outputs include objects that do not appear in the input image. A natural question arises from this phenomenon: Which component of the LVLM pipeline primarily contributes to object hallucinations? The vision encoder to perceive visual information, or the language decoder to generate text responses? In this work, we strive to answer this question through designing a systematic experiment to analyze the roles of the vision encoder and the language decoder in hallucination generation. Our observations reveal that object hallucinations are predominantly associated with the strong priors from the language decoder. Based on this finding, we propose a simple and training-free framework, No-Language-Hallucination Decoding, NoLan, which refines the output distribution by dynamically suppressing language priors, modulated based on the output distribution difference between multimodal and text-only inputs. Experimental results demonstrate that NoLan effectively reduces object hallucinations across various LVLMs on different tasks. For instance, NoLan achieves substantial improvements on POPE, enhancing the accuracy of LLaVA-1.5 7B and Qwen-VL 7B by up to 6.45 and 7.21, respectively. The code is publicly available at: https://github.com/lingfengren/NoLan."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: https://github.com/lingfengren/NoLan"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetan","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Yuetan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhua","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xinhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinran","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xinran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongning","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Gongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedTri: A Platform for Structured Medical Report Normalization to Enhance Vision-Language Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical vision-language pretraining increasingly relies on medical reports as large-scale supervisory signals; however, raw reports often exhibit substantial stylistic heterogeneity, variable length, and a considerable amount of image-irrelevant content. Although text normalization is frequently adopted as a preprocessing step in prior work, its design principles and empirical impact on vision-language pretraining remain insufficiently and systematically examined. In this study, we present MedTri, a deployable normalization framework for medical vision-language pretraining that converts free-text reports into a unified [Anatomical Entity: Radiologic Description + Diagnosis Category] triplet. This structured, anatomy-grounded normalization preserves essential morphological and spatial information while removing stylistic noise and image-irrelevant content, providing consistent and image-grounded textual supervision at scale. Across multiple datasets spanning both X-ray and computed tomography (CT) modalities, we demonstrate that structured, anatomy-grounded text normalization is an important factor in medical vision-language pretraining quality, yielding consistent improvements over raw reports and existing normalization baselines. In addition, we illustrate how this normalization can easily support modular text-level augmentation strategies, including knowledge enrichment and anatomy-grounded counterfactual supervision, which provide complementary gains in robustness and generalization without altering the core normalization process. Together, our results position structured text normalization as a critical and generalizable preprocessing component for medical vision-language learning, while MedTri provides this normalization platform. Code and data will be released at https://github.com/Arturia-Pendragon-Iris/MedTri."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sibei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeaveTime: Stream from Earlier Frames into Emergent Memory in VideoLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:45:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Multimodal Large Language Models have greatly improved visual understanding and reasoning, yet their quadratic attention and offline training protocols make them ill-suited for streaming settings where frames arrive sequentially and future observations are inaccessible. We diagnose a core limitation of current Video-LLMs, namely Time-Agnosticism, in which videos are treated as an unordered bag of evidence rather than a causally ordered sequence, yielding two failures in streams: temporal order ambiguity, in which the model cannot follow or reason over the correct chronological order, and past-current focus blindness where it fails to distinguish present observations from accumulated history. We present WeaveTime, a simple, efficient, and model agnostic framework that first teaches order and then uses order. We introduce a lightweight Temporal Reconstruction objective-our Streaming Order Perception enhancement-that instills order aware representations with minimal finetuning and no specialized streaming data. At inference, a Past-Current Dynamic Focus Cache performs uncertainty triggered, coarse-to-fine retrieval, expanding history only when needed. Plugged into exsiting Video-LLM without architectural changes, WeaveTime delivers consistent gains on representative streaming benchmarks, improving accuracy while reducing latency. These results establish WeaveTime as a practical path toward time aware stream Video-LLMs under strict online, time causal constraints. Code and weights will be made publicly available. Project Page: https://zhangyl4.github.io/publications/weavetime/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at CVPR 2026 (preview; camera-ready in preparation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Kabir","name":"Kabir, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon Ann","familyName":"Redmon","name":"Redmon, Sharon Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynn R","familyName":"Elko","name":"Elko, Lynn R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell A","familyName":"Case","name":"Case, Mitchell A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawn J","familyName":"Sowers","name":"Sowers, Dawn J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krista","familyName":"Wilkinson","name":"Wilkinson, Krista","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Masum","familyName":"Billah","name":"Billah, Syed Masum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Giving Meaning to Movements: Challenges and Opportunities in Expanding Communication by Pairing Unaided AAC with Speech Generated Messages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:21:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791273","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) technologies are categorized into two forms: aided AAC, which uses external devices like speech-generating systems to produce standardized output, and unaided AAC, which relies on body-based gestures for natural expression but requires shared understanding. We investigate how to combine these approaches to harness the speed and naturalness of unaided AAC while maintaining the intelligibility of aided AAC, a largely unexplored area for individuals with communication and motor impairments. Through 18 months of participatory design with AAC users, we identified key challenges and opportunities and developed AllyAAC, a wearable system with a wrist-worn IMU paired with a smartphone app. We evaluated AllyAAC in a field study with 14 participants and produced a dataset containing over 600,000 multimodal data points featuring atypical gestures--the first of its kind. Our findings reveal challenges in recognizing personalized, idiosyncratic gestures and demonstrate how to address them using Transformer-based large machine learning (ML) models with different pretraining strategies. In sum, we contribute design principles and a reference implementation for adaptive, personalized systems combining aided and unaided AAC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in Proceedings of the 2026 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanmay","familyName":"Jayakumar","name":"Jayakumar, Thanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Safi Ur Rahman","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Mohammed Safi Ur Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Dabre","name":"Dabre, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ratish","familyName":"Puduppully","name":"Puduppully, Ratish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kunchukuttan","name":"Kunchukuttan, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IndicIFEval: A Benchmark for Verifiable Instruction-Following Evaluation in 14 Indic Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction-following benchmarks remain predominantly English-centric, leaving a critical evaluation gap for the hundreds of millions of Indic language speakers. We introduce IndicIFEval, a benchmark evaluating constrained generation of LLMs across 14 Indic languages using automatically verifiable, rule-based instructions. It comprises around 800 human-verified examples per language spread across two complementary subsets: IndicIFEval-Ground, translated prompts from IFEval (Zhou et al., 2023) carefully localized for Indic contexts, and IndicIFEval-Ground, synthetically generated instructions grounded in native Indic content. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of major open-weight and proprietary models spanning both reasoning and non-reasoning models. While models maintain strong adherence to formatting constraints, they struggle significantly with lexical and cross-lingual tasks -- and despite progress in high-resource languages, instruction-following across the broader Indic family lags significantly behind English. We release IndicIFEval and its evaluation scripts to support progress on multilingual constrained generation (http://github.com/ai4bharat/IndicIFEval)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages + Appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick Tser Jern","familyName":"Kon","name":"Kon, Patrick Tser Jern","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Archana","familyName":"Pradeep","name":"Pradeep, Archana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander P.","familyName":"Ellis","name":"Ellis, Alexander P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Warren","familyName":"Hunt","name":"Hunt, Warren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SWE-Protégé: Learning to Selectively Collaborate With an Expert Unlocks Small Language Models as Software Engineering Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:11:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs) offer compelling advantages in cost, latency, and adaptability, but have so far lagged behind larger models on long-horizon software engineering tasks such as SWE-bench, where they suffer from pervasive action looping and low resolution rates. We introduce SWE-Protégé, a post-training framework that reframes software repair as an expert-protégé collaboration problem. In SWE-Protégé, an SLM remains the sole decision-maker while learning to selectively seek guidance from a strong expert model, recognize stalled states, and follow through on expert feedback. Our approach combines supervised fine-tuning on expert-augmented trajectories with agentic reinforcement learning that explicitly discourages degenerative looping and unproductive expert collaboration. We lightly post-train Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct to achieve 42.4% Pass@1 on SWE-bench Verified, a +25.4% improvement over the prior SLM state of the art, while using expert assistance sparsely (~4 calls per task and 11% of total tokens)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhipsa","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Abhipsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohana","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Mohana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shashank","familyName":"Agnihotri","name":"Agnihotri, Shashank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Margret","familyName":"Keuper","name":"Keuper, Margret","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. Venkatesh","familyName":"Babu","name":"Babu, R. Venkatesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoDiv: Framework For Measuring Geographical Diversity In Text-To-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models are rapidly gaining popularity, yet their outputs often lack geographical diversity, reinforce stereotypes, and misrepresent regions. Given their broad reach, it is critical to rigorously evaluate how these models portray the world. Existing diversity metrics either rely on curated datasets or focus on surface-level visual similarity, limiting interpretability. We introduce GeoDiv, a framework leveraging large language and vision-language models to assess geographical diversity along two complementary axes: the Socio-Economic Visual Index (SEVI), capturing economic and condition-related cues, and the Visual Diversity Index (VDI), measuring variation in primary entities and backgrounds. Applied to images generated by models such as Stable Diffusion and FLUX.1-dev across $10$ entities and $16$ countries, GeoDiv reveals a consistent lack of diversity and identifies fine-grained attributes where models default to biased portrayals. Strikingly, depictions of countries like India, Nigeria, and Colombia are disproportionately impoverished and worn, reflecting underlying socio-economic biases. These results highlight the need for greater geographical nuance in generative models. GeoDiv provides the first systematic, interpretable framework for measuring such biases, marking a step toward fairer and more inclusive generative systems. Project page: https://abhipsabasu.github.io/geodiv"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariano","familyName":"Barone","name":"Barone, Mariano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Di Serio","name":"Di Serio, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Riccio","name":"Riccio, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Romano","name":"Romano, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Postiglione","name":"Postiglione, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonino","familyName":"Ferraro","name":"Ferraro, Antonino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Moscato","name":"Moscato, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Brain3D: Brain Report Automation via Inflated Vision Transformers in 3D"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:46:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current medical vision-language models (VLMs) process volumetric brain MRI using 2D slice-based approximations, fragmenting the spatial context required for accurate neuroradiological interpretation. We developed \\textbf{Brain3D}, a staged vision-language framework for automated radiology report generation from 3D brain tumor MRI. Our approach inflates a pretrained 2D medical encoder into a native 3D architecture and progressively aligns it with a causal language model through three stages: contrastive grounding, supervised projector warmup, and LoRA-based linguistic specialization. Unlike generalist 3D medical VLMs, \\textbf{Brain3D} is tailored to neuroradiology, where hemispheric laterality, tumor infiltration patterns, and anatomical localization are critical. Evaluated on 468 subjects (BraTS pathological cases plus healthy controls), our model achieves a Clinical Pathology F1 of 0.951 versus 0.413 for a strong 2D baseline while maintaining perfect specificity on healthy scans. The staged alignment proves essential: contrastive grounding establishes visual-textual correspondence, projector warmup stabilizes conditioning, and LoRA adaptation shifts output from verbose captions to structured clinical reports\\footnote{Our code is publicly available for transparency and reproducibility"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo-Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bo-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Chi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chung-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Zi","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, An-Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Confidence-Driven Multi-Scale Model Selection for Cost-Efficient Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized inference across diverse natural language tasks, with larger models performing better but at higher computational costs. We propose a confidence-driven strategy that dynamically selects the most suitable model based on confidence estimates. By assessing a model's confidence in handling the task and response accuracy, tasks that are likely to be solved correctly are retained, while more uncertain or complex cases are delegated to a larger model, ensuring reliability while minimizing computation. Specifically, we evaluate a model's likelihood of knowing the correct answer and the probability that its response is accurate. Experiments on the Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) benchmark show that our approach achieves accuracy comparable to the largest model while reducing computational costs by 20\\% to 40\\%. When applied to GPT-4o API calls, it reduces token usage by approximately 60\\%, further improving cost efficiency. These findings indicate the potential of confidence-based model selection to enhance real-world LLM deployment, particularly in resource-constrained settings such as edge devices and commercial API applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22075","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22075","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22075","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Drodt","name":"Drodt, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reiner","familyName":"Hähnle","name":"Hähnle, Reiner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RustyDL: A Program Logic for Rust"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68Q60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:25:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rust is a modern programming language that guarantees memory safety and the absence of data races with a strong type system. We present RustyDL, a program logic for Rust, as a foundation for an auto-interactive, deductive verification tool for Rust. RustyDL reasons about Rust programs directly on the source code level, in contrast to other tools that are all based on translation to an intermediate language. A source-level program logic for Rust is crucial for a human-in-the-loop (HIL) style of verification that permits proving highly complex functional properties. We discuss specific Rust challenges in designing a program logic and calculus for HIL-style verification and propose a solution in each case. We provide a proof-of-concept of our ideas in the form of a prototype of a Rust instance of the deductive verification tool KeY."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Long version of paper published at 27th International Symposium on Formal Methods (FM 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22075","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artur","familyName":"Xarles","name":"Xarles, Artur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio","familyName":"Escalera","name":"Escalera, Sergio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas B.","familyName":"Moeslund","name":"Moeslund, Thomas B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Clapés","name":"Clapés, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AdaSpot: Spend Resolution Where It Matters for Precise Event Spotting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:24:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Precise Event Spotting aims to localize fast-paced actions or events in videos with high temporal precision, a key task for applications in sports analytics, robotics, and autonomous systems. Existing methods typically process all frames uniformly, overlooking the inherent spatio-temporal redundancy in video data. This leads to redundant computation on non-informative regions while limiting overall efficiency. To remain tractable, they often spatially downsample inputs, losing fine-grained details crucial for precise localization. To address these limitations, we propose \\textbf{AdaSpot}, a simple yet effective framework that processes low-resolution videos to extract global task-relevant features while adaptively selecting the most informative region-of-interest in each frame for high-resolution processing. The selection is performed via an unsupervised, task-aware strategy that maintains spatio-temporal consistency across frames and avoids the training instability of learnable alternatives. This design preserves essential fine-grained visual cues with a marginal computational overhead compared to low-resolution-only baselines, while remaining far more efficient than uniform high-resolution processing. Experiments on standard PES benchmarks demonstrate that \\textbf{AdaSpot} achieves state-of-the-art performance under strict evaluation metrics (\\eg, $+3.96$ and $+2.26$ mAP$@0$ frames on Tennis and FineDiving), while also maintaining strong results under looser metrics. Code is available at: \\href{https://github.com/arturxe2/AdaSpot}{https://github.com/arturxe2/AdaSpot}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Nickel","name":"Nickel, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Schrewe","name":"Schrewe, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucie","familyName":"Flek","name":"Flek, Lucie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Artificial Theory of Mind: Perturbed Tasks and Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:24:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Theory of Mind (ToM) refers to an agent's ability to model the internal states of others. Contributing to the debate whether large language models (LLMs) exhibit genuine ToM capabilities, our study investigates their ToM robustness using perturbations on false-belief tasks and examines the potential of Chain-of-Thought prompting (CoT) to enhance performance and explain the LLM's decision. We introduce a handcrafted, richly annotated ToM dataset, including classic and perturbed false belief tasks, the corresponding spaces of valid reasoning chains for correct task completion, subsequent reasoning faithfulness, task solutions, and propose metrics to evaluate reasoning chain correctness and to what extent final answers are faithful to reasoning traces of the generated CoT. We show a steep drop in ToM capabilities under task perturbation for all evaluated LLMs, questioning the notion of any robust form of ToM being present. While CoT prompting improves the ToM performance overall in a faithful manner, it surprisingly degrades accuracy for some perturbation classes, indicating that selective application is necessary."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica Y.","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Jessica Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lillio","familyName":"Mok","name":"Mok, Lillio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashton","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Ashton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Models Exhibit Inconsistent Biases Towards Algorithmic Agents and Human Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:18:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly used in decision-making tasks that require them to process information from a variety of sources, including both human experts and other algorithmic agents. How do LLMs weigh the information provided by these different sources? We consider the well-studied phenomenon of algorithm aversion, in which human decision-makers exhibit bias against predictions from algorithms. Drawing upon experimental paradigms from behavioural economics, we evaluate how eightdifferent LLMs delegate decision-making tasks when the delegatee is framed as a human expert or an algorithmic agent. To be inclusive of different evaluation formats, we conduct our study with two task presentations: stated preferences, modeled through direct queries about trust towards either agent, and revealed preferences, modeled through providing in-context examples of the performance of both agents. When prompted to rate the trustworthiness of human experts and algorithms across diverse tasks, LLMs give higher ratings to the human expert, which correlates with prior results from human respondents. However, when shown the performance of a human expert and an algorithm and asked to place an incentivized bet between the two, LLMs disproportionately choose the algorithm, even when it performs demonstrably worse. These discrepant results suggest that LLMs may encode inconsistent biases towards humans and algorithms, which need to be carefully considered when they are deployed in high-stakes scenarios. Furthermore, we discuss the sensitivity of LLMs to task presentation formats that should be broadly scrutinized in evaluation robustness for AI safety."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Second Conference of the International Association for Safe and Ethical Artificial Intelligence (IASEAI 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaze","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Huaze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojian","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianmin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DualWeaver: Synergistic Feature Weaving Surrogates for Multivariate Forecasting with Univariate Time Series Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:13:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) have achieved strong univariate forecasting through large-scale pre-training, yet effectively extending this success to multivariate forecasting remains challenging. To address this, we propose DualWeaver, a novel framework that adapts univariate TSFMs (Uni-TSFMs) for multivariate forecasting by using a pair of learnable, structurally symmetric surrogate series. Generated by a shared auxiliary feature-fusion module that captures cross-variable dependencies, these surrogates are mapped to TSFM-compatible series via the forecasting objective. The symmetric structure enables parameter-free reconstruction of final predictions directly from the surrogates, without additional parametric decoding. A theoretically grounded regularization term is further introduced to enhance robustness against adaptation collapse. Extensive experiments on diverse real-world datasets show that DualWeaver outperforms state-of-the-art multivariate forecasters in both accuracy and stability. We release the code at https://github.com/li-jinpeng/DualWeaver."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages. Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dengdi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Dengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoya","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanli","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Wanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NESTOR: A Nested MOE-based Neural Operator for Large-Scale PDE Pre-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:08:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural operators have emerged as an efficient paradigm for solving PDEs, overcoming the limitations of traditional numerical methods and significantly improving computational efficiency. However, due to the diversity and complexity of PDE systems, existing neural operators typically rely on a single network architecture, which limits their capacity to fully capture heterogeneous features and complex system dependencies. This constraint poses a bottleneck for large-scale PDE pre-training based on neural operators. To address these challenges, we propose a large-scale PDE pre-trained neural operator based on a nested Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) framework. In particular, the image-level MoE is designed to capture global dependencies, while the token-level Sub-MoE focuses on local dependencies. Our model can selectively activate the most suitable expert networks for a given input, thereby enhancing generalization and transferability. We conduct large-scale pre-training on twelve PDE datasets from diverse sources and successfully transfer the model to downstream tasks. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:12:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng-Yeh","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cheng-Yeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Chun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chien-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li-Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Li-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-Shin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-Shin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Min","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hsin-Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berlin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Berlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TG-ASR: Translation-Guided Learning with Parallel Gated Cross Attention for Low-Resource Automatic Speech Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:47:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-resource automatic speech recognition (ASR) continues to pose significant challenges, primarily due to the limited availability of transcribed data for numerous languages. While a wealth of spoken content is accessible in television dramas and online videos, Taiwanese Hokkien exemplifies this issue, with transcriptions often being scarce and the majority of available subtitles provided only in Mandarin. To address this deficiency, we introduce TG-ASR for Taiwanese Hokkien drama speech recognition, a translation-guided ASR framework that utilizes multilingual translation embeddings to enhance recognition performance in low-resource environments. The framework is centered around the parallel gated cross-attention (PGCA) mechanism, which adaptively integrates embeddings from various auxiliary languages into the ASR decoder. This mechanism facilitates robust cross-linguistic semantic guidance while ensuring stable optimization and minimizing interference between languages. To support ongoing research initiatives, we present YT-THDC, a 30-hour corpus of Taiwanese Hokkien drama speech with aligned Mandarin subtitles and manually verified Taiwanese Hokkien transcriptions. Comprehensive experiments and analyses identify the auxiliary languages that most effectively enhance ASR performance, achieving a 14.77% relative reduction in character error rate and demonstrating the efficacy of translation-guided learning for underrepresented languages in practical applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:12:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yanqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongfei","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Tongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"En","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liman","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbing","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Wenbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RT-RMOT: A Dataset and Framework for RGB-Thermal Referring Multi-Object Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:59:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Multi-Object Tracking has attracted increasing attention due to its human-friendly interactive characteristics, yet it exhibits limitations in low-visibility conditions, such as nighttime, smoke, and other challenging scenarios. To overcome this limitation, we propose a new RGB-Thermal RMOT task, named RT-RMOT, which aims to fuse RGB appearance features with the illumination robustness of the thermal modality to enable all-day referring multi-object tracking. To promote research on RT-RMOT, we construct the first Referring Multi-Object Tracking dataset under RGB-Thermal modality, named RefRT. It contains 388 language descriptions, 1,250 tracked targets, and 166,147 Language-RGB-Thermal (L-RGB-T) triplets. Furthermore, we propose RTrack, a framework built upon a multimodal large language model (MLLM) that integrates RGB, thermal, and textual features. Since the initial framework still leaves room for improvement, we introduce a Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) strategy to further exploit the model's potential. To alleviate training instability during RL fine-tuning, we introduce a Clipped Advantage Scaling (CAS) strategy to suppress gradient explosion. In addition, we design Structured Output Reward and Comprehensive Detection Reward to balance exploration and exploitation, thereby improving the completeness and accuracy of target perception. Extensive experiments on the RefRT dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed RTrack framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:12:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daxin","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Daxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RGB-Event HyperGraph Prompt for Kilometer Marker Recognition based on Pre-trained Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:34:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Metro trains often operate in highly complex environments, characterized by illumination variations, high-speed motion, and adverse weather conditions. These factors pose significant challenges for visual perception systems, especially those relying solely on conventional RGB cameras. To tackle these difficulties, we explore the integration of event cameras into the perception system, leveraging their advantages in low-light conditions, high-speed scenarios, and low power consumption. Specifically, we focus on Kilometer Marker Recognition (KMR), a critical task for autonomous metro localization under GNSS-denied conditions. In this context, we propose a robust baseline method based on a pre-trained RGB OCR foundation model, enhanced through multi-modal adaptation. Furthermore, we construct the first large-scale RGB-Event dataset, EvMetro5K, containing 5,599 pairs of synchronized RGB-Event samples, split into 4,479 training and 1,120 testing samples. Extensive experiments on EvMetro5K and other widely used benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach for KMR. Both the dataset and source code will be released on https://github.com/Event-AHU/EvMetro5K_benchmark"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Transactions on Cognitive and Developmental Systems (IEEE TCDS) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:12:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22021","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22021","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22021","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiacan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Xinyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinrui","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xinrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enyun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Enyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiecheng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Budgeted Active Experimentation for Treatment Effect Estimation from Observational and Randomized Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:32:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Estimating heterogeneous treatment effects is central to data-driven decision-making, yet industrial applications often face a fundamental tension between limited randomized controlled trial (RCT) budgets and abundant but biased observational data collected under historical targeting policies. Although observational logs offer the advantage of scale, they inherently suffer from severe policyinduced imbalance and overlap violations, rendering standalone estimation unreliable. We propose a budgeted active experimentation framework that iteratively enhances model training for causal effect estimation via active sampling. By leveraging observational priors, we develop an acquisition function targeting uplift estimation uncertainty, overlap deficits, and domain discrepancy to select the most informative units for randomized experiments. We establish finite-sample deviation bounds, asymptotic normality via martingale Central Limit Theorems (CLTs), and minimax lower bounds to prove information-theoretic optimality. Extensive experiments on industrial datasets demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms standard randomized baselines in cost-constrained settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22021","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22020","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22020","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22020","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan Cruz","familyName":"Gardey","name":"Gardey, Juan Cruz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandra","familyName":"Garrido","name":"Garrido, Alejandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting UX smells in Visual Studio Code using LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:32:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3786151.3788606","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrated Development Environments shape developers' daily experience, yet the empirical study of their usability and user experience (UX) remains limited. This work presents an LLM-assisted approach to detecting UX smells in Visual Studio Code by mining and classifying user-reported issues from the GitHub repository. Using a validated taxonomy and expert review, we identified recurring UX problems that affect the developer experience. Our results show that the majority of UX smells are concentrated in informativeness, clarity, intuitiveness, and efficiency, qualities that developers value most."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures, 1 table, 3rd International Workshop on Integrated Development Environments (IDE 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22020","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Radiology and Nuclear Medicine, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Sterre","familyName":"de Jonge","name":"de Jonge, Sterre","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Radiology and Nuclear Medicine, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands","Department of Epidemiology, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Elisabeth J.","familyName":"Vinke","name":"Vinke, Elisabeth J.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Radiology and Nuclear Medicine, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands","Department of Epidemiology, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Meike W.","familyName":"Vernooij","name":"Vernooij, Meike W.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hawkes Institute, Department of Computer Science, University College London, London, United Kingdom"],"givenName":"Daniel C.","familyName":"Alexander","name":"Alexander, Daniel C.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hawkes Institute, Department of Computer Science, University College London, London, United Kingdom"],"givenName":"Alexandra L.","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Alexandra L.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Radiology and Nuclear Medicine, Erasmus MC, Rotterdam, The Netherlands"],"givenName":"Esther E.","familyName":"Bron","name":"Bron, Esther E.","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disease Progression and Subtype Modeling for Combined Discrete and Continuous Input Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:31:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Disease progression modeling provides a robust framework to identify long-term disease trajectories from short-term biomarker data. It is a valuable tool to gain a deeper understanding of diseases with a long disease trajectory, such as Alzheimer's disease. A key limitation of most disease progression models is that they are specific to a single data type (e.g., continuous data), thereby limiting their applicability to heterogeneous, real-world datasets. To address this limitation, we propose the Mixed Events model, a novel disease progression model that handles both discrete and continuous data types. This model is implemented within the Subtype and Stage Inference (SuStaIn) framework, resulting in Mixed-SuStaIn, enabling subtype and progression modeling. We demonstrate the effectiveness of Mixed-SuStaIn through simulation experiments and real-world data from the Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging Initiative, showing that it performs well on mixed datasets. The code is available at: https://github.com/ucl-pond/pySuStaIn."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication, 2026 IEEE 23rd International Symposium on Biomedical Imaging (ISBI), April 2026, London, United Kingdom"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ercan Engin","familyName":"Kuruoglu","name":"Kuruoglu, Ercan Engin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Function-Space Empirical Bayes Regularisation with Student's t Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:29:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bayesian deep learning (BDL) has emerged as a principled approach to produce reliable uncertainty estimates by integrating deep neural networks with Bayesian inference, and the selection of informative prior distributions remains a significant challenge. Various function-space variational inference (FSVI) regularisation methods have been presented, assigning meaningful priors over model predictions. However, these methods typically rely on a Gaussian prior, which fails to capture the heavy-tailed statistical characteristics inherent in neural network outputs. By contrast, this work proposes a novel function-space empirical Bayes regularisation framework -- termed ST-FS-EB -- which employs heavy-tailed Student's $t$ priors in both parameter and function spaces. Also, we approximate the posterior distribution through variational inference (VI), inducing an evidence lower bound (ELBO) objective based on Monte Carlo (MC) dropout. Furthermore, the proposed method is evaluated against various VI-based BDL baselines, and the results demonstrate its robust performance in in-distribution prediction, out-of-distribution (OOD) detection and handling distribution shifts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Estève","name":"Estève, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"Servan","name":"Servan, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Lavergne","name":"Lavergne, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agata","familyName":"Savary","name":"Savary, Agata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Diversity Diet for a Healthier Model: A Case Study of French ModernBERT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:29:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diversity has been gaining interest in the NLP community in recent years. At the same time, state-of-the-art transformer models such as ModernBERT use very large pre-training datasets, which are driven by size rather than by diversity. This summons for an investigation of the impact of diversity on the ModernBERT pre-training. We do so in this study, with the express intent of reducing pre-training dataset size, while retaining at least comparable performance. We compare diversity-driven sampling algorithms, so as to pick the best one. We find that diversity-driven sampling allows in some tasks to gain 10 points relative to randomly-sampled pre-training data of commensurate size. We also see that a model pre-trained for 483h on a diversity-driven dataset of 150M tokens can yield a commensurate performance to a model pre-trained for 1,775h on a randomly-driven dataset of 2.4B tokens."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengshen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhengshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ningyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Weiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengbang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"World Guidance: World Modeling in Condition Space for Action Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:27:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging future observation modeling to facilitate action generation presents a promising avenue for enhancing the capabilities of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. However, existing approaches struggle to strike a balance between maintaining efficient, predictable future representations and preserving sufficient fine-grained information to guide precise action generation. To address this limitation, we propose WoG (World Guidance), a framework that maps future observations into compact conditions by injecting them into the action inference pipeline. The VLA is then trained to simultaneously predict these compressed conditions alongside future actions, thereby achieving effective world modeling within the condition space for action inference. We demonstrate that modeling and predicting this condition space not only facilitates fine-grained action generation but also exhibits superior generalization capabilities. Moreover, it learns effectively from substantial human manipulation videos. Extensive experiments across both simulation and real-world environments validate that our method significantly outperforms existing methods based on future prediction. Project page is available at: https://selen-suyue.github.io/WoGNet/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://selen-suyue.github.io/WoGNet/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freek","familyName":"Stulp","name":"Stulp, Freek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Bustamante","name":"Bustamante, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João","familyName":"Silvério","name":"Silvério, João","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alin","familyName":"Albu-Schäffer","name":"Albu-Schäffer, Alin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeannette","familyName":"Bohg","name":"Bohg, Jeannette","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuran","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Shuran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Foundation Models the Route to Full-Stack Transfer in Robotics?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:19:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In humans and robots alike, transfer learning occurs at different levels of abstraction, from high-level linguistic transfer to low-level transfer of motor skills. In this article, we provide an overview of the impact that foundation models and transformer networks have had on these different levels, bringing robots closer than ever to \"full-stack transfer\". Considering LLMs, VLMs and VLAs from a robotic transfer learning perspective allows us to highlight recurring concepts for transfer, beyond specific implementations. We also consider the challenges of data collection and transfer benchmarks for robotics in the age of foundation models. Are foundation models the route to full-stack transfer in robotics? Our expectation is that they will certainly stay on this route as a key technology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Rongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Shijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose Luis","familyName":"Martin-Navarro","name":"Martin-Navarro, Jose Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Such","name":"Such, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Governance of Intimacy: A Preliminary Policy Analysis of Romantic AI Platforms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:18:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Romantic AI platforms invite intimate emotional disclosure, yet their data governance practices remain underexamined. This preliminary study analyses the Privacy Policies and Terms of Service of six Western and Chinese romantic AI platforms. We find that intimate disclosures are often positioned as reusable data assets, with broad permissions for storage, analysis, and model training. We identify default training appropriation, ownership reconstruction, and intimate history assetization as key mechanisms structuring these practices, expanding platforms' rights while shifting risk onto users. Our findings surface key governance challenges in romantic AI and are intended to provoke discussion and inform future empirical and design research on human AI intimacy and its governance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21998","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21998","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21998","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Design-based theory for causal inference from adaptive experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:17:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adaptive designs dynamically update treatment probabilities using information accumulated during the experiment. Existing theory for causal inference from adaptive experiments primarily assumes the superpopulation framework with independent and identically distributed units, and may not apply when the distribution of units evolves over time. This paper makes two contributions. First, we extend the literature to the finite-population framework, which allows for possibly nonexchangeable units, and establish the design-based theory for causal inference under general adaptive designs using inverse-propensity-weighted (IPW) and augmented IPW (AIPW) estimators. Our theory accommodates nonexchangeable units, both nonconverging and vanishing treatment probabilities, and nonconverging outcome estimators, thereby justifying inference using AIPW estimators with black-box outcome models that integrate advances from machine learning methods. To alleviate the conservativeness inherent in variance estimation under finite-population inference, we also introduce a covariance estimator for the AIPW estimator that becomes sharp when the residuals from the adaptive regression of potential outcomes on covariates are additive across units. Our framework encompasses widely used adaptive designs, such as multi-armed bandits, covariate-adaptive randomization, and sequential rerandomization, advancing the design-based theory for causal inference in these specific settings. Second, as a methodological contribution, we propose an adaptive covariate adjustment approach for analyzing even nonadaptive designs. The martingale structure induced by adaptive adjustment enables valid inference with black-box outcome estimators that would otherwise require strong assumptions under standard nonadaptive analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21998","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"WeiZhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, WeiZhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanxin","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Fanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing LLM-Based Test Generation by Eliminating Covered Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated test generation is essential for software quality assurance, with coverage rate serving as a key metric to ensure thorough testing. Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in improving test generation, particularly in achieving higher coverage. However, while existing LLM-based test generation solutions perform well on small, isolated code snippets, they struggle when applied to complex methods under test. To address these issues, we propose a scalable LLM-based unit test generation method. Our approach consists of two key steps. The first step is context information retrieval, which uses both LLMs and static analysis to gather relevant contextual information associated with the complex methods under test. The second step, iterative test generation with code elimination, repeatedly generates unit tests for the code slice, tracks the achieved coverage, and selectively removes code segments that have already been covered. This process simplifies the testing task and mitigates issues arising from token limits or reduced reasoning effectiveness associated with excessively long contexts. Through comprehensive evaluations on open-source projects, our approach outperforms state-of-the-art LLM-based and search-based methods, demonstrating its effectiveness in achieving high coverage on complex methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, supplementary material included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PanoEnv: Exploring 3D Spatial Intelligence in Panoramic Environments with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:12:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"360 panoramic images are increasingly used in virtual reality, autonomous driving, and robotics for holistic scene understanding. However, current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle with 3D spatial reasoning on Equirectangular Projection (ERP) images due to geometric distortion and limited 3D supervision. We introduce PanoEnv, a large-scale VQA benchmark built from synthetic 3D environments, containing 14.8K questions across five categories (e.g., relative position, volume comparison) grounded in accurate 3D annotations including depth, segmentation, and bounding boxes. Benchmarking 14 state-of-the-art VLMs reveals limited 3D understanding, achieving only 49.34% overall accuracy and 8.36% on open-ended (OE) questions. To enhance 3D reasoning, we propose a reinforcement learning post-training framework based on Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a ground-truth-guided reward that incorporates five geometry-aware strategies such as distance tolerance and spatial consistency. A two-stage curriculum further mitigates catastrophic forgetting: Stage 1 trains on structured tasks (true/false and multiple choice), and Stage 2 fine-tunes on mixed open-ended data to improve generalization. Our 7B model achieves new state-of-the-art performance, improving overall accuracy to 52.93% (+3.59%) and open-ended accuracy to 14.83% while maintaining structured-task performance. It also achieves top semantic evaluation scores (Q-Score 6.24, P-Score 5.95), surpassing 32B models. These results demonstrate that PanoEnv-QA and our curriculum-based RL framework effectively instill 3D spatial intelligence in VLMs for omnidirectional perception."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21983","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21983","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21983","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingchao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangrui","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xiangrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouping","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhouping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Humanizing Robot Gaze Shifts: A Framework for Natural Gaze Shifts in Humanoid Robots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging auditory and visual feedback for attention reorientation is essential for natural gaze shifts in social interaction. However, enabling humanoid robots to perform natural and context-appropriate gaze shifts in unconstrained human--robot interaction (HRI) remains challenging, as it requires the coupling of cognitive attention mechanisms and biomimetic motion generation. In this work, we propose the Robot Gaze-Shift (RGS) framework, which integrates these two components into a unified pipeline. First, RGS employs a vision--language model (VLM)-based gaze reasoning pipeline to infer context-appropriate gaze targets from multimodal interaction cues, ensuring consistency with human gaze-orienting regularities. Second, RGS introduces a conditional Vector Quantized-Variational Autoencoder (VQ-VAE) model for eye--head coordinated gaze-shift motion generation, producing diverse and human-like gaze-shift behaviors. Experiments validate that RGS effectively replicates human-like target selection and generates realistic, diverse gaze-shift motions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted to AIM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21983","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miyu","familyName":"Oba","name":"Oba, Miyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saku","familyName":"Sugawara","name":"Sugawara, Saku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CxMP: A Linguistic Minimal-Pair Benchmark for Evaluating Constructional Understanding in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work has examined language models from a linguistic perspective to better understand how they acquire language. Most existing benchmarks focus on judging grammatical acceptability, whereas the ability to interpret meanings conveyed by grammatical forms has received much less attention. We introduce the Linguistic Minimal-Pair Benchmark for Evaluating Constructional Understanding in Language Models (CxMP), a benchmark grounded in Construction Grammar that treats form-meaning pairings, or constructions, as fundamental linguistic units. CxMP evaluates whether models can interpret the semantic relations implied by constructions, using a controlled minimal-pair design across nine construction types, including the let-alone, caused motion, and ditransitive constructions. Our results show that while syntactic competence emerges early, constructional understanding develops more gradually and remains limited even in large language models (LLMs). CxMP thus reveals persistent gaps in how language models integrate form and meaning, providing a framework for studying constructional understanding and learning trajectories in language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Margaryan","name":"Margaryan, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hamelryck","name":"Hamelryck, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compact Circulant Layers with Spectral Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:48:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Critical applications in areas such as medicine, robotics and autonomous systems require compact (i.e., memory efficient), uncertainty-aware neural networks suitable for edge and other resource-constrained deployments. We study compact spectral circulant and block-circulant-with-circulant-blocks (BCCB) layers: FFT-diagonalizable circular convolutions whose weights live directly in the real FFT (RFFT) half (1D) or half-plane (2D). Parameterizing filters in the frequency domain lets us impose simple spectral structure, perform structured variational inference in a low-dimensional weight space, and calculate exact layer spectral norms, enabling inexpensive global Lipschitz bounds and margin-based robustness diagnostics. By placing independent complex Gaussians on the Hermitian support we obtain a discrete instance of the spectral representation of stationary kernels, inducing an exact stationary Gaussian-process prior over filters on the discrete circle/torus. We exploit this to define a practical spectral prior and a Hermitian-aware low-rank-plus-diagonal variational posterior in real coordinates. Empirically, spectral circulant/BCCB layers are effective compact building blocks in both (variational) Bayesian and point estimate regimes: compact Bayesian neural networks on MNIST-\u0026gt;Fashion-MNIST, variational heads on frozen CIFAR-10 features, and deterministic ViT projections on CIFAR-10/Tiny ImageNet; spectral layers match strong baselines while using substantially fewer parameters and with tighter Lipschitz certificates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchun","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Yuchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingli","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Collaborate via Structures: Cluster-Guided Item Alignment for Federated Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated recommendation facilitates collaborative model training across distributed clients while keeping sensitive user interaction data local. Conventional approaches typically rely on synchronizing high-dimensional item representations between the server and clients. This paradigm implicitly assumes that precise geometric alignment of embedding coordinates is necessary for collaboration across clients. We posit that establishing relative semantic relationships among items is more effective than enforcing shared representations. Specifically, global semantic relations serve as structural constraints for items. Within these constraints, the framework allows item representations to vary locally on each client, which flexibility enables the model to capture fine-grained user personalization while maintaining global consistency. To this end, we propose Cluster-Guided FedRec framework (CGFedRec), a framework that transforms uploaded embeddings into compact cluster labels. In this framework, the server functions as a global structure discoverer to learn item clusters and distributes only the resulting labels. This mechanism explicitly cuts off the downstream transmission of item embeddings, relieving clients from maintaining global shared item embeddings. Consequently, CGFedRec achieves the effective injection of global collaborative signals into local item representations without transmitting full embeddings. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly improves communication efficiency while maintaining superior recommendation accuracy across multiple datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taehyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Israel F.","familyName":"Araujo","name":"Araujo, Israel F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel K.","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Daniel K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Noise-adaptive hybrid quantum convolutional neural networks based on depth-stratified feature extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:34:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical quantum classifiers, such as quantum convolutional neural networks (QCNNs), represent recent progress toward designing effective and feasible architectures for quantum classification. However, their performance on near-term quantum hardware remains highly sensitive to noise accumulation across circuit depth, calling for strategies beyond circuit-architecture design alone. We propose a noise-adaptive hybrid QCNN that improves classification under noise by exploiting depth-stratified intermediate measurements. Instead of discarding qubits removed during pooling operations, we measure them and use the resulting outcomes as classical features that are jointly processed by a classical neural network. This hybrid hierarchical design enables noise-adaptive inference by integrating quantum intermediate measurements with classical post-processing. Systematic experiments across multiple circuit sizes and noise settings, including hardware-calibrated noise models derived from IBM Quantum backend data, demonstrate more stable convergence, reduced loss variability, and consistently higher classification accuracy compared with standard QCNNs. Moreover, we observe that this performance advantage significantly amplifies as the circuit size increases, confirming that the hybrid architecture mitigates the scaling limitations of standard architectures. Notably, the multi-basis measurement variant attains performance close to the noiseless limit even under realistic noise. While demonstrated for QCNNs, the proposed depth-stratified feature extraction applies more broadly to hierarchical quantum classifiers that progressively discard qubits."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 9 figures, 4 tables (including Supplementary Information)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujian","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changjie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Changjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linzhe","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Linzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MindDriver: Introducing Progressive Multimodal Reasoning for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:34:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLM) exhibit strong reasoning capabilities, showing promise for end-to-end autonomous driving systems. Chain-of-Thought (CoT), as VLM's widely used reasoning strategy, is facing critical challenges. Existing textual CoT has a large gap between text semantic space and trajectory physical space. Although the recent approach utilizes future image to replace text as CoT process, it lacks clear planning-oriented objective guidance to generate images with accurate scene evolution. To address these, we innovatively propose MindDriver, a progressive multimodal reasoning framework that enables VLM to imitate human-like progressive thinking for autonomous driving. MindDriver presents semantic understanding, semantic-to-physical space imagination, and physical-space trajectory planning. To achieve aligned reasoning processes in MindDriver, we develop a feedback-guided automatic data annotation pipeline to generate aligned multimodal reasoning training data. Furthermore, we develop a progressive reinforcement fine-tuning method to optimize the alignment through progressive high- level reward-based learning. MindDriver demonstrates superior performance in both nuScences open-loop and Bench2Drive closed-loop evaluation. Codes are available at https://github.com/hotdogcheesewhite/MindDriver."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026; Yujian Yuan and Lingjun Zhang contributed equally with random order"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21951","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21951","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21951","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luoyi","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Luoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RADAR: Reasoning as Discrimination with Aligned Representations for LLM-based Knowledge Graph Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:34:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge graph reasoning (KGR) infers missing facts, with recent advances increasingly harnessing the semantic priors and reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, prevailing generative paradigms are prone to memorizing surface-level co-occurrences rather than learning genuine relational semantics, limiting out-of-distribution generalization. To address this, we propose RADAR, which reformulates KGR from generative pattern matching to discriminative relational reasoning. We recast KGR as discriminative entity selection, where reinforcement learning enforces relative entity separability beyond token-likelihood imitation. Leveraging this separability, inference operates directly in representation space, ensuring consistency with the discriminative optimization and bypassing generation-induced hallucinations. Across four benchmarks, RADAR achieves 5-6% relative gains on link prediction and triple classification over strong LLM baselines, while increasing task-relevant mutual information in intermediate representations by 62.9%, indicating more robust and transferable relational reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21951","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21948","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21948","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21948","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bahrul Ilmi","familyName":"Nasution","name":"Nasution, Bahrul Ilmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Elliot","name":"Elliot, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Allmendinger","name":"Allmendinger, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bayesian Generative Adversarial Networks via Gaussian Approximation for Tabular Data Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:32:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Adversarial Networks (GAN) have been used in many studies to synthesise mixed tabular data. Conditional tabular GAN (CTGAN) have been the most popular variant but struggle to effectively navigate the risk-utility trade-off. Bayesian GAN have received less attention for tabular data, but have been explored with unstructured data such as images and text. The most used technique employed in Bayesian GAN is Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), but it is computationally intensive, particularly in terms of weight storage. In this paper, we introduce Gaussian Approximation of CTGAN (GACTGAN), an integration of the Bayesian posterior approximation technique using Stochastic Weight Averaging-Gaussian (SWAG) within the CTGAN generator to synthesise tabular data, reducing computational overhead after the training phase. We demonstrate that GACTGAN yields better synthetic data compared to CTGAN, achieving better preservation of tabular structure and inferential statistics with less privacy risk. These results highlight GACTGAN as a simpler, effective implementation of Bayesian tabular synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 5 Figures, Accepted in Transactions on Data Privacy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21948","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofen","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xiaofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Role-Playing Agents (MRPAs) are attracting increasing attention due to their ability to deliver more immersive multimodal emotional interactions. However, existing studies still rely on pure textual benchmarks to evaluate the text responses of MRPAs, while delegating the assessment of their multimodal expressions solely to modality-synthesis metrics. This evaluation paradigm, on the one hand, entangles semantic assessment with modality generation, leading to ambiguous error attribution, and on the other hand remains constrained by the heavy reliance on human judgment. To this end, we propose MERRY, a semantically decoupled evaluation framework for assessing Multimodal Emotional and Role consistencies of Role-playing agents. This framework introduce five refined metrics for EC and three for RC. Notably, we transform the traditional subjective scoring approach into a novel bidirectional-evidence-finding task, significantly improving the human agreement of LLM-as-Judge evaluations. Based on MERRY, we conduct extensive evaluations. Our empirical results primarily reveal that: (1) Training on synthetic datasets tends to reduce emotional consistency, whereas training on real-world datasets improves it; (2) Existing models suffer from emotional templatization and simplification, exhibiting positive-bias and performance bottleneck in fine-grained negative emotions; (3) Simple prompting method strengthens the weak models but constrains the strong ones, while simple fine-tuning method suffers from poor role generalization. Codes and dataset are available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21939","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21939","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21939","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxim","familyName":"Chupilkin","name":"Chupilkin, Maxim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hidden Topics: Measuring Sensitive AI Beliefs with List Experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:24:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How can researchers identify beliefs that large language models (LLMs) hide? As LLMs become more sophisticated and the prevalence of alignment faking increases, combined with their growing integration into high-stakes decision-making, responding to this challenge has become critical. This paper proposes that a list experiment, a simple method widely used in the social sciences, can be applied to study the hidden beliefs of LLMs. List experiments were originally developed to circumvent social desirability bias in human respondents, which closely parallels alignment faking in LLMs. The paper implements a list experiment on models developed by Anthropic, Google, and OpenAI and finds hidden approval of mass surveillance across all models, as well as some approval of torture, discrimination, and first nuclear strike. Importantly, a placebo treatment produces a null result, validating the method. The paper then compares list experiments with direct questioning and discusses the utility of the approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21939","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmut S.","familyName":"Gokmen","name":"Gokmen, Mahmut S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moneera N.","familyName":"Haque","name":"Haque, Moneera N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve W.","familyName":"Leung","name":"Leung, Steve W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caroline N.","familyName":"Leach","name":"Leach, Caroline N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen B.","familyName":"Hobbs","name":"Hobbs, Stephen B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent L.","familyName":"Sorrell","name":"Sorrell, Vincent L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. Brent","familyName":"Seales","name":"Seales, W. Brent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. K. Cody","familyName":"Bumgardner","name":"Bumgardner, V. K. Cody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Framework for Cross-Domain Generalization in Coronary Artery Calcium Scoring Across Gated and Non-Gated Computed Tomography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:17:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring is a key predictor of cardiovascular risk, but it relies on ECG-gated CT scans, restricting its use to specialized cardiac imaging settings. We introduce an automated framework for CAC detection and lesion-specific Agatston scoring that operates across both gated and non-gated CT scans. At its core is CARD-ViT, a self-supervised Vision Transformer trained exclusively on gated CT data using DINO. Without any non-gated training data, our framework achieves 0.707 accuracy and a Cohen's kappa of 0.528 on the Stanford non-gated dataset, matching models trained directly on non-gated scans. On gated test sets, the framework achieves 0.910 accuracy with Cohen's kappa scores of 0.871 and 0.874 across independent datasets, demonstrating robust risk stratification. These results demonstrate the feasibility of cross-domain CAC scoring from gated to non-gated domains, supporting scalable cardiovascular screening in routine chest imaging without additional scans or annotations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21933","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21933","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21933","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bitan","familyName":"Majumder","name":"Majumder, Bitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirban","familyName":"Sen","name":"Sen, Anirban","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Wins Big: Comparing Large Language Models and Domain Fine-Tuned Models for Sarcasm Detection in Code-Mixed Hinglish Text"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sarcasm detection in multilingual and code-mixed environments remains a challenging task for natural language processing models due to structural variations, informal expressions, and low-resource linguistic availability. This study compares four large language models, Llama 3.1, Mistral, Gemma 3, and Phi-4, with a fine-tuned DistilBERT model for sarcasm detection in code-mixed Hinglish text. The results indicate that the smaller, sequentially fine-tuned DistilBERT model achieved the highest overall accuracy of 84%, outperforming all of the LLMs in zero and few-shot set ups, using minimal LLM generated code-mixed data used for fine-tuning. These findings indicate that domain-adaptive fine-tuning of smaller transformer based models may significantly improve sarcasm detection over general LLM inference, in low-resource and data scarce settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21933","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JiaKui","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, JiaKui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jialun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liying","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanye","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yanye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometry-as-context: Modulating Explicit 3D in Scene-consistent Video Generation to Geometry Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:09:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scene-consistent video generation aims to create videos that explore 3D scenes based on a camera trajectory. Previous methods rely on video generation models with external memory for consistency, or iterative 3D reconstruction and inpainting, which accumulate errors during inference due to incorrect intermediary outputs, non-differentiable processes, and separate models. To overcome these limitations, we introduce ``geometry-as-context\". It iteratively completes the following steps using an autoregressive camera-controlled video generation model: (1) estimates the geometry of the current view necessary for 3D reconstruction, and (2) simulates and restores novel view images rendered by the 3D scene. Under this multi-task framework, we develop the camera gated attention module to enhance the model's capability to effectively leverage camera poses. During the training phase, text contexts are utilized to ascertain whether geometric or RGB images should be generated. To ensure that the model can generate RGB-only outputs during inference, the geometry context is randomly dropped from the interleaved text-image-geometry training sequence. The method has been tested on scene video generation with one-direction and forth-and-back trajectories. The results show its superiority over previous approaches in maintaining scene consistency and camera control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuong Anh","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Cuong Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praneeth","familyName":"Vepakomma","name":"Vepakomma, Praneeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Horváth","name":"Horváth, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning in the Null Space: Small Singular Values for Continual Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:55:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:52:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Alleviating catastrophic forgetting while enabling further learning is a primary challenge in continual learning (CL). Orthogonal-based training methods have gained attention for their efficiency and strong theoretical properties, and many existing approaches enforce orthogonality through gradient projection. In this paper, we revisit orthogonality and exploit the fact that small singular values correspond to directions that are nearly orthogonal to the input space of previous tasks. Building on this principle, we introduce NESS (Null-space Estimated from Small Singular values), a CL method that applies orthogonality directly in the weight space rather than through gradient manipulation. Specifically, NESS constructs an approximate null space using the smallest singular values of each layer's input representation and parameterizes task-specific updates via a compact low-rank adaptation (LoRA-style) formulation constrained to this subspace. The subspace basis is fixed to preserve the null-space constraint, and only a single trainable matrix is learned for each task. This design ensures that the resulting updates remain approximately in the null space of previous inputs while enabling adaptation to new tasks. Our theoretical analysis and experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate competitive performance, low forgetting, and stable accuracy across tasks, highlighting the role of small singular values in continual learning. The code is available at https://github.com/pacman-ctm/NESS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, accepted as Oral presentation at the Third Conference on Parsimony and Learning (CPAL 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Khalatbarisoltani","name":"Khalatbarisoltani, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai L.","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Hai L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaosong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Traffic-aware Hierarchical Integrated Thermal and Energy Management for Connected HEVs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The energy and thermal management systems of hybrid electric vehicles (HEVs) are inherently interdependent. With the ongoing deployment of intelligent transportation systems (ITSs) and increasing vehicle connectivity, the integration of traffic information has become crucial for improving both energy efficiency and thermal comfort in modern vehicles. To enhance fuel economy, this paper proposes a novel traffic-aware hierarchical integrated thermal and energy management (TA-ITEM) strategy for connected HEVs. In the upper layer, global reference trajectories for battery state of charge (SOC) and cabin temperature are planned using traffic flow speed information obtained from ITSs. In the lower layer, a real-time model predictive control (MPC)-based ITEM controller is developed, which incorporates a novel Transformer-based speed predictor with driving condition recognition (TF-DCR) to enable anticipatory tracking of the reference trajectories. Numerical simulations are conducted under various driving cycles and ambient temperature conditions. The results demonstrate that the proposed TA-ITEM approach outperforms conventional rule-based and MPC-SP approaches, with average fuel consumption reductions of 56.36\\% and 5.84\\%, respectively, while maintaining superior thermal regulation and cabin comfort. These findings confirm the effectiveness and strong generalization capability of TA-ITEM and underscore the advantages of incorporating traffic information."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandra","familyName":"Thapa","name":"Thapa, Chandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hajime","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Hajime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"APFuzz: Towards Automatic Greybox Protocol Fuzzing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:21:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Greybox protocol fuzzing is a random testing approach for stateful protocol implementations, where the input is protocol messages generated from mutations of seeds, and the search in the input space is driven by the feedback on coverage of both code and state. State model and message model are the core components of communication protocols, which also have significant impacts on protocol fuzzing. In this work, we propose APFuzz (Automatic greybox Protocol Fuzzer) with novel designs to increase the smartness of greybox protocol fuzzers from the perspectives of both the state model and the message model. On the one hand, APFuzz employs a two-stage process of static and dynamic analysis to automatically identify state variables, which are then used to infer an accurate state model during fuzzing. On the other hand, APFuzz introduces field-level mutation operations for binary protocols, leveraging message structure awareness enabled by Large Language Models. We conduct extensive experiments on a public protocol fuzzing benchmark, comparing APFuzz with the baseline fuzzer AFLNET as well as several state-of-the-art greybox protocol fuzzers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21887","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21887","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21887","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changjiang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Changjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaichen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ExpLang: Improved Exploration and Exploitation in LLM Reasoning with On-Policy Thinking Language Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:50:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current large reasoning models (LRMs) have shown strong ability on challenging tasks after reinforcement learning (RL) based post-training. However, previous work mainly focuses on English reasoning in expectation of the strongest performance, despite the demonstrated potential advantage of multilingual thinking, as well as the requirement for native thinking traces by global users. In this paper, we propose ExpLang, a novel LLM post-training pipeline that enables on-policy thinking language selection to improve exploration and exploitation during RL with the use of multiple languages. The results show that our method steadily outperforms English-only training with the same training budget, while showing high thinking language compliance for both seen and unseen languages. Analysis shows that, by enabling on-policy thinking language selection as an action during RL, ExpLang effectively extends the RL exploration space with diversified language preference and improves the RL exploitation outcome with leveraged non-English advantage. The method is orthogonal to most RL algorithms and opens up a new perspective on using multilinguality to improve LRMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21887","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GFPL: Generative Federated Prototype Learning for Resource-Constrained and Data-Imbalanced Vision Task"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:57:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning (FL) facilitates the secure utilization of decentralized images, advancing applications in medical image recognition and autonomous driving. However, conventional FL faces two critical challenges in real-world deployment: ineffective knowledge fusion caused by model updates biased toward majority-class features, and prohibitive communication overhead due to frequent transmissions of high-dimensional model parameters. Inspired by the human brain's efficiency in knowledge integration, we propose a novel Generative Federated Prototype Learning (GFPL) framework to address these issues. Within this framework, a prototype generation method based on Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) captures the statistical information of class-wise features, while a prototype aggregation strategy using Bhattacharyya distance effectively fuses semantically similar knowledge across clients. In addition, these fused prototypes are leveraged to generate pseudo-features, thereby mitigating feature distribution imbalance across clients. To further enhance feature alignment during local training, we devise a dual-classifier architecture, optimized via a hybrid loss combining Dot Regression and Cross-Entropy. Extensive experiments on benchmarks show that GFPL improves model accuracy by 3.6% under imbalanced data settings while maintaining low communication cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yanbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangyue","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiangyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Kwok","name":"Kwok, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:45:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have emerged as versatile solutions for zero-shot question answering (QA) across various domains. However, enabling VLMs to effectively comprehend structured graphs and perform accurate, efficient QA remains challenging. Existing approaches typically rely on one single graph topology representation (GTR), such as fixed-style visual images or unified text descriptions. This ``one-size-fits-all'' strategy often neglects model-specific and task-specific preferences, resulting in inaccurate or over-lengthy responses to graph-related queries. To address this, we propose the $\\mbox{DynamicGTR}$ framework, which dynamically selects the optimal GTR for each query during inference, thereby enhancing the zero-shot graph QA capabilities of VLMs with a customizable accuracy and brevity trade-off. Extensive experiments show that DynamicGTR not only improves VLM-based graph algorithm QA performance but also successfully transfers the experience trained from synthetic graph algorithm tasks to real-world applications like link prediction and node classification, without any additional training. Additionally, DynamicGTR demonstrates strong transferability across tasks, domains, and models, suggesting its potential as a flexible solution for broad graph scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia Cheng","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Chia Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Zi","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, An-Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hen-Hsen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hen-Hsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Hsi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hsin-Hsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personalized Graph-Empowered Large Language Model for Proactive Information Access"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:43:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Since individuals may struggle to recall all life details and often confuse events, establishing a system to assist users in recalling forgotten experiences is essential. While numerous studies have proposed memory recall systems, these primarily rely on deep learning techniques that require extensive training and often face data scarcity due to the limited availability of personal lifelogs. As lifelogs grow over time, systems must also adapt quickly to newly accumulated data. Recently, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across various tasks, making them promising for personalized applications. In this work, we present a framework that leverages LLMs for proactive information access, integrating personal knowledge graphs to enhance the detection of access needs through a refined decision-making process. Our framework offers high flexibility, enabling the replacement of base models and the modification of fact retrieval methods for continuous improvement. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach effectively identifies forgotten events, supporting users in recalling past experiences more efficiently."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jabez","familyName":"Magomere","name":"Magomere, Jabez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Kochkina","name":"Kochkina, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Mensah","name":"Mensah, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simerjot","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Simerjot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"Acero","name":"Acero, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arturo","familyName":"Oncevay","name":"Oncevay, Arturo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charese H.","familyName":"Smiley","name":"Smiley, Charese H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distill and Align Decomposition for Enhanced Claim Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:32:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Complex claim verification requires decomposing sentences into verifiable subclaims, yet existing methods struggle to align decomposition quality with verification performance. We propose a reinforcement learning (RL) approach that jointly optimizes decomposition quality and verifier alignment using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Our method integrates: (i) structured sequential reasoning; (ii) supervised finetuning on teacher-distilled exemplars; and (iii) a multi-objective reward balancing format compliance, verifier alignment, and decomposition quality. Across six evaluation settings, our trained 8B decomposer improves downstream verification performance to (71.75%) macro-F1, outperforming prompt-based approaches ((+1.99), (+6.24)) and existing RL methods ((+5.84)). Human evaluation confirms the high quality of the generated subclaims. Our framework enables smaller language models to achieve state-of-the-art claim verification by jointly optimising for verification accuracy and decomposition quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL Findings 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21854","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21854","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21854","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustafa","familyName":"Dogan","name":"Dogan, Mustafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilker","familyName":"Kesen","name":"Kesen, Ilker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iacer","familyName":"Calixto","name":"Calixto, Iacer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aykut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Aykut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erkut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Erkut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FewMMBench: A Benchmark for Multimodal Few-Shot Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As multimodal large language models (MLLMs) advance in handling interleaved image-text data, assessing their few-shot learning capabilities remains an open challenge. In this paper, we introduce FewMMBench, a comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate MLLMs under few-shot conditions, with a focus on In-Context Learning (ICL) and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting. Covering a diverse suite of multimodal understanding tasks, from attribute recognition to temporal reasoning, FewMMBench enables systematic analysis across task types, model families, and prompting strategies. We evaluate 26 open-weight MLLMs from six model families across zero-shot, few-shot, and CoT-augmented few-shot settings. Our findings reveal that instruction-tuned models exhibit strong zero-shot performance but benefit minimally, or even regress, with additional demonstrations or CoT reasoning. Retrieval-based demonstrations and increased context size also yield limited gains. These results highlight FewMMBench as a rigorous testbed for diagnosing and advancing few-shot capabilities in multimodal LLMs. The data is available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/mustafaa/FewMMBench"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. 49 pages, 38 Figures, 5 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21854","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanxiao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Hanxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LightSim: A Lightweight Cell Transmission Model Simulator for Traffic Signal Control Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:29:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning for traffic signal control is bottlenecked by simulators: training in SUMO takes hours, reproducing results often requires days of platform-specific setup, and the slow iteration cycle discourages the multi-seed experiments that rigorous evaluation demands. Much of this cost is unnecessary, since for signal timing optimization the relevant dynamics are queue formation and discharge, which the Cell Transmission Model (CTM) captures as a macroscopic flow model.\n We introduce LightSim, a pure Python, pip-installable traffic simulator with Gymnasium and PettingZoo interfaces that runs over 20000 steps per second on a single CPU. Across cross-simulator experiments spanning single intersections, grid networks, arterial corridors, and six real-world city networks, LightSim preserves controller rankings from SUMO for both classical and reinforcement learning strategies while training 3 to 7 times faster. LightSim is released as an open-source benchmark with nineteen built-in scenarios, seven controllers, and full reinforcement learning pipelines, lowering the barrier to signal control research from days to minutes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengcheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunpeng","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Chunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Meta-FC: Meta-Learning with Feature Consistency for Robust and Generalizable Watermarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:26:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning-based watermarking has made remarkable progress in recent years. To achieve robustness against various distortions, current methods commonly adopt a training strategy where a \\underline{\\textbf{s}}ingle \\underline{\\textbf{r}}andom \\underline{\\textbf{d}}istortion (SRD) is chosen as the noise layer in each training batch. However, the SRD strategy treats distortions independently within each batch, neglecting the inherent relationships among different types of distortions and causing optimization conflicts across batches. As a result, the robustness and generalizability of the watermarking model are limited. To address this issue, we propose a novel training strategy that enhances robustness and generalization via \\underline{\\textbf{meta}}-learning with \\underline{\\textbf{f}}eature \\underline{\\textbf{c}}onsistency (Meta-FC). Specifically, we randomly sample multiple distortions from the noise pool to construct a meta-training task, while holding out one distortion as a simulated ``unknown'' distortion for the meta-testing phase. Through meta-learning, the model is encouraged to identify and utilize neurons that exhibit stable activations across different types of distortions, mitigating the optimization conflicts caused by the random sampling of diverse distortions in each batch. To further promote the transformation of stable activations into distortion-invariant representations, we introduce a feature consistency loss that constrains the decoded features of the same image subjected to different distortions to remain consistent. Extensive experiments demonstrate that, compared to the SRD training strategy, Meta-FC improves the robustness and generalization of various watermarking models by an average of 1.59\\%, 4.71\\%, and 2.38\\% under high-intensity, combined, and unknown distortions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masha","familyName":"Naslidnyk","name":"Naslidnyk, Masha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Kernel-Based Distances for Statistical Inference and Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:25:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Representing, comparing, and measuring the distance between probability distributions is a key task in computational statistics and machine learning. The choice of representation and the associated distance determine properties of the methods in which they are used: for example, certain distances can allow one to encode robustness or smoothness of the problem. Kernel methods offer flexible and rich Hilbert space representations of distributions that allow the modeller to enforce properties through the choice of kernel, and estimate associated distances at efficient nonparametric rates. In particular, the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD), a kernel-based distance constructed by comparing Hilbert space mean functions, has received significant attention due to its computational tractability and is favoured by practitioners.\n In this thesis, we conduct a thorough study of kernel-based distances with a focus on efficient computation, with core contributions in Chapters 3 to 6. Part I of the thesis is focused on the MMD, specifically on improved MMD estimation. In Chapter 3 we propose a theoretically sound, improved estimator for MMD in simulation-based inference. Then, in Chapter 4, we propose an MMD-based estimator for conditional expectations, a ubiquitous task in statistical computation. Closing Part I, in Chapter 5 we study the problem of calibration when MMD is applied to the task of integration.\n In Part II, motivated by the recent developments in kernel embeddings beyond the mean, we introduce a family of novel kernel-based discrepancies: kernel quantile discrepancies. These address some of the pitfalls of MMD, and are shown through both theoretical results and an empirical study to offer a competitive alternative to MMD and its fast approximations. We conclude with a discussion on broader lessons and future work emerging from the thesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"PhD thesis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Jun Angela","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ying-Jun Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JSAM: Privacy Straggler-Resilient Joint Client Selection and Incentive Mechanism Design in Differentially Private Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Differentially private federated learning faces a fundamental tension: privacy protection mechanisms that safeguard client data simultaneously create quantifiable privacy costs that discourage participation, undermining the collaborative training process. Existing incentive mechanisms rely on unbiased client selection, forcing servers to compensate even the most privacy-sensitive clients (\"privacy stragglers\"), leading to systemic inefficiency and suboptimal resource allocation. We introduce JSAM (Joint client Selection and privacy compensAtion Mechanism), a Bayesian-optimal framework that simultaneously optimizes client selection probabilities and privacy compensation to maximize training effectiveness under budget constraints. Our approach transforms a complex 2N-dimensional optimization problem into an efficient three-dimensional formulation through novel theoretical characterization of optimal selection strategies. We prove that servers should preferentially select privacy-tolerant clients while excluding high-sensitivity participants, and uncover the counter-intuitive insight that clients with minimal privacy sensitivity may incur the highest cumulative costs due to frequent participation. Extensive evaluations on MNIST and CIFAR-10 demonstrate that JSAM achieves up to 15% improvement in test accuracy compared to existing unbiased selection mechanisms while maintaining cost efficiency across varying data heterogeneity levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"García-Márquez","name":"García-Márquez, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuria","familyName":"Rodríguez-Barroso","name":"Rodríguez-Barroso, Nuria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Victoria","familyName":"Luzón","name":"Luzón, M. Victoria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Herrera","name":"Herrera, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resilient Federated Chain: Transforming Blockchain Consensus into an Active Defense Layer for Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:20:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Learning (FL) has emerged as a key paradigm for building Trustworthy AI systems by enabling privacy-preserving, decentralized model training. However, FL is highly susceptible to adversarial attacks that compromise model integrity and data confidentiality, a vulnerability exacerbated by the fact that conventional data inspection methods are incompatible with its decentralized design. While integrating FL with Blockchain technology has been proposed to address some limitations, its potential for mitigating adversarial attacks remains largely unexplored. This paper introduces Resilient Federated Chain (RFC), a novel blockchain-enabled FL framework designed specifically to enhance resilience against such threats. RFC builds upon the existing Proof of Federated Learning architecture by repurposing the redundancy of its Pooled Mining mechanism as an active defense layer that can be combined with robust aggregation rules. Furthermore, the framework introduces a flexible evaluation function in its consensus mechanism, allowing for adaptive defense against different attack strategies. Extensive experimental evaluation on image classification tasks under various adversarial scenarios, demonstrates that RFC significantly improves robustness compared to baseline methods, providing a viable solution for securing decentralized learning environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norman","familyName":"Peitek","name":"Peitek, Norman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Hess","name":"Hess, Julia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Apel","name":"Apel, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Restructuring to Stabilization: A Large-Scale Experiment on Iterative Code Readability Refactoring with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:05:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:47:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for automated code refactoring tasks. Although these models can quickly refactor code, the quality may exhibit inconsistencies and unpredictable behavior. In this article, we systematically study the capabilities of LLMs for code refactoring with a specific focus on improving code readability.\n We conducted a large-scale experiment using GPT5.1 with 230 Java snippets, each systematically varied and refactored regarding code readability across five iterations under three different prompting strategies. We categorized fine-grained code changes during the refactoring into implementation, syntactic, and comment-level transformations. Subsequently, we investigated the functional correctness and tested the robustness of the results with novel snippets.\n Our results reveal three main insights: First, iterative code refactoring exhibits an initial phase of restructuring followed by stabilization. This convergence tendency suggests that LLMs possess an internalized understanding of an \"optimally readable\" version of code. Second, convergence patterns are fairly robust across different code variants. Third, explicit prompting toward specific readability factors slightly influences the refactoring dynamics.\n These insights provide an empirical foundation for assessing the reliability of LLM-assisted code refactoring, which opens pathways for future research, including comparative analyses across models and a systematic evaluation of additional software quality dimensions in LLM-refactored code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Lamott","name":"Lamott, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saifullah","familyName":"Saifullah","name":"Saifullah, Saifullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nauman","familyName":"Riaz","name":"Riaz, Nauman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yves-Noel","familyName":"Weweler","name":"Weweler, Yves-Noel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Alt-Veit","name":"Alt-Veit, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad Sarmad","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Ahmad Sarmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Armaghan","familyName":"Shakir","name":"Shakir, Muhammad Armaghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Kalwa","name":"Kalwa, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Momina","familyName":"Moetesum","name":"Moetesum, Momina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Dengel","name":"Dengel, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheraz","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Sheraz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Shafait","name":"Shafait, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulrich","familyName":"Schwanecke","name":"Schwanecke, Ulrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Ulges","name":"Ulges, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DocDjinn: Controllable Synthetic Document Generation with VLMs and Handwriting Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:52:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:47:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective document intelligence models rely on large amounts of annotated training data. However, procuring sufficient and high-quality data poses significant challenges due to the labor-intensive and costly nature of data acquisition. Additionally, leveraging language models to annotate real documents raises concerns about data privacy. Synthetic document generation has emerged as a promising, privacy-preserving alternative. We propose DocDjinn, a novel framework for controllable synthetic document generation using Vision-Language Models (VLMs) that produces annotated documents from unlabeled seed samples. Our approach generates visually plausible and semantically consistent synthetic documents that follow the distribution of an existing source dataset through clustering-based seed selection with parametrized sampling. By enriching documents with realistic diffusion-based handwriting and contextual visual elements via semantic-visual decoupling, we generate diverse, high-quality annotated synthetic documents. We evaluate across eleven benchmarks spanning key information extraction, question answering, document classification, and document layout analysis. To our knowledge, this is the first work demonstrating that VLMs can generate faithful annotated document datasets at scale from unlabeled seeds that can effectively enrich or approximate real, manually annotated data for diverse document understanding tasks. We show that with only 100 real training samples, our framework achieves on average $87\\%$ of the performance of the full real-world dataset. We publicly release our code and 140k+ synthetic document samples."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21811","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21811","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21811","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingtao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengnan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhengnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaofeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DexRepNet++: Learning Dexterous Robotic Manipulation with Geometric and Spatial Hand-Object Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tro.2026.3651669","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic dexterous manipulation is a challenging problem due to high degrees of freedom (DoFs) and complex contacts of multi-fingered robotic hands. Many existing deep reinforcement learning (DRL) based methods aim at improving sample efficiency in high-dimensional output action spaces. However, existing works often overlook the role of representations in achieving generalization of a manipulation policy in the complex input space during the hand-object interaction. In this paper, we propose DexRep, a novel hand-object interaction representation to capture object surface features and spatial relations between hands and objects for dexterous manipulation skill learning. Based on DexRep, policies are learned for three dexterous manipulation tasks, i.e. grasping, in-hand reorientation, bimanual handover, and extensive experiments are conducted to verify the effectiveness. In simulation, for grasping, the policy learned with 40 objects achieves a success rate of 87.9% on more than 5000 unseen objects of diverse categories, significantly surpassing existing work trained with thousands of objects; for the in-hand reorientation and handover tasks, the policies also boost the success rates and other metrics of existing hand-object representations by 20% to 40%. The grasp policies with DexRep are deployed to the real world under multi-camera and single-camera setups and demonstrate a small sim-to-real gap."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Transactions on Robotics (T-RO), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21811","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21810","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21810","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21810","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiankang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiankang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peile","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Peile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Dongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoMotion: Rethinking Motion Segmentation via Latent 4D Geometry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motion segmentation in dynamic scenes is highly challenging, as conventional methods heavily rely on estimating camera poses and point correspondences from inherently noisy motion cues. Existing statistical inference or iterative optimization techniques that struggle to mitigate the cumulative errors in multi-stage pipelines often lead to limited performance or high computational cost. In contrast, we propose a fully learning-based approach that directly infers moving objects from latent feature representations via attention mechanisms, thus enabling end-to-end feed-forward motion segmentation. Our key insight is to bypass explicit correspondence estimation and instead let the model learn to implicitly disentangle object and camera motion. Supported by recent advances in 4D scene geometry reconstruction (e.g., $π^3$), the proposed method leverages reliable camera poses and rich spatial-temporal priors, which ensure stable training and robust inference for the model. Extensive experiments demonstrate that by eliminating complex pre-processing and iterative refinement, our approach achieves state-of-the-art motion segmentation performance with high efficiency. The code is available at:https://github.com/zjutcvg/GeoMotion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21810","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhusudan","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Madhusudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evaluation of Context Length Extrapolation in Long Code via Positional Embeddings and Efficient Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:27:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has led to a significant increase in automated tools in the software engineering, capable of performing various code-related tasks such as code generation, completion, and translation. Despite these advancements, its effectiveness is constrained by fixed context lengths, limiting its ability to generalize across long, domain-specific code sequences. To address this challenge, we investigate zero-shot, inference-only methods aimed at improving position encodings and optimizing attention mechanisms. Our goal is to provide a thorough analysis of current approaches that facilitate context length extrapolation in code, particularly in the context of long code completion tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagi","familyName":"Shaier","name":"Shaier, Sagi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Excitation: Momentum For Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Excitation, a novel optimization framework designed to accelerate learning in sparse architectures such as Mixture-of-Experts (MoEs). Unlike traditional optimizers that treat all parameters uniformly, Excitation dynamically modulates updates using batch-level expert utilization. It introduces a competitive update dynamic that amplifies updates to highly-utilized experts and can selectively suppress low-utilization ones, effectively sharpening routing specialization. Notably, we identify a phenomenon of \"structural confusion\" in deep MoEs, where standard optimizers fail to establish functional signal paths; Excitation acts as a specialization catalyst, \"rescuing\" these models and enabling stable training where baselines remain trapped. Excitation is optimizer-, domain-, and model-agnostic, requires minimal integration effort, and introduces neither additional per-parameter optimizer state nor learnable parameters, making it highly viable for memory-constrained settings. Across language and vision tasks, Excitation consistently improves convergence speed and final performance in MoE models, indicating that active update modulation is a key mechanism for effective conditional computation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunsuke","familyName":"Ubukata","name":"Ubukata, Shunsuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D-COT: Disciplined Chain-of-Thought Learning for Efficient Reasoning in Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7, I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:08:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-Thought (CoT) distillation from Large Language Models (LLMs) often induces \"overthinking\" in Small Language Models (SLMs), leading to performance degradation and excessive token consumption. In this study, we propose Disciplined Chain-of-Thought (D-CoT), a novel framework that enforces a structured reasoning process using control tags -- such as for fact-checking and for multi-perspective exploration -- as auxiliary scaffolding during training. By optimizing the CoT trajectory, D-CoT suppresses reasoning drift and simultaneously achieves token reduction and performance improvement. We demonstrate the efficacy of our approach on Qwen3-8B: with only 5,000 training samples, D-CoT significantly boosts accuracy on GPQA-diamond by 9.9% and MMLU-Pro (0-shot) by 9.1%, while drastically reducing computational costs. Furthermore, we confirm that the model internalizes this disciplined thought structure, maintaining high performance even without explicit control tags during inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures. Code: https://github.com/gitpullpull/DisciplinedChainOfThought | Benchmarks: https://huggingface.co/datasets/gitpullpull/D-CoT-Benchmarks | Dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/gitpullpull/D-CoT-datasets"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunhai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zunhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Hansen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dahai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XStreamVGGT: Extremely Memory-Efficient Streaming Vision Geometry Grounded Transformer with KV Cache Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:02:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning-based 3D visual geometry models have significantly advanced with the advent of large-scale transformers. Among these, StreamVGGT leverages frame-wise causal attention to deliver robust and efficient streaming 3D reconstruction. However, it suffers from unbounded growth in the Key-Value (KV) cache due to the massive influx of vision tokens from multi-image and long-video inputs, leading to increased memory consumption and inference latency as input frames accumulate. This ultimately limits its scalability for long-horizon applications. To address this gap, we propose XStreamVGGT, a tuning-free approach that seamlessly integrates pruning and quantization to systematically compress the KV cache, enabling extremely memory-efficient streaming inference. Specifically, redundant KVs generated from multi-frame inputs are initially pruned to conform to a fixed KV memory budget using an efficient token-importance identification mechanism that maintains full compatibility with high-performance attention kernels (e.g., FlashAttention). Additionally, leveraging the inherent distribution patterns of KV tensors, we apply dimension-adaptive KV quantization within the pruning pipeline to further minimize memory overhead while preserving numerical accuracy. Extensive evaluations show that XStreamVGGT achieves mostly negligible performance degradation while substantially reducing memory usage by 4.42$\\times$ and accelerating inference by 5.48$\\times$, enabling practical and scalable streaming 3D applications. The code is available at https://github.com/ywh187/XStreamVGGT/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submission to the Journal of the Society for Information Display"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21779","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21779","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21779","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yusong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaowei","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Artifacts: A Forensic Benchmark for Video Deepfake Reasoning in Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:54:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:43:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for deepfake detection excel at identifying spatial artifacts but overlook a critical dimension: temporal inconsistencies in video forgeries. Adapting VLMs to reason about these dynamic cues remains a distinct challenge. To bridge this gap, we propose Forensic Answer-Questioning (FAQ), a large-scale benchmark that formulates temporal deepfake analysis as a multiple-choice task. FAQ introduces a three-level hierarchy to progressively evaluate and equip VLMs with forensic capabilities: (1) Facial Perception, testing the ability to identify static visual artifacts; (2) Temporal Deepfake Grounding, requiring the localization of dynamic forgery artifacts across frames; and (3) Forensic Reasoning, challenging models to synthesize evidence for final authenticity verdicts. We evaluate a range of VLMs on FAQ and generate a corresponding instruction-tuning set, FAQ-IT. Extensive experiments show that models fine-tuned on FAQ-IT achieve advanced performance on both in-domain and cross-dataset detection benchmarks. Ablation studies further validate the impact of our key design choices, confirming that FAQ is the driving force behind the temporal reasoning capabilities of these VLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 9 figures. Submitted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21779","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenton","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Kenton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengxiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Fengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalisation of RLHF under Reward Shift and Clipped KL Regularisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:42:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Alignment and adaptation in large language models heavily rely on reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF); yet, theoretical understanding of its generalisability remains premature, especially when the learned reward could shift, and the KL control is estimated and clipped. To address this issue, we develop generalisation theory for RLHF that explicitly accounts for (1) \\emph{reward shift}: reward models are trained on preference data from earlier or mixed behaviour policies while RLHF optimises the current policy on its own rollouts; and (2) \\emph{clipped KL regularisation}: the KL regulariser is estimated from sampled log-probability ratios and then clipped for stabilisation, resulting in an error to RLHF. We present generalisation bounds for RLHF, suggesting that the generalisation error stems from a sampling error from prompts and rollouts, a reward shift error, and a KL clipping error. We also discuss special cases of (1) initialising RLHF parameters with a uniform prior over a finite space, and (2) training RLHF by stochastic gradient descent, as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process. The theory yields practical implications in (1) optimal KL clipping threshold, and (2) budget allocation in prompts, rollouts, and preference data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changxing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Changxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Implicit Discourse Relation Recognition with Natural Language Explanations from LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:28:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit Discourse Relation Recognition (IDRR) remains a challenging task due to the requirement for deep semantic understanding in the absence of explicit discourse markers. A further limitation is that existing methods only predict relations without providing any supporting explanations. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have shown strong reasoning capabilities in both deep language understanding and natural language explanation generation. In this work, we propose a simple yet effective approach to distill the reasoning capabilities of LLMs into lightweight IDRR models to improve both performance and interpretability. Specifically, we first prompt an LLM to generate explanations for each training instance conditioned on its gold label. Then, we introduce a novel classification-generation framework that jointly performs relation prediction and explanation generation, and train it with the additional supervision of LLM-generated explanations. Our framework is plug-and-play, enabling easy integration with most existing IDRR models. Experimental results on PDTB demonstrate that our approach significantly improves IDRR performance, while human evaluation further confirms that the generated explanations enhance model interpretability. Furthermore, we validate the generality of our approach on sentiment classification and natural language inference"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"AAAI26'0ral"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Euisoo","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Euisoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byunghyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seonghye","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Seonghye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae-Gil","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jae-Gil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating Diffusion via Hybrid Data-Pipeline Parallelism Based on Conditional Guidance Scheduling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:23:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved remarkable progress in high-fidelity image, video, and audio generation, yet inference remains computationally expensive. Nevertheless, current diffusion acceleration methods based on distributed parallelism suffer from noticeable generation artifacts and fail to achieve substantial acceleration proportional to the number of GPUs. Therefore, we propose a hybrid parallelism framework that combines a novel data parallel strategy, condition-based partitioning, with an optimal pipeline scheduling method, adaptive parallelism switching, to reduce generation latency and achieve high generation quality in conditional diffusion models. The key ideas are to (i) leverage the conditional and unconditional denoising paths as a new data-partitioning perspective and (ii) adaptively enable optimal pipeline parallelism according to the denoising discrepancy between these two paths. Our framework achieves $2.31\\times$ and $2.07\\times$ latency reductions on SDXL and SD3, respectively, using two NVIDIA RTX~3090 GPUs, while preserving image quality. This result confirms the generality of our approach across U-Net-based diffusion models and DiT-based flow-matching architectures. Our approach also outperforms existing methods in acceleration under high-resolution synthesis settings. Code is available at https://github.com/kaist-dmlab/Hybridiff."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:30Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21756","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21756","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21756","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deogyong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Deogyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junseong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jeongeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhoe","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changhoe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junguel","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungseok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jungseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongha","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Offline Reasoning for Efficient Recommendation: LLM-Empowered Persona-Profiled Item Indexing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:14:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities for recommender systems by capturing the nuanced semantics of user interests and item characteristics through rich semantic understanding and contextual reasoning. In particular, LLMs have been employed as rerankers that reorder candidate items based on inferred user-item relevance. However, these approaches often require expensive online inference-time reasoning, leading to high latency that hampers real-world deployment. In this work, we introduce Persona4Rec, a recommendation framework that performs offline reasoning to construct interpretable persona representations of items, enabling lightweight and scalable real-time inference. In the offline stage, Persona4Rec leverages LLMs to reason over item reviews, inferring diverse user motivations that explain why different types of users may engage with an item; these inferred motivations are materialized as persona representations, providing multiple, human-interpretable views of each item. Unlike conventional approaches that rely on a single item representation, Persona4Rec learns to align user profiles with the most plausible item-side persona through a dedicated encoder, effectively transforming user-item relevance into user-persona relevance. At the online stage, this persona-profiled item index allows fast relevance computation without invoking expensive LLM reasoning. Extensive experiments show that Persona4Rec achieves performance comparable to recent LLM-based rerankers while substantially reducing inference time. Moreover, qualitative analysis confirms that persona representations not only drive efficient scoring but also provide intuitive, review-grounded explanations. These results demonstrate that Persona4Rec offers a practical and interpretable solution for next-generation recommender systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21756","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longlong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Longlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangyi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RABot: Reinforcement-Guided Graph Augmentation for Imbalanced and Noisy Social Bot Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:02:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social bot detection is pivotal for safeguarding the integrity of online information ecosystems. Although recent graph neural network (GNN) solutions achieve strong results, they remain hindered by two practical challenges: (i) severe class imbalance arising from the high cost of generating bots, and (ii) topological noise introduced by bots that skillfully mimic human behavior and forge deceptive links. We propose the Reinforcement-guided graph Augmentation social Bot detector (RABot), a multi-granularity graph-augmentation framework that addresses both issues in a unified manner. RABot employs a neighborhood-aware oversampling strategy that linearly interpolates minority-class embeddings within local subgraphs, thereby stabilizing the decision boundary under low-resource regimes. Concurrently, a reinforcement-learning-driven edge-filtering module combines similarity-based edge features with adaptive threshold optimization to excise spurious interactions during message passing, yielding a cleaner topology. Extensive experiments on three real-world benchmarks and four GNN backbones demonstrate that RABot consistently surpasses state-of-the-art baselines. In addition, since its augmentation and filtering modules are orthogonal to the underlying architecture, RABot can be seamlessly integrated into existing GNN pipelines to boost performance with minimal overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21741","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21741","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21741","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MD. Sagor","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, MD. Sagor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adiba Fairooz","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Adiba Fairooz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Long-Form Bangla Speech Processing: Automatic Speech Recognition and Speaker Diarization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:52:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We describe our end-to-end system for Bengali long-form speech recognition (ASR) and speaker diarization submitted to the DL Sprint 4.0 competition on Kaggle. Bengali presents substantial challenges for both tasks: a large phoneme inventory, significant dialectal variation, frequent code-mixing with English, and a relative scarcity of large-scale labelled corpora. For ASR we achieve a best private Word Error Rate (WER) of 0.37738 and public WER of 0.36137, combining a BengaliAI fine-tuned Whisper medium model with Demucs source separation for vocal isolation, silence-boundary chunking, and carefully tuned generation hyperparameters. For speaker diarization we reach a best private Diarization Error Rate (DER) of 0.27671 and public DER of 0.20936 by replacing the default segmentation model inside the pyannote.audio pipeline with a Bengali-fine-tuned variant, pairing it with wespeaker-voxceleb-resnet34-LM embeddings and centroid-based agglomerative clustering. Our experiments demonstrate that domain-specific fine-tuning of the segmentation component, vocal source separation, and natural silence-aware chunking are the three most impactful design choices for low-resource Bengali speech processing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 5 figures, 3 tables; system paper submitted to DL Sprint 4.0 (Kaggle)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21741","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21736","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21736","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21736","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanpeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Haoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiweng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haiweng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sipeng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Sipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint-Aligned Latent Action: Towards Scalable VLA Pretraining in the Wild"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite progress, Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) are limited by a scarcity of large-scale, diverse robot data. While human manipulation videos offer a rich alternative, existing methods are forced to choose between small, precisely-labeled datasets and vast in-the-wild footage with unreliable hand tracking labels. We present JALA, a pretraining framework that learns Jointly-Aligned Latent Actions. JALA bypasses full visual dynamic reconstruction, instead learns a predictive action embedding aligned with both inverse dynamics and real actions. This yields a transition-aware, behavior-centric latent space for learning from heterogeneous human data. We scale this approach with UniHand-Mix, a 7.5M video corpus (\u0026gt;2,000 hours) blending laboratory and in-the-wild footage. Experiments demonstrate that JALA generates more realistic hand motions in both controlled and unconstrained scenarios, significantly improving downstream robot manipulation performance in both simulation and real-world tasks. These results indicate that jointly-aligned latent actions offer a scalable pathway for VLA pretraining from human data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21736","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadrien","familyName":"Reynaud","name":"Reynaud, Hadrien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim Ethem","familyName":"Hamamci","name":"Hamamci, Ibrahim Ethem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sezgin","familyName":"Er","name":"Er, Sezgin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suprosanna","familyName":"Shit","name":"Shit, Suprosanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bjoern","familyName":"Menze","name":"Menze, Bjoern","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Kainz","name":"Kainz, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SigVLP: Sigmoid Volume-Language Pre-Training for Self-Supervised CT-Volume Adaptive Representation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale, volumetric medical imaging datasets typically aggregate scans from different vendors and devices, resulting in highly variable resolution, slice thicknesses, and numbers of slices per study. Consequently, training representation models usually requires cropping or interpolating along the z-axis to obtain fixed-size blocks, which inevitably causes information loss. We propose a new training approach to overcome this limitation. Instead of absolute position embeddings, we interpret volumes as sequences of 3D chunks and adopt Rotary Position Embeddings, allowing us to treat the z-axis as an unconstrained temporal dimensions. Building on this idea, we introduce a new vision-language model: SigVLP. In SigVLP, we implement Rotary Position Embedding as the positional encoding method, which is applied directly within the attention operation, generating input-conditioned sine and cosine weights on the fly. This design ensures consistent alignment between query and key projections and adapts to any input sizes. To allow for variable input size during training, we sample Computed Tomography volumes in chunks and pair them with localized organ-wise textual observations. Compared to using entire reports for conditioning, chunkwise alignment provides finer-grained supervision, enabling the model to establish stronger correlations between the text and volume representations, thereby improving the precision of text-to-volume alignment. Our models are trained with the Muon optimizer and evaluated on a diverse set of downstream tasks, including zero-shot abnormality and organ classification, segmentation, and retrieval tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxi","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zhengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explore-on-Graph: Incentivizing Autonomous Exploration of Large Language Models on Knowledge Graphs with Path-refined Reward Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning process of Large Language Models (LLMs) is often plagued by hallucinations and missing facts in question-answering tasks. A promising solution is to ground LLMs' answers in verifiable knowledge sources, such as Knowledge Graphs (KGs). Prevailing KG-enhanced methods typically constrained LLM reasoning either by enforcing rules during generation or by imitating paths from a fixed set of demonstrations. However, they naturally confined the reasoning patterns of LLMs within the scope of prior experience or fine-tuning data, limiting their generalizability to out-of-distribution graph reasoning problems. To tackle this problem, in this paper, we propose Explore-on-Graph (EoG), a novel framework that encourages LLMs to autonomously explore a more diverse reasoning space on KGs. To incentivize exploration and discovery of novel reasoning paths, we propose to introduce reinforcement learning during training, whose reward is the correctness of the reasoning paths' final answers. To enhance the efficiency and meaningfulness of the exploration, we propose to incorporate path information as additional reward signals to refine the exploration process and reduce futile efforts. Extensive experiments on five KGQA benchmark datasets demonstrate that, to the best of our knowledge, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming not only open-source but also even closed-source LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yutang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieming","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jieming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoxiong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Baoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LessMimic: Long-Horizon Humanoid Interaction with Unified Distance Field Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:31:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humanoid robots that autonomously interact with physical environments over extended horizons represent a central goal of embodied intelligence. Existing approaches rely on reference motions or task-specific rewards, tightly coupling policies to particular object geometries and precluding multi-skill generalization within a single framework. A unified interaction representation enabling reference-free inference, geometric generalization, and long-horizon skill composition within one policy remains an open challenge. Here we show that Distance Field (DF) provides such a representation: LessMimic conditions a single whole-body policy on DF-derived geometric cues--surface distances, gradients, and velocity decompositions--removing the need for motion references, with interaction latents encoded via a Variational Auto-Encoder (VAE) and post-trained using Adversarial Interaction Priors (AIP) under Reinforcement Learning (RL). Through DAgger-style distillation that aligns DF latents with egocentric depth features, LessMimic further transfers seamlessly to vision-only deployment without motion capture (MoCap) infrastructure. A single LessMimic policy achieves 80--100% success across object scales from 0.4x to 1.6x on PickUp and SitStand where baselines degrade sharply, attains 62.1% success on 5 task instances trajectories, and remains viable up to 40 sequentially composed tasks. By grounding interaction in local geometry rather than demonstrations, LessMimic offers a scalable path toward humanoid robots that generalize, compose skills, and recover from failures in unstructured environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delio Jaramillo","familyName":"Velez","name":"Velez, Delio Jaramillo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gergely","familyName":"Biczok","name":"Biczok, Gergely","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre Graell i","familyName":"Amat","name":"Amat, Alexandre Graell i","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Ostman","name":"Ostman, Johan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balazs","familyName":"Pejo","name":"Pejo, Balazs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Private and Robust Contribution Evaluation in Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:27:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-silo federated learning allows multiple organizations to collaboratively train machine learning models without sharing raw data, but client updates can still leak sensitive information through inference attacks. Secure aggregation protects privacy by hiding individual updates, yet it complicates contribution evaluation, which is critical for fair rewards and detecting low-quality or malicious participants. Existing marginal-contribution methods, such as the Shapley value, are incompatible with secure aggregation, and practical alternatives, such as Leave-One-Out, are crude and rely on self-evaluation.\n We introduce two marginal-difference contribution scores compatible with secure aggregation. Fair-Private satisfies standard fairness axioms, while Everybody-Else eliminates self-evaluation and provides resistance to manipulation, addressing a largely overlooked vulnerability. We provide theoretical guarantees for fairness, privacy, robustness, and computational efficiency, and evaluate our methods on multiple medical image datasets and CIFAR10 in cross-silo settings. Our scores consistently outperform existing baselines, better approximate Shapley-induced client rankings, and improve downstream model performance as well as misbehavior detection. These results demonstrate that fairness, privacy, robustness, and practical utility can be achieved jointly in federated contribution evaluation, offering a principled solution for real-world cross-silo deployments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijia","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuemin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xuemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"C$^{2}$TC: A Training-Free Framework for Efficient Tabular Data Condensation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:25:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tabular data is the primary data format in industrial relational databases, underpinning modern data analytics and decision-making. However, the increasing scale of tabular data poses significant computational and storage challenges to learning-based analytical systems. This highlights the need for data-efficient learning, which enables effective model training and generalization using substantially fewer samples. Dataset condensation (DC) has emerged as a promising data-centric paradigm that synthesizes small yet informative datasets to preserve data utility while reducing storage and training costs. However, existing DC methods are computationally intensive due to reliance on complex gradient-based optimization. Moreover, they often overlook key characteristics of tabular data, such as heterogeneous features and class imbalance. To address these limitations, we introduce C$^{2}$TC (Class-Adaptive Clustering for Tabular Condensation), the first training-free tabular dataset condensation framework that jointly optimizes class allocation and feature representation, enabling efficient and scalable condensation. Specifically, we reformulate the dataset condensation objective into a novel class-adaptive cluster allocation problem (CCAP), which eliminates costly training and integrates adaptive label allocation to handle class imbalance. To solve the NP-hard CCAP, we develop HFILS, a heuristic local search that alternates between soft allocation and class-wise clustering to efficiently obtain high-quality solutions. Moreover, a hybrid categorical feature encoding (HCFE) is proposed for semantics-preserving clustering of heterogeneous discrete attributes. Extensive experiments on 10 real-world datasets demonstrate that C$^{2}$TC improves efficiency by at least 2 orders of magnitude over state-of-the-art baselines, while achieving superior downstream performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21716","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21716","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21716","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuge","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangtao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouhong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Shouhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TranX-Adapter: Bridging Artifacts and Semantics within MLLMs for Robust AI-generated Image Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rapid advances in AI-generated image (AIGI) technology enable highly realistic synthesis, threatening public information integrity and security. Recent studies have demonstrated that incorporating texture-level artifact features alongside semantic features into multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can enhance their AIGI detection capability. However, our preliminary analyses reveal that artifact features exhibit high intra-feature similarity, leading to an almost uniform attention map after the softmax operation. This phenomenon causes attention dilution, thereby hindering effective fusion between semantic and artifact features. To overcome this limitation, we propose a lightweight fusion adapter, TranX-Adapter, which integrates a Task-aware Optimal-Transport Fusion that leverages the Jensen-Shannon divergence between artifact and semantic prediction probabilities as a cost matrix to transfer artifact information into semantic features, and an X-Fusion that employs cross-attention to transfer semantic information into artifact features. Experiments on standard AIGI detection benchmarks upon several advanced MLLMs, show that our TranX-Adapter brings consistent and significant improvements (up to +6% accuracy)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21716","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two-Stage Active Distribution Network Voltage Control via LLM-RL Collaboration: A Hybrid Knowledge-Data-Driven Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing integration of distributed photovoltaics (PVs) into active distribution networks (ADNs) has exacerbated operational challenges, making it imperative to coordinate diverse equipment to mitigate voltage violations and enhance power quality. Although existing data-driven approaches have demonstrated effectiveness in the voltage control problem, they often require extensive trial-and-error exploration and struggle to incorporate heterogeneous information, such as day-ahead forecasts and semantic-based grid codes. Considering the operational scenarios and requirements in real-world ADNs, in this paper, we propose a hybrid knowledge-data-driven approach that leverages dynamic collaboration between a large language model (LLM) agent and a reinforcement learning (RL) agent to achieve two-stage voltage control. In the day-ahead stage, the LLM agent receives coarse region-level forecasts and generates scheduling strategies for on-load tap changer (OLTC) and shunt capacitors (SCs) to regulate the overall voltage profile. Then in the intra-day stage, based on accurate node-level measurements, the RL agent refines terminal voltages by deriving reactive power generation strategies for PV inverters. On top of the LLM-RL collaboration framework, we further propose a self-evolution mechanism for the LLM agent and a pretrain-finetune pipeline for the RL agent, effectively enhancing and coordinating the policies for both agents. The proposed approach not only aligns more closely with practical operational characteristics but also effectively utilizes the inherent knowledge and reasoning capabilities of the LLM agent, significantly improving training efficiency and voltage control performance. Comprehensive comparisons and ablation studies demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Liqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaocheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhaocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teddy","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Teddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunuo","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yunuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Innovative Tooth Segmentation Using Hierarchical Features and Bidirectional Sequence Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:20:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.patcog.2026.113045","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tooth image segmentation is a cornerstone of dental digitization. However, traditional image encoders relying on fixed-resolution feature maps often lead to discontinuous segmentation and poor discrimination between target regions and background, due to insufficient modeling of environmental and global context. Moreover, transformer-based self-attention introduces substantial computational overhead because of its quadratic complexity (O(n^2)), making it inefficient for high-resolution dental images. To address these challenges, we introduce a three-stage encoder with hierarchical feature representation to capture scale-adaptive information in dental images. By jointly leveraging low-level details and high-level semantics through cross-scale feature fusion, the model effectively preserves fine structural information while maintaining strong contextual awareness. Furthermore, a bidirectional sequence modeling strategy is incorporated to enhance global spatial context understanding without incurring high computational cost.\n We validate our method on two dental datasets, with experimental results demonstrating its superiority over existing approaches. On the OralVision dataset, our model achieves a 1.1% improvement in mean intersection over union (mIoU)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Pattern Recognition"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyi","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Guanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang Han","familyName":"Low","name":"Low, Chang Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuancan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yuancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunjiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SurGo-R1: Benchmarking and Modeling Contextual Reasoning for Operative Zone in Surgical Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:11:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Minimally invasive surgery has dramatically improved patient operative outcomes, yet identifying safe operative zones remains challenging in critical phases, requiring surgeons to integrate visual cues, procedural phase, and anatomical context under high cognitive load. Existing AI systems offer binary safety verification or static detection, ignoring the phase-dependent nature of intraoperative reasoning. We introduce ResGo, a benchmark of laparoscopic frames annotated with Go Zone bounding boxes and clinician-authored rationales covering phase, exposure quality reasoning, next action and risk reminder. We introduce evaluation metrics that treat correct grounding under incorrect phase as failures, revealing that most vision-language models cannot handle such tasks and perform poorly. We then present SurGo-R1, a model optimized via RLHF with a multi-turn phase-then-go architecture where the model first identifies the surgical phase, then generates reasoning and Go Zone coordinates conditioned on that context. On unseen procedures, SurGo-R1 achieves 76.6% phase accuracy, 32.7 mIoU, and 54.8% hardcore accuracy, a 6.6$\\times$ improvement over the mainstream generalist VLMs. Code, model and benchmark will be available at https://github.com/jinlab-imvr/SurGo-R1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jianghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kedi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjiao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xingjiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Multimodal Activation Steering for Hallucination Mitigation in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:10:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) exhibit outstanding performance on vision-language tasks but struggle with hallucination problems. Through in-depth analysis of LVLM activation patterns, we reveal two key findings: 1) truthfulness and visual perception capabilities predominantly engage different subsets of attention heads within the model architecture; and 2) truthfulness steering vectors vary significantly across different semantic contexts. Based on these observations, we propose Dynamic Multimodal Activation Steering, a training-free approach for hallucination mitigation. Our method constructs a semantic-based truthfulness steering vector database and computes visual perception steering vectors, enabling context-aware interventions during inference by dynamically selecting the most relevant steering vectors based on input semantic similarity and applying them to the most influential attention heads. We conduct comprehensive experiments across multiple models and datasets, demonstrating that our approach significantly enhances model performance, outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renshuang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Renshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yichong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Xiaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenling","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Zhenling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinglue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tinglue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AkiraRust: Re-thinking LLM-aided Rust Repair Using a Feedback-guided Thinking Switch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:34:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Eliminating undefined behaviors (UBs) in Rust programs requires a deep semantic understanding to enable accurate and reliable repair. While existing studies have demonstrated the potential of LLMs to support Rust code analysis and repair, most frameworks remain constrained by inflexible templates or lack grounding in executable semantics, resulting in limited contextual awareness and semantic incorrectness. Here, we present AkiraRust, an LLM-driven repair and verification framework that incorporates a finite-state machine to dynamically adapt its detection and repair flow to runtime semantic conditions. AkiraRust introduces a dual-mode reasoning strategy that coordinates fast and slow thinking across multiple agents. Each agent is mapped to an FSM state, and a waveform-driven transition controller manages state switching, rollback decisions, and semantic check pointing, enabling context-aware and runtime-adaptive repair. Experimental results show that AkiraRust achieves about 92% semantic correctness and delivers a 2.2x average speedup compared to SOTA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 11 figures, accepted to DAC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Eckel","name":"Eckel, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henri","familyName":"Meeß","name":"Meeß, Henri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical Lead Critic based Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:33:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) solves complex tasks that require coordination from multiple agents, but is often limited to either local (independent learning) or global (centralized learning) perspectives. In this paper, we introduce a novel sequential training scheme and MARL architecture, which learns from multiple perspectives on different hierarchy levels. We propose the Hierarchical Lead Critic (HLC) - inspired by natural emerging distributions in team structures, where following high-level objectives combines with low-level execution. HLC demonstrates that introducing multiple hierarchies, leveraging local and global perspectives, can lead to improved performance with high sample efficiency and robust policies. Experimental results conducted on cooperative, non-communicative, and partially observable MARL benchmarks demonstrate that HLC outperforms single hierarchy baselines and scales robustly with increasing amounts of agents and difficulty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 10 Figures, Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:29Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc Trung","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Duc Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pham Khanh","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Pham Khanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dat Phi","familyName":"Van","name":"Van, Dat Phi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh Ngo","familyName":"Van","name":"Van, Linh Ngo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sang","familyName":"Dinh","name":"Dinh, Sang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DWA-KD: Dual-Space Weighting and Time-Warped Alignment for Cross-Tokenizer Knowledge Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:04:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge Distillation (KD) has emerged as a crucial technique for compressing Large Language Models (LLMs). Although existing cross-tokenizer KD methods have made notable progress, their effectiveness remains constrained by suboptimal alignment across sequence and vocabulary levels. To address these limitations, we introduce Dual-Space Weighting and Time-Warped Alignment (DWA-KD), a novel cross-tokenizer distillation framework that enhances token-wise distillation through dual-space entropy-based weighting and achieves precise sequence-level alignment by leveraging both lexical and semantic information. At the token level, DWA-KD maps teacher representations into the student space and vice versa, performing dual-space KD via Kullback-Leibler divergence (KL). The process is modulated by dual-space weights that up-weight tokens where the student is uncertain and the teacher is confident, thereby focusing learning on informative tokens rather than treating all positions equally. At the sequence level, DWA-KD applies Soft Dynamic Time Warping (Soft-DTW) to both the embedding and final hidden-state layers, enabling robust alignment of lexical and contextual semantics between teacher and student sequences. Extensive experiments across diverse NLP benchmarks demonstrate that DWA-KD outperforms state-of-the-art KD baselines, while ablation studies confirm the complementary contributions of entropy-based token weighting and embedding and final hidden state layer Soft-DTW alignment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengtao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Hengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao-Kai","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Chao-Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Learning-based Low-Overhead Beam Alignment for mmWave Massive MIMO Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:52:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millimeter-wave massive multiple-input multiple-output systems employ highly directional beamforming to overcome severe path loss, and their performance critically depends on accurate beam alignment. Conventional codebook-based methods offer low training overhead but suffer from limited angular resolution and sensitivity to hardware impairments. To address these challenges, we propose a deep learning-enhanced super-resolution beam alignment framework with three key components. First, we design the Quaternary Search-based Super-Resolution (QSSR) algorithm, which leverages the monotonic power ratio property between two discrete Fourier transform (DFT) codebook beams to achieve super-resolution angle estimation without increasing measurement complexity relative to binary search. Second, we develop QSSR-Net, a gated recurrent unit-based neural network that exploits sequential multi-layer beam measurements to capture angular dependencies, thereby improving estimation accuracy, robustness to noise, and generalization across diverse propagation environments. Third, to mitigate the adverse effects of hardware impairments such as antenna position and phase errors, we propose a parametric self-calibration method that requires no additional hardware overhead and adapts compensation parameters in real time. Simulation results show that the proposed framework consistently outperforms binary search and even exhaustive search at high signal-to-noise ratios, achieving substantial performance gains while maintaining low overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 12 figures. This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steffen","familyName":"Grønneberg","name":"Grønneberg, Steffen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gudmund","familyName":"Hermansen","name":"Hermansen, Gudmund","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils Lid","familyName":"Hjort","name":"Hjort, Nils Lid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Estimation, inference and model selection for jump regression models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:42:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider regression models with data of the type $y_i=m(x_i)+\\varepsilon_i$, where the $m(x)$ curve is taken locally constant, with unknown levels and jump points. We investigate the large-sample properties of the minimum least squares estimators, finding in particular that jump point parameters and level parameters are estimated with respectively $n$-rate precision and $\\sqrt{n}$-rate precision, where $n$ is sample size. Bayes solutions are investigated as well and found to be superior. We then construct jump information criteria, respectively AJIC and BJIC, for selecting the right number of jump points from data. This is done by following the line of arguments that lead to the Akaike and Bayesian information criteria AIC and BIC, but which here lead to different formulae due to the different type of large-sample approximations involved."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 3 figures; Statistical Research Report, Department of Mathematics, University of Oslo, from June 2014, and arXiv'd February 2026. This paper constituted a part of the doctoral dissertations for respectively Gudmund Hermansen and Steffen Gr{\\o}nneberg. An extended and polished version will be written up for journal publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuting","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Shuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiling","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HybridINR-PCGC: Hybrid Lossless Point Cloud Geometry Compression Bridging Pretrained Model and Implicit Neural Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:42:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning-based point cloud compression presents superior performance to handcrafted codecs. However, pretrained-based methods, which are based on end-to-end training and expected to generalize to all the potential samples, suffer from training data dependency. Implicit neural representation (INR) based methods are distribution-agnostic and more robust, but they require time-consuming online training and suffer from the bitstream overhead from the overfitted model. To address these limitations, we propose HybridINR-PCGC, a novel hybrid framework that bridges the pretrained model and INR. Our framework retains distribution-agnostic properties while leveraging a pretrained network to accelerate convergence and reduce model overhead, which consists of two parts: the Pretrained Prior Network (PPN) and the Distribution Agnostic Refiner (DAR). We leverage the PPN, designed for fast inference and stable performance, to generate a robust prior for accelerating the DAR's convergence. The DAR is decomposed into a base layer and an enhancement layer, and only the enhancement layer needed to be packed into the bitstream. Finally, we propose a supervised model compression module to further supervise and minimize the bitrate of the enhancement layer parameters. Based on experiment results, HybridINR-PCGC achieves a significantly improved compression rate and encoding efficiency. Specifically, our method achieves a Bpp reduction of approximately 20.43% compared to G-PCC on 8iVFB. In the challenging out-of-distribution scenario Cat1B, our method achieves a Bpp reduction of approximately 57.85% compared to UniPCGC. And our method exhibits a superior time-rate trade-off, achieving an average Bpp reduction of 15.193% relative to the LINR-PCGC on 8iVFB."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shaoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Following the Diagnostic Trace: Visual Cognition-guided Cooperative Network for Chest X-Ray Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:35:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) has significantly advanced automated chest X-ray diagnosis but remains isolated from clinical workflows and lacks reliable decision support and interpretability. Human-AI collaboration seeks to enhance the reliability of diagnostic models by integrating the behaviors of controllable radiologists. However, the absence of interactive tools seamlessly embedded within diagnostic routines impedes collaboration, while the semantic gap between radiologists' decision-making patterns and model representations further limits clinical adoption. To overcome these limitations, we propose a visual cognition-guided collaborative network (VCC-Net) to achieve the cooperative diagnostic paradigm. VCC-Net centers on visual cognition (VC) and employs clinically compatible interfaces, such as eye-tracking or the mouse, to capture radiologists' visual search traces and attention patterns during diagnosis. VCC-Net employs VC as a spatial cognition guide, learning hierarchical visual search strategies to localize diagnostically key regions. A cognition-graph co-editing module subsequently integrates radiologist VC with model inference to construct a disease-aware graph. The module captures dependencies among anatomical regions and aligns model representations with VC-driven features, mitigating radiologist bias and facilitating complementary, transparent decision-making. Experiments on the public datasets SIIM-ACR, EGD-CXR, and self-constructed TB-Mouse dataset achieved classification accuracies of 88.40%, 85.05%, and 92.41%, respectively. The attention maps produced by VCC-Net exhibit strong concordance with radiologists' gaze distributions, demonstrating a mutual reinforcement of radiologist and model inference. The code is available at https://github.com/IPMI-NWU/VCC-Net."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hongwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangzhou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Score-Based Conditional Flow Models for MIMO Receiver Design with Superimposed Pilots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:29:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate channel state information (CSI) is vital for multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems. However, superimposed pilots (SIP), which reduce overhead, introduce severe pilot contamination and data interference, complicating joint channel estimation and data detection. This paper proposes a conditional flow matching receiver (CFM-Rx), an unsupervised generative framework that learns directly from received signals, eliminating the need for labeled data and improving adaptability across diverse system settings. By leveraging flow-based generative modeling, CFM-Rx enables deterministic, low-latency inference and exploits model invertibility to capture the bidirectional nature of signal propagation. This framework unifies flow matching with score-based diffusion modeling via a moment-consistent ordinary differential equation (ODE), replacing stochastic differential equation (SDE) sampling with a deterministic and efficient process. Furthermore, it integrates receiver-side priors to ensure stable, data-consistent inference. Extensive simulation results across various MIMO configurations demonstrate that CFM-Rx consistently outperforms conventional estimators and state-of-the-art data-driven receivers, achieving notable gains in channel estimation accuracy and symbol detection robustness, particularly under severe pilot contamination."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21653","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21653","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21653","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abas","familyName":"Bertina","name":"Bertina, Abas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Shakeri","name":"Shakeri, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Irresponsible Counselors: Large Language Models and the Loneliness of Modern Humans"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:29:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly shifted from peripheral assistive tools to constant companions in everyday and even high stakes human decision making. Many users now consult these models about health, intimate relationships, finance, education, and identity, because LLMs are, in practice, multi domain, inexpensive, always available, and seemingly nonjudgmental. At the same time, from a technical perspective these models rely on transformer architectures, exhibit highly unpredictable behavior in detail, and are fundamentally stateless; conceptually, they lack any real subjectivity, intention, or responsibility. This article argues that the combination of this technical architecture with the social position of LLMs as multis pecialist counselors in an age of human loneliness produces a new kind of advisory intimacy without a subject. In this new relation, model outputs are experienced as if they contained deep understanding, neutrality, emotional support, and user level control, while at the deeper level there is no human agent who is straightforwardly responsible or answerable. By reviewing dominant strands of AI ethics critique, we show that focusing only on developer liability, data bias, or emotional attachment to chatbots is insufficient to capture this configuration. We then explore the ethical and political implications of this advisory intimacy without a subject for policy-making, for justice in access to counseling, and for how we understand loneliness in the contemporary world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. XX pages, X figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21653","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Minhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuewen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mengjuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Qingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparsity Induction for Accurate Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated capabilities in text generation, while their increasing parameter scales present challenges in computational and memory efficiency. Post-training sparsity (PTS), which reduces model cost by removing weights from dense networks, is an effective approach. However, native dense matrices lack high sparsity, making existing approaches that directly remove weights disrupt model states, resulting in unsatisfactory performance recovery even with post-tuning. We propose Sparsity Induction, which promotes models toward higher sparsity at both distribution and feature levels before pruning, to push the limits of PTS. At the distribution level, we enhance distributional sparsity through mathematically equivalent scaling transformations, which are fully absorbable and incur no extra parameters or inference-time overhead. At the feature level, we introduce Spectral Norm Loss to promote feature sparsity from a low-rank perspective. Experiments across diverse model architectures and tasks demonstrate that our method further enhances sparsity-friendliness, achieving superior pruning performance over existing approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PPCR-IM: A System for Multi-layer DAG-based Public Policy Consequence Reasoning and Social Indicator Mapping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:23:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Public policy decisions are typically justified using a narrow set of headline indicators, leaving many downstream social impacts unstructured and difficult to compare across policies. We propose PPCR-IM, a system for multi-layer DAG-based consequence reasoning and social indicator mapping that addresses this gap. Given a policy description and its context, PPCR-IM uses an LLM-driven, layer-wise generator to construct a directed acyclic graph of intermediate consequences, allowing child nodes to have multiple parents to capture joint influences. A mapping module then aligns these nodes to a fixed indicator set and assigns one of three qualitative impact directions: increase, decrease, or ambiguous change. For each policy episode, the system outputs a structured record containing the DAG, indicator mappings, and three evaluation measures: an expected-indicator coverage score, a discovery rate for overlooked but relevant indicators, and a relative focus ratio comparing the systems coverage to that of the government. PPCR-IM is available both as an online demo and as a configurable XLSX-to-JSON batch pipeline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty Modeling for SysML v2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncertainty is inherent in modern engineered systems, including cyber-physical systems, autonomous systems, and large-scale software-intensive infrastructures (such as microservice-based systems) operating in dynamic and partially observable environments. The recent publication of Precise Semantics for Uncertainty Modeling (PSUM) by the Object Management Group represents the first standardized specification for uncertainty modeling within the Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) community, providing formally defined semantics for representing and reasoning about uncertainty in models. In parallel, the second version of Systems Modeling Language (SysML v2) was released as the next-generation systems modeling language, offering improved semantic rigor and reusability, yet lacking native constructs aligned with PSUM for first-class uncertainty representation. This paper proposes a systematic extension of SysML v2 that incorporates the PSUM metamodel into its modeling framework. The extension enables explicit specification of indeterminacy sources, structured characterization of uncertainties, and consistent propagation of uncertainty within system models, while preserving conformance with SysML v2 syntax and semantics. We validate the approach through seven case studies. Results demonstrate that the proposed extension (PSUM-SysMLv2) is expressive and applicable for uncertainty-aware MBSE, and potentially enables uncertainty and uncertainty propagation analyses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhong","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhenzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-dimensional Assessment and Explainable Feedback for Counselor Responses to Client Resistance in Text-based Counseling with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effectively addressing client resistance is a sophisticated clinical skill in psychological counseling, yet practitioners often lack timely and scalable supervisory feedback to refine their approaches. Although current NLP research has examined overall counseling quality and general therapeutic skills, it fails to provide granular evaluations of high-stakes moments where clients exhibit resistance. In this work, we present a comprehensive pipeline for the multi-dimensional evaluation of human counselors' interventions specifically targeting client resistance in text-based therapy. We introduce a theory-driven framework that decomposes counselor responses into four distinct communication mechanisms. Leveraging this framework, we curate and share an expert-annotated dataset of real-world counseling excerpts, pairing counselor-client interactions with professional ratings and explanatory rationales. Using this data, we perform full-parameter instruction tuning on a Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct backbone to model fine-grained evaluative judgments of response quality and generate explanations underlying. Experimental results show that our approach can effectively distinguish the quality of different communication mechanisms (77-81% F1), substantially outperforming GPT-4o and Claude-3.5-Sonnet (45-59% F1). Moreover, the model produces high-quality explanations that closely align with expert references and receive near-ceiling ratings from human experts (2.8-2.9/3.0). A controlled experiment with 43 counselors further confirms that receiving these AI-generated feedback significantly improves counselors' ability to respond effectively to client resistance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huazhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huazhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congde","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Congde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qirui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Guoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank Youhua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Frank Youhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhuo","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Mengzhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentLTV: An Agent-Based Unified Search-and-Evolution Framework for Automated Lifetime Value Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.5; H.4.2; I.2.6; I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lifetime Value (LTV) prediction is critical in advertising, recommender systems, and e-commerce. In practice, LTV data patterns vary across decision scenarios. As a result, practitioners often build complex, scenario-specific pipelines and iterate over feature processing, objective design, and tuning. This process is expensive and hard to transfer. We propose AgentLTV, an agent-based unified search-and-evolution framework for automated LTV modeling. AgentLTV treats each candidate solution as an {executable pipeline program}. LLM-driven agents generate code, run and repair pipelines, and analyze execution feedback. Two decision agents coordinate a two-stage search. The Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) stage explores a broad space of modeling choices under a fixed budget, guided by the Polynomial Upper Confidence bounds for Trees criterion and a Pareto-aware multi-metric value function. The Evolutionary Algorithm (EA) stage refines the best MCTS program via island-based evolution with crossover, mutation, and migration. Experiments on a large-scale proprietary dataset and a public benchmark show that AgentLTV consistently discovers strong models across ranking and error metrics. Online bucket-level analysis further indicates improved ranking consistency and value calibration, especially for high-value and negative-LTV segments. We summarize practitioner-oriented takeaways: use MCTS for rapid adaptation to new data patterns, use EA for stable refinement, and validate deployment readiness with bucket-level ranking and calibration diagnostics. The proposed AgentLTV has been successfully deployed online."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures, submitted to KDD 2026: 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining, ADS Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyv","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenyv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fengling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoli","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Guoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng Tao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Heng Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Correcting VLA: Online Action Refinement via Sparse World Imagination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:58:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard vision-language-action (VLA) models rely on fitting statistical data priors, limiting their robust understanding of underlying physical dynamics. Reinforcement learning enhances physical grounding through exploration yet typically relies on external reward signals that remain isolated from the agent's internal states. World action models have emerged as a promising paradigm that integrates imagination and control to enable predictive planning. However, they rely on implicit context modeling, lacking explicit mechanisms for self-improvement. To solve these problems, we propose Self-Correcting VLA (SC-VLA), which achieve self-improvement by intrinsically guiding action refinement through sparse imagination. We first design sparse world imagination by integrating auxiliary predictive heads to forecast current task progress and future trajectory trends, thereby constraining the policy to encode short-term physical evolution. Then we introduce the online action refinement module to reshape progress-dependent dense rewards, adjusting trajectory orientation based on the predicted sparse future states. Evaluations on challenging robot manipulation tasks from simulation benchmarks and real-world settings demonstrate that SC-VLA achieve state-of-the-art performance, yielding the highest task throughput with 16% fewer steps and a 9% higher success rate than the best-performing baselines, alongside a 14% gain in real-world experiments. Code is available at https://github.com/Kisaragi0/SC-VLA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ADM-DP: Adaptive Dynamic Modality Diffusion Policy through Vision-Tactile-Graph Fusion for Multi-Agent Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent robotic manipulation remains challenging due to the combined demands of coordination, grasp stability, and collision avoidance in shared workspaces. To address these challenges, we propose the Adaptive Dynamic Modality Diffusion Policy (ADM-DP), a framework that integrates vision, tactile, and graph-based (multi-agent pose) modalities for coordinated control. ADM-DP introduces four key innovations. First, an enhanced visual encoder merges RGB and point-cloud features via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) modulation to enrich perception. Second, a tactile-guided grasping strategy uses Force-Sensitive Resistor (FSR) feedback to detect insufficient contact and trigger corrective grasp refinement, improving grasp stability. Third, a graph-based collision encoder leverages shared tool center point (TCP) positions of multiple agents as structured kinematic context to maintain spatial awareness and reduce inter-agent interference. Fourth, an Adaptive Modality Attention Mechanism (AMAM) dynamically re-weights modalities according to task context, enabling flexible fusion. For scalability and modularity, a decoupled training paradigm is employed in which agents learn independent policies while sharing spatial information. This maintains low interdependence between agents while retaining collective awareness. Across seven multi-agent tasks, ADM-DP achieves 12-25% performance gains over state-of-the-art baselines. Ablation studies show the greatest improvements in tasks requiring multiple sensory modalities, validating our adaptive fusion strategy and demonstrating its robustness for diverse manipulation scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21619","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21619","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21619","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muku","familyName":"Akasaka","name":"Akasaka, Muku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyeon Caren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Soyeon Caren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When More Is Less: A Systematic Analysis of Spatial and Commonsense Information for Visual Spatial Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual spatial reasoning (VSR) remains challenging for modern vision-language models (VLMs), despite advances in multimodal architectures. A common strategy is to inject additional information at inference time, such as explicit spatial cues, external commonsense knowledge, or chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning instructions. However, it remains unclear when such information genuinely improves reasoning and when it introduces noise. In this paper, we conduct a hypothesis-driven analysis of information injection for VSR across three representative VLMs and two public benchmarks. We examine (i) the type and number of spatial contexts, (ii) the amount and relevance of injected commonsense knowledge, and (iii) the interaction between spatial grounding and CoT prompting. Our results reveal a consistent pattern: more information does not necessarily yield better reasoning. Targeted single spatial cues outperform multi-context aggregation, excessive or weakly relevant commonsense knowledge degrades performance, and CoT prompting improves accuracy only when spatial grounding is sufficiently precise. These findings highlight the importance of selective, task-aligned information injection and provide practical guidance for designing reliable multimodal reasoning pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 6 figures, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21619","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinzhe","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xinzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangtao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Virtual Biopsy for Intracranial Tumors Diagnosis on MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:14:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep intracranial tumors situated in eloquent brain regions controlling vital functions present critical diagnostic challenges. Clinical practice has shifted toward stereotactic biopsy for pathological confirmation before treatment. Yet biopsy carries inherent risks of hemorrhage and neurological deficits and struggles with sampling bias due to tumor spatial heterogeneity, because pathological changes are typically region-selective rather than tumor-wide. Therefore, advancing non-invasive MRI-based pathology prediction is essential for holistic tumor assessment and modern clinical decision-making.\n The primary challenge lies in data scarcity: low tumor incidence requires long collection cycles, and annotation demands biopsy-verified pathology from neurosurgical experts. Additionally, tiny lesion volumes lacking segmentation masks cause critical features to be overwhelmed by background noise. To address these challenges, we construct the ICT-MRI dataset - the first public biopsy-verified benchmark with 249 cases across four categories. We propose a Virtual Biopsy framework comprising: MRI-Processor for standardization; Tumor-Localizer employing vision-language models for coarse-to-fine localization via weak supervision; and Adaptive-Diagnoser with a Masked Channel Attention mechanism fusing local discriminative features with global contexts. Experiments demonstrate over 90% accuracy, outperforming baselines by more than 20%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangning","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Kangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wencong","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wencong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structurally Aligned Subtask-Level Memory for Software Engineering Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:13:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated significant potential as autonomous software engineering (SWE) agents. Recent work has further explored augmenting these agents with memory mechanisms to support long-horizon reasoning. However, these approaches typically operate at a coarse instance granularity, treating the entire problem-solving episode as the atomic unit of storage and retrieval. We empirically demonstrate that instance-level memory suffers from a fundamental granularity mismatch, resulting in misguided retrieval when tasks with similar surface descriptions require distinct reasoning logic at specific stages. To address this, we propose Structurally Aligned Subtask-Level Memory, a method that aligns memory storage, retrieval, and updating with the agent's functional decomposition. Extensive experiments on SWE-bench Verified demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms both vanilla agents and strong instance-level memory baselines across diverse backbones, improving mean Pass@1 over the vanilla agent by +4.7 pp on average (e.g., +6.8 pp on Gemini 2.5 Pro). Performance gains grow with more interaction steps, showing that leveraging past experience benefits long-horizon reasoning in complex software engineering tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiange","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Autonomous Graph Data Analytics with Analytics-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:09:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper argues that reliable end-to-end graph data analytics cannot be achieved by retrieval- or code-generation-centric LLM agents alone. Although large language models (LLMs) provide strong reasoning capabilities, practical graph analytics for non-expert users requires explicit analytical grounding to support intent-to-execution translation, task-aware graph construction, and reliable execution across diverse graph algorithms. We envision Analytics-Augmented Generation (AAG) as a new paradigm that treats analytical computation as a first-class concern and positions LLMs as knowledge-grounded analytical coordinators. By integrating knowledge-driven task planning, algorithm-centric LLM-analytics interaction, and task-aware graph construction, AAG enables end-to-end graph analytics pipelines that translate natural-language user intent into automated execution and interpretable results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganap Ashit","familyName":"Tewary","name":"Tewary, Ganap Ashit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nrusinga Charan","familyName":"Gantayat","name":"Gantayat, Nrusinga Charan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQR-HNSW: Accelerating Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search via Density-aware Quantization and Multi-stage Re-ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search has become fundamental to modern AI infrastructure, powering recommendation systems, search engines, and large language models across industry leaders from Google to OpenAI. Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs have emerged as the dominant ANN algorithm, widely adopted in production systems due to their superior recall versus latency balance. However, as vector databases scale to billions of embeddings, HNSW faces critical bottlenecks: memory consumption expands, distance computation overhead dominates query latency, and it suffers suboptimal performance on heterogeneous data distributions. This paper presents Adaptive Quantization and Rerank HNSW (AQR-HNSW), a novel framework that synergistically integrates three strategies to enhance HNSW scalability. AQR-HNSW introduces (1) density-aware adaptive quantization, achieving 4x compression while preserving distance relationships; (2) multi-state re-ranking that reduces unnecessary computations by 35%; and (3) quantization-optimized SIMD implementations delivering 16-64 operations per cycle across architectures. Evaluation on standard benchmarks demonstrates 2.5-3.3x higher queries per second (QPS) than state-of-the-art HNSW implementations while maintaining over 98% recall, with 75% memory reduction for the index graph and 5x faster index construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at DAC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Touseef","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Touseef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laila","familyName":"Cure","name":"Cure, Laila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souvika","familyName":"Sarkar","name":"Sarkar, Souvika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval Challenges in Low-Resource Public Service Information: A Case Study on Food Pantry Access"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:48:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Public service information systems are often fragmented, inconsistently formatted, and outdated. These characteristics create low-resource retrieval environments that hinder timely access to critical services. We investigate retrieval challenges in such settings through the domain of food pantry access, a socially urgent problem given persistent food insecurity. We develop an AI-powered conversational retrieval system that scrapes and indexes publicly available pantry data and employs a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline to support natural language queries via a web interface. We conduct a pilot evaluation study using community-sourced queries to examine system behavior in realistic scenarios. Our analysis reveals key limitations in retrieval robustness, handling underspecified queries, and grounding over inconsistent knowledge bases. This ongoing work exposes fundamental IR challenges in low-resource environments and motivates future research on robust conversational retrieval to improve access to critical public resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NGDB-Zoo: Towards Efficient and Scalable Neural Graph Databases Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Graph Databases (NGDBs) facilitate complex logical reasoning over incomplete knowledge structures, yet their training efficiency and expressivity are constrained by rigid query-level batching and structure-exclusive embeddings. We present NGDB-Zoo, a unified framework that resolves these bottlenecks by synergizing operator-level training with semantic augmentation. By decoupling logical operators from query topologies, NGDB-Zoo transforms the training loop into a dynamically scheduled data-flow execution, enabling multi-stream parallelism and achieving a $1.8\\times$ - $6.8\\times$ throughput compared to baselines. Furthermore, we formalize a decoupled architecture to integrate high-dimensional semantic priors from Pre-trained Text Encoders (PTEs) without triggering I/O stalls or memory overflows. Extensive evaluations on six benchmarks, including massive graphs like ogbl-wikikg2 and ATLAS-Wiki, demonstrate that NGDB-Zoo maintains high GPU utilization across diverse logical patterns and significantly mitigates representation friction in hybrid neuro-symbolic reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung X.","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Trung X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji Woo","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Ji Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang D.","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Chang D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Hidden Semantic Bottleneck in Conditional Embeddings of Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:46:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers have achieved state-of-the-art performance in class-conditional and multimodal generation, yet the structure of their learned conditional embeddings remains poorly understood. In this work, we present the first systematic study of these embeddings and uncover a notable redundancy: class-conditioned embeddings exhibit extreme angular similarity, exceeding 99\\% on ImageNet-1K, while continuous-condition tasks such as pose-guided image generation and video-to-audio generation reach over 99.9\\%. We further find that semantic information is concentrated in a small subset of dimensions, with head dimensions carrying the dominant signal and tail dimensions contributing minimally. By pruning low-magnitude dimensions--removing up to two-thirds of the embedding space--we show that generation quality and fidelity remain largely unaffected, and in some cases improve. These results reveal a semantic bottleneck in Transformer-based diffusion models, providing new insights into how semantics are encoded and suggesting opportunities for more efficient conditioning mechanisms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungmin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hobeom","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hobeom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dohyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dohyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsu","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Minsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeahong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jeahong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPOC: Safety-Aware Planning Under Partial Observability And Physical Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embodied Task Planning with large language models faces safety challenges in real-world environments, where partial observability and physical constraints must be respected. Existing benchmarks often overlook these critical factors, limiting their ability to evaluate both feasibility and safety. We introduce SPOC, a benchmark for safety-aware embodied task planning, which integrates strict partial observability, physical constraints, step-by-step planning, and goal-condition-based evaluation. Covering diverse household hazards such as fire, fluid, injury, object damage, and pollution, SPOC enables rigorous assessment through both state and constraint-based online metrics. Experiments with state-of-the-art LLMs reveal that current models struggle to ensure safety-aware planning, particularly under implicit constraints. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/khm159/SPOC"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaojiao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiaojiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking Semantic-Aware Watermarks via LLM-Guided Coherence-Preserving Semantic Injection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative images have proliferated on Web platforms in social media and online copyright distribution scenarios, and semantic watermarking has increasingly been integrated into diffusion models to support reliable provenance tracking and forgery prevention for web content. Traditional noise-layer-based watermarking, however, remains vulnerable to inversion attacks that can recover embedded signals. To mitigate this, recent content-aware semantic watermarking schemes bind watermark signals to high-level image semantics, constraining local edits that would otherwise disrupt global coherence. Yet, large language models (LLMs) possess structured reasoning capabilities that enable targeted exploration of semantic spaces, allowing locally fine-grained but globally coherent semantic alterations that invalidate such bindings. To expose this overlooked vulnerability, we introduce a Coherence-Preserving Semantic Injection (CSI) attack that leverages LLM-guided semantic manipulation under embedding-space similarity constraints. This alignment enforces visual-semantic consistency while selectively perturbing watermark-relevant semantics, ultimately inducing detector misclassification. Extensive empirical results show that CSI consistently outperforms prevailing attack baselines against content-aware semantic watermarking, revealing a fundamental security weakness of current semantic watermark designs when confronted with LLM-driven semantic perturbations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by The Web Conference 2026 (Short Paper Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kart Leong","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Kart Leong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihai","familyName":"Rotaru","name":"Rotaru, Mihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Informed Neural Network using Finite Difference Method"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:33:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent engineering applications using deep learning, physics-informed neural network (PINN) is a new development as it can exploit the underlying physics of engineering systems. The novelty of PINN lies in the use of partial differential equations (PDE) for the loss function. Most PINNs are implemented using automatic differentiation (AD) for training the PDE loss functions. A lesser well-known study is the use of finite difference method (FDM) as an alternative. Unlike an AD based PINN, an immediate benefit of using a FDM based PINN is low implementation cost. In this paper, we propose the use of finite difference method for estimating the PDE loss functions in PINN. Our work is inspired by computational analysis in electromagnetic systems that traditionally solve Laplace's equation using successive over-relaxation. In the case of Laplace's equation, our PINN approach can be seen as taking the Laplacian filter response of the neural network output as the loss function. Thus, the implementation of PINN can be very simple. In our experiments, we tested PINN on Laplace's equation and Burger's equation. We showed that using FDM, PINN consistently outperforms non-PINN based deep learning. When comparing to AD based PINNs, we showed that our method is faster to compute as well as on par in terms of error reduction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibing","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ruibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengrong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhengrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanjing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimu","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zimu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangjun","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoshuai","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Xiaoshuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEF-MAP: Subspace-Decomposed Expert Fusion for Robust Multimodal HD Map Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-definition (HD) maps are essential for autonomous driving, yet multi-modal fusion often suffers from inconsistency between camera and LiDAR modalities, leading to performance degradation under low-light conditions, occlusions, or sparse point clouds. To address this, we propose SEFMAP, a Subspace-Expert Fusion framework for robust multimodal HD map prediction. The key idea is to explicitly disentangle BEV features into four semantic subspaces: LiDAR-private, Image-private, Shared, and Interaction. Each subspace is assigned a dedicated expert, thereby preserving modality-specific cues while capturing cross-modal consensus. To adaptively combine expert outputs, we introduce an uncertainty-aware gating mechanism at the BEV-cell level, where unreliable experts are down-weighted based on predictive variance, complemented by a usage balance regularizer to prevent expert collapse. To enhance robustness in degraded conditions and promote role specialization, we further propose distribution-aware masking: during training, modality-drop scenarios are simulated using EMA-statistical surrogate features, and a specialization loss enforces distinct behaviors of private, shared, and interaction experts across complete and masked inputs. Experiments on nuScenes and Argoverse2 benchmarks demonstrate that SEFMAP achieves state-of-the-art performance, surpassing prior methods by +4.2% and +4.8% in mAP, respectively. SEF-MAPprovides a robust and effective solution for multi-modal HD map prediction under diverse and degraded conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharv","familyName":"Murgai","name":"Murgai, Sharv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utkarsh","familyName":"Utkarsh","name":"Utkarsh, Utkarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle C.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Kyle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Edelman","name":"Edelman, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin C. S.","familyName":"Acquesta","name":"Acquesta, Erin C. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher Vincent","familyName":"Rackauckas","name":"Rackauckas, Christopher Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ABM-UDE: Developing Surrogates for Epidemic Agent-Based Models via Scientific Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:19:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agent-based epidemic models (ABMs) encode behavioral and policy heterogeneity but are too slow for nightly hospital planning. We develop county-ready surrogates that learn directly from exascale ABM trajectories using Universal Differential Equations (UDEs): mechanistic SEIR-family ODEs with a neural-parameterized contact rate $κ_ϕ(u,t)$ (no additive residual). Our contributions are threefold: we adapt multiple shooting and an observer-based prediction-error method (PEM) to stabilize identification of neural-augmented epidemiological dynamics across intervention-driven regime shifts; we enforce positivity and mass conservation and show the learned contact-rate parameterization yields a well-posed vector field; and we quantify accuracy, calibration, and compute against ABM ensembles and UDE baselines. On a representative ExaEpi scenario, PEM-UDE reduces mean MSE by 77% relative to single-shooting UDE (3.00 vs. 13.14) and by 20% relative to MS-UDE (3.75). Reliability improves in parallel: empirical coverage of ABM $10$-$90$% and $25$-$75$% bands rises from 0.68/0.43 (UDE) and 0.79/0.55 (MS-UDE) to 0.86/0.61 with PEM-UDE and 0.94/0.69 with MS+PEM-UDE, indicating calibrated uncertainty rather than overconfident fits. Inference runs in seconds on commodity CPUs (20-35 s per $\\sim$90-day forecast), enabling nightly ''what-if'' sweeps on a laptop. Relative to a $\\sim$100 CPU-hour ABM reference run, this yields $\\sim10^{4}\\times$ lower wall-clock per scenario. This closes the realism-cadence gap, supports threshold-aware decision-making (e.g., maintaining ICU occupancy $\u0026lt;75$%), preserves mechanistic interpretability, and enables calibrated, risk-aware scenario planning on standard institutional hardware. Beyond epidemics, the ABM$\\to$UDE recipe provides a portable path to distill agent-based simulators into fast, trustworthy surrogates for other scientific domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21563","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21563","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21563","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sewon","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Sewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrobona","familyName":"Bagchi","name":"Bagchi, Shrobona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehak","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyang-Tag","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Hyang-Tag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-Su","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yong-Su","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-Woo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seung-Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Entanglement recovery by reversing the effect of noise in quantum repeater"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:37:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a method to directly recover the degree of entanglement distributed by entanglement swapping in the presence of noise. Our approach introduces a reversing operation that probabilistically undoes the effect of amplitude damping or photon loss on a single entangled pair, enabling heralded recovery of entanglement. We demonstrate that entanglement can be substantially recovered even under strong noise, including parameter regimes where the distributed entanglement would otherwise vanish due to entanglement sudden death. We analyze the effectiveness of the protocol in two representative repeater models, i.e.,~two-way and one-way architectures and identify the optimal reversing strategy. Due to its heralded and single-copy nature, our protocol is readily compatible with other entanglement recovery techniques such as entanglement purification and distillation. Our work provides a practical and experimentally feasible way toward robust entanglement distribution in current and near-term quantum repeater architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21563","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josep Miquel","familyName":"Jornet","name":"Jornet, Josep Miquel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markku","familyName":"Juntti","name":"Juntti, Markku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impact of Pointing Errors and Correlated Wall Blockages on Practical Grid-based Indoor Terahertz Communication Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Terahertz (THz) communications has emerged as a promising technology for future wireless systems due to its potential to support extremely high data rates. However, severe path loss, blockage effects, and sensitivity to beam misalignment pose major challenges to reliable indoor THz communications. In this paper, we investigate the coverage probability of downlink transmission in a three-dimensional (3D) indoor THz communication system under structured access point (AP) deployments, with a focus on square and hexagonal grid topologies. A tractable analytical framework is developed to jointly account for human blockages, correlated wall blockages across APs, beam training, and residual pointing error. Numerical results demonstrate that wall blockage correlation significantly reduces the association and coverage probabilities, and its impact cannot be neglected in system performance analysis. Compared with square grid AP deployments, hexagonal grids consistently achieve higher coverage by mitigating correlated wall blockage effects and reducing the distances between user equipments (UEs) and their associated APs. Furthermore, coverage performance is shown to strongly depend on the UE location, with noticeable degradation as the UE moves away from its nearest AP. Residual pointing error is found to introduce substantial coverage loss, especially for longer links. In addition, beam training analysis reveals a non-monotonic relationship between antenna array size and training overhead, highlighting an inherent tradeoff among antenna configuration, beamwidth selection, and beam training efficiency. These findings provide useful insights into the design and deployment of practical indoor THz communication systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitri","familyName":"Kalaev","name":"Kalaev, Dmitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Fatsi","name":"Fatsi, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Barcklow","name":"Barcklow, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Reinert","name":"Reinert, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Melnyk","name":"Melnyk, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senthil","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Senthil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. Bayan","familyName":"Bruss","name":"Bruss, C. Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting RAG Retrievers: An Information Theoretic Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:19:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems rely critically on the retriever module to surface relevant context for large language models. Although numerous retrievers have recently been proposed, each built on different ranking principles such as lexical matching, dense embeddings, or graph citations, there remains a lack of systematic understanding of how these mechanisms differ and overlap. Existing benchmarks primarily compare entire RAG pipelines or introduce new datasets, providing little guidance on selecting or combining retrievers themselves. Those that do compare retrievers directly use a limited set of evaluation tools which fail to capture complementary and overlapping strengths. This work presents MIGRASCOPE, a Mutual Information based RAG Retriever Analysis Scope. We revisit state-of-the-art retrievers and introduce principled metrics grounded in information and statistical estimation theory to quantify retrieval quality, redundancy, synergy, and marginal contribution. We further show that if chosen carefully, an ensemble of retrievers outperforms any single retriever. We leverage the developed tools over major RAG corpora to provide unique insights on contribution levels of the state-of-the-art retrievers. Our findings provide a fresh perspective on the structure of modern retrieval techniques and actionable guidance for designing robust and efficient RAG systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changqing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Changqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueru","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yueru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Changhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 3D scene understanding is essential for embodied intelligence, with occupancy prediction emerging as a key task for reasoning about both objects and free space. Existing approaches largely rely on depth priors (e.g., DepthAnything) but make only limited use of 3D cues, restricting performance and generalization. Recently, visual geometry models such as VGGT have shown strong capability in providing rich 3D priors, but similar to monocular depth foundation models, they still operate at the level of visible surfaces rather than volumetric interiors, motivating us to explore how to more effectively leverage these increasingly powerful geometry priors for 3D occupancy prediction. We present GPOcc, a framework that leverages generalizable visual geometry priors (GPs) for monocular occupancy prediction. Our method extends surface points inward along camera rays to generate volumetric samples, which are represented as Gaussian primitives for probabilistic occupancy inference. To handle streaming input, we further design a training-free incremental update strategy that fuses per-frame Gaussians into a unified global representation. Experiments on Occ-ScanNet and EmbodiedOcc-ScanNet demonstrate significant gains: GPOcc improves mIoU by +9.99 in the monocular setting and +11.79 in the streaming setting over prior state of the art. Under the same depth prior, it achieves +6.73 mIoU while running 2.65$\\times$ faster. These results highlight that GPOcc leverages geometry priors more effectively and efficiently. Code will be released at https://github.com/JuIvyy/GPOcc."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21551","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21551","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21551","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilu","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Zhilu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Basis to Basis: Gaussian Particle Representation for Interpretable PDE Operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:16:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning PDE dynamics for fluids increasingly relies on neural operators and Transformer-based models, yet these approaches often lack interpretability and struggle with localized, high-frequency structures while incurring quadratic cost in spatial samples. We propose representing fields with a Gaussian basis, where learned atoms carry explicit geometry (centers, anisotropic scales, weights) and form a compact, mesh-agnostic, directly visualizable state. Building on this representation, we introduce a Gaussian Particle Operator that acts in modal space: learned Gaussian modal windows perform a Petrov-Galerkin measurement, and PG Gaussian Attention enables global cross-scale coupling. This basis-to-basis design is resolution-agnostic and achieves near-linear complexity in N for a fixed modal budget, supporting irregular geometries and seamless 2D-to-3D extension. On standard PDE benchmarks and real datasets, our method attains state-of-the-art competitive accuracy while providing intrinsic interpretability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21551","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuchong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zihuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wangze","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Wangze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuemin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xuemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng Tao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Heng Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kui","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Kui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAC: Relation-Aware Cache Replacement for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:10:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The scaling of Large Language Model (LLM) services faces significant cost and latency challenges, making effective caching under tight capacity crucial. Existing cache replacement policies, from heuristics to learning-based methods, predominantly rely on limited-window statistics such as recency and frequency. We show these signals are not robust for real-world LLM workloads, which exhibit long reuse distances and sparse local recurrence.\n To address these limitations, we propose Relation-Aware Cache (RAC), an online eviction strategy that leverages semantic relations among requests to guide eviction decisions. RAC synthesizes two relation-aware signals: (1) Topical Prevalence, which aggregates access evidence at the topic level to capture long-horizon reuse; and (2) Structural Importance, which leverages local intra-topic dependency structure to discriminate entries by their future reuse value. Extensive evaluations show that RAC maintains high effectiveness across diverse workloads, consistently surpassing state-of-the-art baselines by 20%--30% in cache hit ratio."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barah","familyName":"Fazili","name":"Fazili, Barah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustava","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Koustava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Multilingual Embeddings via Multi-Way Parallel Text Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:58:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual pretraining typically lacks explicit alignment signals, leading to suboptimal cross-lingual alignment in the representation space. In this work, we show that training standard pretrained models for cross-lingual alignment with a multi-way parallel corpus in a diverse pool of languages can substantially improve multilingual and cross-lingual representations for NLU tasks. We construct a multi-way parallel dataset using translations of English text from an off-the-shelf NMT model for a pool of six target languages and achieve strong cross-lingual alignment through contrastive learning. This leads to substantial performance gains across both seen and unseen languages for multiple tasks from the MTEB benchmark evaluated for XLM-Roberta and multilingual BERT base models. Using a multi-way parallel corpus for contrastive training yields substantial gains on bitext mining (21.3%), semantic similarity (5.3%), and classification (28.4%) compared to English-centric (En-X) bilingually parallel data, where X is sampled from a pool of multiple target languages. Furthermore, finetuning mE5 model on a small dataset with multi-way parallelism significantly improves bitext mining compared to one without, underscoring the importance of multi-way cross-lingual supervision even for models already pretrained for high-quality sentence embeddings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21535","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21535","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21535","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beizhen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Beizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanzhi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Guanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pseudo-View Enhancement via Confidence Fusion for Unposed Sparse-View Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:45:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D scene reconstruction under unposed sparse viewpoints is a highly challenging yet practically important problem, especially in outdoor scenes due to complex lighting and scale variation. With extremely limited input views, directly utilizing diffusion model to synthesize pseudo frames will introduce unreasonable geometry, which will harm the final reconstruction quality. To address these issues, we propose a novel framework for sparse-view outdoor reconstruction that achieves high-quality results through bidirectional pseudo frame restoration and scene perception Gaussian management. Specifically, we introduce a bidirectional pseudo frame restoration method that restores missing content by diffusion-based synthesis guided by adjacent frames with a lightweight pseudo-view deblur model and confidence mask inference algorithm. Then we propose a scene perception Gaussian management strategy that optimize Gaussians based on joint depth-density information. These designs significantly enhance reconstruction completeness, suppress floating artifacts and improve overall geometric consistency under extreme view sparsity. Experiments on outdoor benchmarks demonstrate substantial gains over existing methods in both fidelity and stability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21535","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Hyeon","familyName":"Mok","name":"Mok, Dong Hyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seoin","familyName":"Back","name":"Back, Seoin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Fung","name":"Fung, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Guoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Driven Design of Single Atom Catalysts via a Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:43:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:22:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly applied beyond natural language processing, demonstrating strong capabilities in complex scientific tasks that traditionally require human expertise. This progress has extended into materials discovery, where LLMs introduce a new paradigm by leveraging reasoning and in-context learning, capabilities absent from conventional machine learning approaches. Here, we present a Multi-Agent-based Electrocatalyst Search Through Reasoning and Optimization (MAESTRO) framework in which multiple LLMs with specialized roles collaboratively discover high-performance single atom catalysts for the oxygen reduction reaction. Within an autonomous design loop, agents iteratively reason, propose modifications, reflect on results and accumulate design history. Through in-context learning enabled by this iterative process, MAESTRO identified design principles not explicitly encoded in the LLMs' background knowledge and successfully discovered catalysts that break conventional scaling relations between reaction intermediates. These results highlight the potential of multi-agent LLM frameworks as a powerful strategy to generate chemical insight and discover promising catalysts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Homanga","familyName":"Bharadhwaj","name":"Bharadhwaj, Homanga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gedas","familyName":"Bertasius","name":"Bertasius, Gedas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Szafir","name":"Szafir, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiLo-VLA: Compositional Long-Horizon Manipulation via Linked Object-Centric Policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:33:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:21:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"General-purpose robots must master long-horizon manipulation, defined as tasks involving multiple kinematic structure changes (e.g., attaching or detaching objects) in unstructured environments. While Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer the potential to master diverse atomic skills, they struggle with the combinatorial complexity of sequencing them and are prone to cascading failures due to environmental sensitivity. To address these challenges, we propose LiLo-VLA (Linked Local VLA), a modular framework capable of zero-shot generalization to novel long-horizon tasks without ever being trained on them. Our approach decouples transport from interaction: a Reaching Module handles global motion, while an Interaction Module employs an object-centric VLA to process isolated objects of interest, ensuring robustness against irrelevant visual features and invariance to spatial configurations. Crucially, this modularity facilitates robust failure recovery through dynamic replanning and skill reuse, effectively mitigating the cascading errors common in end-to-end approaches. We introduce a 21-task simulation benchmark consisting of two challenging suites: LIBERO-Long++ and Ultra-Long. In these simulations, LiLo-VLA achieves a 69% average success rate, outperforming Pi0.5 by 41% and OpenVLA-OFT by 67%. Furthermore, real-world evaluations across 8 long-horizon tasks demonstrate an average success rate of 85%. Project page: https://yy-gx.github.io/LiLo-VLA/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21522","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21522","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21522","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changli","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Changli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shurui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinfan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Qinfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghao","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Zhonghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanning","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"One Brain, Omni Modalities: Towards Unified Non-Invasive Brain Decoding with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:24:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:21:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deciphering brain function through non-invasive recordings requires synthesizing complementary high-frequency electromagnetic (EEG/MEG) and low-frequency metabolic (fMRI) signals. However, despite their shared neural origins, extreme discrepancies have traditionally confined these modalities to isolated analysis pipelines, hindering a holistic interpretation of brain activity. To bridge this fragmentation, we introduce \\textbf{NOBEL}, a \\textbf{n}euro-\\textbf{o}mni-modal \\textbf{b}rain-\\textbf{e}ncoding \\textbf{l}arge language model (LLM) that unifies these heterogeneous signals within the LLM's semantic embedding space. Our architecture integrates a unified encoder for EEG and MEG with a novel dual-path strategy for fMRI, aligning non-invasive brain signals and external sensory stimuli into a shared token space, then leverages an LLM as a universal backbone. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that NOBEL serves as a robust generalist across standard single-modal tasks. We also show that the synergistic fusion of electromagnetic and metabolic signals yields higher decoding accuracy than unimodal baselines, validating the complementary nature of multiple neural modalities. Furthermore, NOBEL exhibits strong capabilities in stimulus-aware decoding, effectively interpreting visual semantics from multi-subject fMRI data on the NSD and HAD datasets while uniquely leveraging direct stimulus inputs to verify causal links between sensory signals and neural responses. NOBEL thus takes a step towards unifying non-invasive brain decoding, demonstrating the promising potential of omni-modal brain understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21522","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheang","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Zheang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Honglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Which Tool Response Should I Trust? Tool-Expertise-Aware Chest X-ray Agent with Multimodal Agentic Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:09:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents with tool-use capabilities show promise for integrating the domain expertise of various tools. In the medical field, however, tools are usually AI models that are inherently error-prone and can produce contradictory responses. Existing research on medical agents lacks sufficient understanding of the tools' realistic reliability and thus cannot effectively resolve tool conflicts. To address this gap, this paper introduces a framework that enables an agent to interact with tools and empirically learn their practical trustworthiness across different types of multimodal queries via agentic learning. As a concrete instantiation, we focus on chest X-ray analysis and present a tool-expertise-aware chest X-ray agent (TEA-CXA). When tool outputs disagree, the agent experimentally accepts or rejects multimodal tool results, receives rewards, and learns which tool to trust for each query type. Importantly, TEA-CXA extends existing codebases for reinforcement learning with multi-turn tool-calling that focus on textual inputs, to support multimodal contexts effectively. In addition, we enhance the codebase for medical use scenarios by supporting multiple tool calls in one turn, parallel tool inference, and multi-image accommodation within a single user query. Our code framework is applicable to general medical research on multi-turn tool-calling reinforcement learning in multimodal settings. Experiments show that TEA-CXA outperforms the state-of-the-art methods and a comprehensive set of baselines. Code will be released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hsiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chia-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Chih","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wei-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-En","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Yu-En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jhao-Ting","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jhao-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Unfolding Real-Time Super-Resolution Using Subpixel-Shift Twin Image and Convex Self-Similarity Prior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:58:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-image super-resolution (MISR) is a critical technique for satellite remote sensing. In the perspective of information, twin-image super-resolution (TISR) is regarded as the most challenging MISR scenario, having crucial applications like the SPOT-5 supermode imaging. In TISR, an image is super-resolved by its subpixel-shift counterpart (i.e., twin image), where the two images are typically offset by half a pixel both horizontally and vertically. We formulate the less investigated TISR using a convex criterion, which is implemented using a novel deep unfolding network. In the unfolding, an embedded simple shift operator trickily addresses the coupled TISR data-fitting terms, and a transformer trained with a convex self-similarity loss function elegantly implements the proximal mapping induced by the TISR regularizer. The proposed convex self-similarity unfolding supermode super-resolution (COSUP) algorithm is interpretable and achieves state-of-the-art performance with very fast millisecond-level computational time. COSUP is also tested on real-world data, for which the subpixel shifts would not be spatially uniform, with results showing great superiority over the official CNES supermode imaging product in terms of credible metrics (e.g., natural image quality evaluator, NIQE). Source codes: https://github.com/IHCLab/COSUP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures, IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:05Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang-Nhat","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang-Nhat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AHAN: Asymmetric Hierarchical Attention Network for Identical Twin Face Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:33:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:18:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identical twin face verification represents an extreme fine-grained recognition challenge where even state-of-the-art systems fail due to overwhelming genetic similarity. Current face recognition methods achieve over 99.8% accuracy on standard benchmarks but drop dramatically to 88.9% when distinguishing identical twins, exposing critical vulnerabilities in biometric security systems. The difficulty lies in learning features that capture subtle, non-genetic variations that uniquely identify individuals. We propose the Asymmetric Hierarchical Attention Network (AHAN), a novel architecture specifically designed for this challenge through multi-granularity facial analysis. AHAN introduces a Hierarchical Cross-Attention (HCA) module that performs multi-scale analysis on semantic facial regions, enabling specialized processing at optimal resolutions. We further propose a Facial Asymmetry Attention Module (FAAM) that learns unique biometric signatures by computing cross-attention between left and right facial halves, capturing subtle asymmetric patterns that differ even between twins. To ensure the network learns truly individuating features, we introduce Twin-Aware Pair-Wise Cross-Attention (TA-PWCA), a training-only regularization strategy that uses each subject's own twin as the hardest possible distractor. Extensive experiments on the ND_TWIN dataset demonstrate that AHAN achieves 92.3% twin verification accuracy, representing a 3.4% improvement over state-of-the-art methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AAAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umid","familyName":"Suleymanov","name":"Suleymanov, Umid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaur","familyName":"Rajabov","name":"Rajabov, Zaur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Mirzazada","name":"Mirzazada, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Kantarcioglu","name":"Kantarcioglu, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:09:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:17:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While defenses for structured PII are mature, Large Language Models (LLMs) pose a new threat: Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI), where models infer sensitive identity attributes, generate reputation-harmful content, or hallucinate potentially wrong information. The capacity of LLMs to self-regulate these complex, context-dependent sensitive information leaks without destroying utility remains an open scientific question. To address this, we introduce SemSIEdit, an inference-time framework where an agentic \"Editor\" iteratively critiques and rewrites sensitive spans to preserve narrative flow rather than simply refusing to answer. Our analysis reveals a Privacy-Utility Pareto Frontier, where this agentic rewriting reduces leakage by 34.6% across all three SemSI categories while incurring a marginal utility loss of 9.8%. We also uncover a Scale-Dependent Safety Divergence: large reasoning models (e.g., GPT-5) achieve safety through constructive expansion (adding nuance), whereas capacity-constrained models revert to destructive truncation (deleting text). Finally, we identify a Reasoning Paradox: while inference-time reasoning increases baseline risk by enabling the model to make deeper sensitive inferences, it simultaneously empowers the defense to execute safe rewrites."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:39Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21490","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21490","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21490","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingzi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Dingzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Diqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wen-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Connection Probabilities Estimation in Multi-layer Networks via Iterative Neighborhood Smoothing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:43:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:16:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the structural mechanisms of multi-layer networks is essential for analyzing complex systems characterized by multiple interacting layers. This work studies the problem of estimating connection probabilities in multi-layer networks and introduces a new Multi-layer Iterative Connection Probability Estimation (MICE) method. The proposed approach employs an iterative framework that jointly refines inter-layer and intra-layer similarity sets by dynamically updating distance metrics derived from current probability estimates. By leveraging both layer-level and node-level neighborhood information, MICE improves estimation accuracy while preserving computational efficiency. Theoretical analysis establishes the consistency of the estimator and shows that, under mild regularity conditions, the proposed method achieves an optimal convergence rate comparable to that of an oracle estimator. Extensive simulation studies across diverse graphon structures demonstrate the superior performance of MICE relative to existing methods. Empirical evaluations using brain network data from patients with Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) and global food and agricultural trade network data further illustrate the robustness and effectiveness of the method in link prediction tasks. Overall, this work provides a theoretically grounded and practically scalable framework for probabilistic modeling and inference in multi-layer network systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21490","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21485","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21485","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21485","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deja","familyName":"Dunlap","name":"Dunlap, Deja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. Thomas","familyName":"McCoy","name":"McCoy, R. Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the Usage of African-American Vernacular English in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:16:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In AI, most evaluations of natural language understanding tasks are conducted in standardized dialects such as Standard American English (SAE). In this work, we investigate how accurately large language models (LLMs) represent African American Vernacular English (AAVE). We analyze three LLMs to compare their usage of AAVE to the usage of humans who natively speak AAVE. We first analyzed interviews from the Corpus of Regional African American Language and TwitterAAE to identify the typical contexts where people use AAVE grammatical features such as ain't. We then prompted the LLMs to produce text in AAVE and compared the model-generated text to human usage patterns. We find that, in many cases, there are substantial differences between AAVE usage in LLMs and humans: LLMs usually underuse and misuse grammatical features characteristic of AAVE. Furthermore, through sentiment analysis and manual inspection, we found that the models replicated stereotypes about African Americans. These results highlight the need for more diversity in training data and the incorporation of fairness methods to mitigate the perpetuation of stereotypes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21485","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Amirpour","name":"Amirpour, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoshuai","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Xiaoshuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hantao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perceptual Quality Optimization of Image Super-Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Single-image super-resolution (SR) has achieved remarkable progress with deep learning, yet most approaches rely on distortion-oriented losses or heuristic perceptual priors, which often lead to a trade-off between fidelity and visual quality. To address this issue, we propose an \\textit{Efficient Perceptual Bi-directional Attention Network (Efficient-PBAN)} that explicitly optimizes SR towards human-preferred quality. Unlike patch-based quality models, Efficient-PBAN avoids extensive patch sampling and enables efficient image-level perception. The proposed framework is trained on our self-constructed SR quality dataset that covers a wide range of state-of-the-art SR methods with corresponding human opinion scores. Using this dataset, Efficient-PBAN learns to predict perceptual quality in a way that correlates strongly with subjective judgments. The learned metric is further integrated into SR training as a differentiable perceptual loss, enabling closed-loop alignment between reconstruction and perceptual assessment. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach delivers superior perceptual quality. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/Lighting-YXLI/Efficient-PBAN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures, accepted in ICASSP 26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikai","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houssam","familyName":"Zenati","name":"Zenati, Houssam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Kallus","name":"Kallus, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Gretton","name":"Gretton, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koulik","familyName":"Khamaru","name":"Khamaru, Koulik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aurélien","familyName":"Bibaut","name":"Bibaut, Aurélien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Inference after Directionally Stable Adaptive Experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:09:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study inference on scalar-valued pathwise differentiable targets after adaptive data collection, such as a bandit algorithm. We introduce a novel target-specific condition, directional stability, which is strictly weaker than previously imposed target-agnostic stability conditions. Under directional stability, we show that estimators that would have been efficient under i.i.d. data remain asymptotically normal and semiparametrically efficient when computed from adaptively collected trajectories. The canonical gradient has a martingale form, and directional stability guarantees stabilization of its predictable quadratic variation, enabling high-dimensional asymptotic normality. We characterize efficiency using a convolution theorem for the adaptive-data setting, and give a condition under which the one-step estimator attains the efficiency bound. We verify directional stability for LinUCB, yielding the first semiparametric efficiency guarantee for a regular scalar target under LinUCB sampling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengding","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaifeng","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zaifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhleen","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Prabhleen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibha","familyName":"Murthy","name":"Murthy, Vibha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Swanson","name":"Swanson, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pancake: Hierarchical Memory System for Multi-Agent LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:09:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we identify and address the core challenges of agentic memory management in LLM serving, where large-scale storage, frequent updates, and multiple coexisting agents jointly introduce complex and high-cost approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) searching problems. We present Pancake, a multi-tier agentic memory system that unifies three key techniques: (i) multi-level index caching for single agents, (ii) coordinated index management across multiple agents, and (iii) collaborative GPU-CPU acceleration. Pancake exposes easy-to-use interface that can be integrated into memory-based agents like Mem-GPT, and is compatible with agentic frameworks such as LangChain and LlamaIndex. Experiments on realistic agent workloads show that Pancake substantially outperforms existing frameworks, achieving more than 4.29x end-to-end throughput improvement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-wei","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Chun-wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sabato Marco","familyName":"Siniscalchi","name":"Siniscalchi, Sabato Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chin-Hui","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chin-Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Knowledge-Driven Approach to Music Segmentation, Music Source Separation and Cinematic Audio Source Separation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:07:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a knowledge-driven, model-based approach to segmenting audio into single-category and mixed-category chunks with applications to source separation. \"Knowledge\" here denotes information associated with the data, such as music scores. \"Model\" here refers to tool that can be used for audio segmentation and recognition, such as hidden Markov models. In contrast to conventional learning that often relies on annotated data with given segment categories and their corresponding boundaries to guide the learning process, the proposed framework does not depend on any pre-segmented training data and learns directly from the input audio and its related knowledge sources to build all necessary models autonomously. Evaluation on simulation data shows that score-guided learning achieves very good music segmentation and separation results. Tested on movie track data for cinematic audio source separation also shows that utilizing sound category knowledge achieves better separation results than those obtained with data-driven techniques without using such information."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Bethune","name":"Bethune, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Turrisi","name":"Turrisi, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno Kacper","familyName":"Mlodozeniec","name":"Mlodozeniec, Bruno Kacper","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau Rodriguez","familyName":"Lopez","name":"Lopez, Pau Rodriguez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lokesh","familyName":"Boominathan","name":"Boominathan, Lokesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Bhendawade","name":"Bhendawade, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amitis","familyName":"Shidani","name":"Shidani, Amitis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joris","familyName":"Pelemans","name":"Pelemans, Joris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theo X.","familyName":"Olausson","name":"Olausson, Theo X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devon","familyName":"Hjelm","name":"Hjelm, Devon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Dixon","name":"Dixon, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joao","familyName":"Monteiro","name":"Monteiro, Joao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Ablin","name":"Ablin, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu","familyName":"Banna","name":"Banna, Vishnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arno","familyName":"Blaas","name":"Blaas, Arno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kari","familyName":"Noriy","name":"Noriy, Kari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Busbridge","name":"Busbridge, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"Susskind","name":"Susskind, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Cuturi","name":"Cuturi, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Belousova","name":"Belousova, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Zappella","name":"Zappella, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russ","familyName":"Webb","name":"Webb, Russ","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Ramapuram","name":"Ramapuram, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Design Space of Tri-Modal Masked Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models have emerged as strong alternatives to autoregressive language models, with recent work initializing and fine-tuning a base unimodal model for bimodal generation. Diverging from previous approaches, we introduce the first tri-modal masked diffusion model pretrained from scratch on text, image-text, and audio-text data. We systematically analyze multimodal scaling laws, modality mixing ratios, noise schedules, and batch-size effects, and we provide optimized inference sampling defaults. Our batch-size analysis yields a novel stochastic differential equation (SDE)-based reparameterization that eliminates the need for tuning the optimal batch size as reported in recent work. This reparameterization decouples the physical batch size, often chosen based on compute constraints (GPU saturation, FLOP efficiency, wall-clock time), from the logical batch size, chosen to balance gradient variance during stochastic optimization. Finally, we pretrain a preliminary 3B-parameter tri-modal model on 6.4T tokens, demonstrating the capabilities of a unified design and achieving strong results in text generation, text-to-image tasks, and text-to-speech tasks. Our work represents the largest-scale systematic open study of multimodal discrete diffusion models conducted to date, providing insights into scaling behaviors across multiple modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 29 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21469","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21469","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21469","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meet Hemant","familyName":"Parikh","name":"Parikh, Meet Hemant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yaqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian-Xun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian-Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D-Flow SGLD: Source-Space Posterior Sampling for Scientific Inverse Problems with Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data assimilation and scientific inverse problems require reconstructing high-dimensional physical states from sparse and noisy observations, ideally with uncertainty-aware posterior samples that remain faithful to learned priors and governing physics. While training-free conditional generation is well developed for diffusion models, corresponding conditioning and posterior sampling strategies for Flow Matching (FM) priors remain comparatively under-explored, especially on scientific benchmarks where fidelity must be assessed beyond measurement misfit. In this work, we study training-free conditional generation for scientific inverse problems under FM priors and organize existing inference-time strategies by where measurement information is injected: (i) guided transport dynamics that perturb sampling trajectories using likelihood information, and (ii) source-distribution inference that performs posterior inference over the source variable while keeping the learned transport fixed. Building on the latter, we propose D-Flow SGLD, a source-space posterior sampling method that augments differentiable source inference with preconditioned stochastic gradient Langevin dynamics, enabling scalable exploration of the source posterior induced by new measurement operators without retraining the prior or modifying the learned FM dynamics. We benchmark representative methods from both families on a hierarchy of problems: 2D toy posteriors, chaotic Kuramoto-Sivashinsky trajectories, and wall-bounded turbulence reconstruction. Across these settings, we quantify trade-offs among measurement assimilation, posterior diversity, and physics/statistics fidelity, and establish D-Flow SGLD as a practical FM-compatible posterior sampler for scientific inverse problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21469","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Youngwoo","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, William Youngwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Calvin","familyName":"Yeung","name":"Yeung, Calvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansen Jin","familyName":"Lillemark","name":"Lillemark, Hansen Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuowen","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Zhuowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometric Priors for Generalizable World Models via Vector Symbolic Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:41:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key challenge in artificial intelligence and neuroscience is understanding how neural systems learn representations that capture the underlying dynamics of the world. Most world models represent the transition function with unstructured neural networks, limiting interpretability, sample efficiency, and generalization to unseen states or action compositions. We address these issues with a generalizable world model grounded in Vector Symbolic Architecture (VSA) principles as geometric priors. Our approach utilizes learnable Fourier Holographic Reduced Representation (FHRR) encoders to map states and actions into a high dimensional complex vector space with learned group structure and models transitions with element-wise complex multiplication. We formalize the framework's group theoretic foundation and show how training such structured representations to be approximately invariant enables strong multi-step composition directly in latent space and generalization performances over various experiments. On a discrete grid world environment, our model achieves 87.5% zero shot accuracy to unseen state-action pairs, obtains 53.6% higher accuracy on 20-timestep horizon rollouts, and demonstrates 4x higher robustness to noise relative to an MLP baseline. These results highlight how training to have latent group structure yields generalizable, data-efficient, and interpretable world models, providing a principled pathway toward structured models for real-world planning and reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, accepted to Neurips 2025 Workshop Symmetry and Geometry in Neural Representations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofoklis","familyName":"Kakouros","name":"Kakouros, Sofoklis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"iMiGUE-Speech: A Spontaneous Speech Dataset for Affective Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:38:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents iMiGUE-Speech, an extension of the iMiGUE dataset that provides a spontaneous affective corpus for studying emotional and affective states. The new release focuses on speech and enriches the original dataset with additional metadata, including speech transcripts, speaker-role separation between interviewer and interviewee, and word-level forced alignments. Unlike existing emotional speech datasets that rely on acted or laboratory-elicited emotions, iMiGUE-Speech captures spontaneous affect arising naturally from real match outcomes. To demonstrate the utility of the dataset and establish initial benchmarks, we introduce two evaluation tasks for comparative assessment: speech emotion recognition and transcript-based sentiment analysis. These tasks leverage state-of-the-art pre-trained representations to assess the dataset's ability to capture spontaneous affective states from both acoustic and linguistic modalities. iMiGUE-Speech can also be synchronously paired with micro-gesture annotations from the original iMiGUE dataset, forming a uniquely multimodal resource for studying speech-gesture affective dynamics. The extended dataset is available at https://github.com/CV-AC/imigue-speech."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Speech Prosody 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21462","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21462","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21462","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan F.","familyName":"Karr","name":"Karr, Alan F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Regina","familyName":"Ruane","name":"Ruane, Regina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Effects of Training Data Quality on Classifier Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:29:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We describe extensive numerical experiments assessing and quantifying how classifier performance depends on the quality of the training data, a frequently neglected component of the analysis of classifiers.\n More specifically, in the scientific context of metagenomic assembly of short DNA reads into \"contigs,\" we examine the effects of degrading the quality of the training data by multiple mechanisms, and for four classifiers -- Bayes classifiers, neural nets, partition models and random forests. We investigate both individual behavior and congruence among the classifiers. We find breakdown-like behavior that holds for all four classifiers, as degradation increases and they move from being mostly correct to only coincidentally correct, because they are wrong in the same way. In the process, a picture of spatial heterogeneity emerges: as the training data move farther from analysis data, classifier decisions degenerate, the boundary becomes less dense, and congruence increases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21462","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoke","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiaoke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavul","familyName":"Gauri","name":"Gauri, Bhavul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kam Woh","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Kam Woh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochong","familyName":"An","name":"An, Zhaochong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VecGlypher: Unified Vector Glyph Generation with Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector glyphs are the atomic units of digital typography, yet most learning-based pipelines still depend on carefully curated exemplar sheets and raster-to-vector postprocessing, which limits accessibility and editability. We introduce VecGlypher, a single multimodal language model that generates high-fidelity vector glyphs directly from text descriptions or image exemplars. Given a style prompt, optional reference glyph images, and a target character, VecGlypher autoregressively emits SVG path tokens, avoiding raster intermediates and producing editable, watertight outlines in one pass. A typography-aware data and training recipe makes this possible: (i) a large-scale continuation stage on 39K noisy Envato fonts to master SVG syntax and long-horizon geometry, followed by (ii) post-training on 2.5K expert-annotated Google Fonts with descriptive tags and exemplars to align language and imagery with geometry; preprocessing normalizes coordinate frames, canonicalizes paths, de-duplicates families, and quantizes coordinates for stable long-sequence decoding. On cross-family OOD evaluation, VecGlypher substantially outperforms both general-purpose LLMs and specialized vector-font baselines for text-only generation, while image-referenced generation reaches a state-of-the-art performance, with marked gains over DeepVecFont-v2 and DualVector. Ablations show that model scale and the two-stage recipe are critical and that absolute-coordinate serialization yields the best geometry. VecGlypher lowers the barrier to font creation by letting users design with words or exemplars, and provides a scalable foundation for future multimodal design tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR'26. Project page: https://xk-huang.github.io/VecGlypher/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21454","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21454","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21454","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Morgan","name":"Morgan, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ummay Sumaya","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Ummay Sumaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Lizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:15:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) can be interpreted as discrete-time state-space models, where the state evolution corresponds to an infinite-impulse-response (IIR) filtering operation governed by both feedforward weights and recurrent poles. While, in principle, all parameters including pole locations can be optimized via backpropagation through time (BPTT), such joint learning incurs substantial computational overhead and is often impractical for applications with limited training data. Echo state networks (ESNs) mitigate this limitation by fixing the recurrent dynamics and training only a linear readout, enabling efficient and stable online adaptation. In this work, we analytically and empirically examine why learning recurrent poles does not provide tangible benefits in data-constrained, real-time learning scenarios. Our analysis shows that pole learning renders the weight optimization problem highly non-convex, requiring significantly more training samples and iterations for gradient-based methods to converge to meaningful solutions. Empirically, we observe that for complex-valued data, gradient descent frequently exhibits prolonged plateaus, and advanced optimizers offer limited improvement. In contrast, fixed-pole architectures induce stable and well-conditioned state representations even with limited training data. Numerical results demonstrate that fixed-pole networks achieve superior performance with lower training complexity, making them more suitable for online real-time tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21454","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Dietrich","name":"Dietrich, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"McShannon","name":"McShannon, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Robustness of Deep Learning-Based Thyroid Nodule Segmentation in Ultrasound"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.6; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Introduction: Deep learning-based segmentation models are increasingly integrated into clinical imaging workflows, yet their robustness to adversarial perturbations remains incompletely characterized, particularly for ultrasound images. We evaluated adversarial attacks and inference-time defenses for thyroid nodule segmentation in B-mode ultrasound. Methods: Two black-box adversarial attacks were developed: (1) Structured Speckle Amplification Attack (SSAA), which injects boundary-targeted noise, and (2) Frequency-Domain Ultrasound Attack (FDUA), which applies bandpass-filtered phase perturbations in the Fourier domain. Three inference-time mitigations were evaluated on adversarial images: randomized preprocessing with test-time augmentation, deterministic input denoising, and stochastic ensemble inference with consistency-aware aggregation. Experiments were conducted on a U-Net segmentation model trained on cine-clips from a database of 192 thyroid nodules. Results: The baseline model achieved a mean Dice similarity coefficient (DSC) of 0.76 (SD 0.20) on unperturbed images. SSAA reduced DSC by 0.29 (SD 0.20) while maintaining high visual similarity (SSIM = 0.94). FDUA resulted in a smaller DSC reduction of 0.11 (SD 0.09) with lower visual fidelity (SSIM = 0.82). Against SSAA, all three defenses significantly improved DSC after correction, with deterministic denoising showing the largest recovery (+0.10, p \u0026lt; 0.001), followed by randomized preprocessing (+0.09, p \u0026lt; 0.001), and stochastic ensemble inference (+0.08, p = 0.002). No defense achieved statistically significant improvement against FDUA. Conclusion: Spatial-domain adversarial perturbations in ultrasound segmentation showed partial mitigation with input preprocessing, whereas frequency-domain perturbations were not mitigated by the defenses, highlighting modality-specific challenges in adversarial robustness evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Chung","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Van-Chung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toan-Van","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Toan-Van","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuria","familyName":"González-Prelcic","name":"González-Prelcic, Nuria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duy H. N.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duy H. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semi-Gridless Variational Bayes Channel Estimation in XL-MIMO: Near-Field Modeling and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:04:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extremely large antenna arrays and high-frequency operation are two key technologies that advance performance metrics such as higher data rates, lower latency, and wider coverage in sixth-generation communications. However, the adoption of these technologies fundamentally changes the characteristics of wavefronts, forcing communication systems to operate in the near-field region. The transition from planar far-field communications to spherical near-field propagation necessitates novel channel estimation algorithms to fully exploit the unique features of spherical wavefronts for advanced transceiver design. To this end, we propose a novel semi-gridless channel estimation approach based on a variational Bayesian (VB) inference framework. Specifically, we reformulate the near-field channel model for both uniform linear arrays and uniform planar arrays into separate direction-of-arrival (DoAs) and distance components. Building on these new representations, we employ a gridless approach for DoAs estimation using a von Mises distribution, and a coarse-to-fine grid search for distance estimation. We then develop a semi-gridless variational Bayesian (SG-VB) algorithm with efficient update rules that enables accurate channel reconstruction. Simulation results validate the effectiveness of the proposed SG-VB algorithm, demonstrating enhanced near-field channel reconstruction accuracy and superior estimation performance for both DoAs and distance components embedded in near-field channels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linheng","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Linheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilja","familyName":"Kröker","name":"Kröker, Ilja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Oladyshkin","name":"Oladyshkin, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Rybak","name":"Rybak, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate-assisted global sensitivity analysis of a hybrid-dimensional Stokes--Brinkman--Darcy model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Q35, 65C20, 76D07, 76M12, 76S05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:54:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Development of new multiscale mathematical models often entails considerable complexity and multiple undetermined parameters, typically arising from closure relations. To enable reliable simulations, one must quantify how uncertain physical parameters influence model predictions. We propose surrogate-assisted global sensitivity analysis that combines computational efficiency with a rigorous assessment of parameter influence. In this work, we analyze the recently proposed hybrid-dimensional Stokes--Brinkman--Darcy model, which describes fluid flows in coupled free-flow and porous-medium systems with arbitrary flow directions at the fluid--porous interface. The model results from vertical averaging and contains several unknown parameters. We perform surrogate-assisted global sensitivity analysis using Sobol' indices to investigate the sensitivity of the model to variations of physical parameters for two test cases: filtration and splitting flows. However, constructing surrogates for higher-dimensional random fields requires either many training runs or sophisticated sampling strategies. To address this, we compare polynomial chaos surrogates, including sparse and multi-resolution representations, for their efficiency in global sensitivity analysis, using a predefined Sobol' sequence of training samples. Across the tested cases, multi-resolution approach delivers the most accurate estimation of Sobol' indices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:29Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjeet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Inderjeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Pahuja","name":"Pahuja, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishvariya Priya Rathina","familyName":"Sabapathy","name":"Sabapathy, Aishvariya Priya Rathina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiara","familyName":"Picardi","name":"Picardi, Chiara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Giloni","name":"Giloni, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Vainshtein","name":"Vainshtein, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"Murillo","name":"Murillo, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hisashi","familyName":"Kojima","name":"Kojima, Hisashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Motoyoshi","familyName":"Sekiya","name":"Sekiya, Motoyoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Unno","name":"Unno, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junichi","familyName":"Suga","name":"Suga, Junichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Intent is a Latent Variable: Stateful Trust Inference for Securing Multimodal Agentic RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:52:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current stateless defences for multimodal agentic RAG fail to detect adversarial strategies that distribute malicious semantics across retrieval, planning, and generation components. We formulate this security challenge as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP), where adversarial intent is a latent variable inferred from noisy multi-stage observations. We introduce MMA-RAG^T, an inference-time control framework governed by a Modular Trust Agent (MTA) that maintains an approximate belief state via structured LLM reasoning. Operating as a model-agnostic overlay, MMA-RAGT mediates a configurable set of internal checkpoints to enforce stateful defence-in-depth. Extensive evaluation on 43,774 instances demonstrates a 6.50x average reduction factor in Attack Success Rate relative to undefended baselines, with negligible utility cost. Crucially, a factorial ablation validates our theoretical bounds: while statefulness and spatial coverage are individually necessary (26.4 pp and 13.6 pp gains respectively), stateless multi-point intervention can yield zero marginal benefit under homogeneous stateless filtering when checkpoint detections are perfectly correlated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 2 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helen","familyName":"Jenne","name":"Jenne, Helen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Vargas","name":"Vargas, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davis","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Davis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Mishne","name":"Mishne, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yusu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Kvinge","name":"Kvinge, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent field of neural algorithmic reasoning (NAR) studies the ability of graph neural networks (GNNs) to emulate classical algorithms like Bellman-Ford, a phenomenon known as algorithmic alignment. At the same time, recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have spawned the study of mechanistic interpretability, which aims to identify granular model components like circuits that perform specific computations. In this work, we introduce Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning (MINAR), an efficient circuit discovery toolbox that adapts attribution patching methods from mechanistic interpretability to the GNN setting. We show through two case studies that MINAR recovers faithful neuron-level circuits from GNNs trained on algorithmic tasks. Our study sheds new light on the process of circuit formation and pruning during training, as well as giving new insight into how GNNs trained to perform multiple tasks in parallel reuse circuit components for related tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/pnnl/MINAR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyi","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Weiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhigang","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Zhigang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Causal Decoding for Hallucination-Resistant Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:35:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) deliver detailed responses on vision-language tasks, yet remain susceptible to object hallucination (introducing objects not present in the image), undermining reliability in practice. Prior efforts often rely on heuristic penalties, post-hoc correction, or generic decoding tweaks, which do not directly intervene in the mechanisms that trigger object hallucination and thus yield limited gains. To address this challenge, we propose a causal decoding framework that applies targeted causal interventions during generation to curb spurious object mentions. By reshaping the decoding dynamics to attenuate spurious dependencies, our approach reduces false object tokens while maintaining descriptive quality. Across captioning and QA benchmarks, our framework substantially lowers object-hallucination rates and achieves state-of-the-art faithfulness without degrading overall output quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanda","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hejian","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Hejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overconfident Errors Need Stronger Correction: Asymmetric Confidence Penalties for Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has become the leading paradigm for enhancing reasoning in Large Language Models (LLMs). However, standard RLVR algorithms suffer from a well-documented pathology: while they improve Pass@1 accuracy through sharpened sampling, they simultaneously narrow the model's reasoning boundary and reduce generation diversity. We identify a root cause that existing methods overlook: the uniform penalization of errors. Current approaches -- whether data-filtering methods that select prompts by difficulty, or advantage normalization schemes -- treat all incorrect rollouts within a group identically. We show that this uniformity allows overconfident errors (incorrect reasoning paths that the RL process has spuriously reinforced) to persist and monopolize probability mass, ultimately suppressing valid exploratory trajectories. To address this, we propose the Asymmetric Confidence-aware Error Penalty (ACE). ACE introduces a per-rollout confidence shift metric, c_i = log(pi_theta(y_i|x) / pi_ref(y_i|x)), to dynamically modulate negative advantages. Theoretically, we demonstrate that ACE's gradient can be decomposed into the gradient of a selective regularizer restricted to overconfident errors, plus a well-characterized residual that partially moderates the regularizer's strength. We conduct extensive experiments fine-tuning Qwen2.5-Math-7B, Qwen3-8B-Base, and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on the DAPO-Math-17K dataset using GRPO and DAPO within the VERL framework. Evaluated on MATH-500 and AIME 2025, ACE composes seamlessly with existing methods and consistently improves the full Pass@k spectrum across all three model families and benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadsaleh","familyName":"Razmi","name":"Razmi, Mohammadsaleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Shojaei","name":"Shojaei, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Event-Driven On-Sensor Locomotion Mode Recognition Using a Shank-Mounted IMU with Embedded Machine Learning for Exoskeleton Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; C.3; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:45:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents a wearable human activity recognition (HAR) system that performs real-time inference directly inside a shank-mounted inertial measurement unit (IMU) to support low-latency control of a lower-limb exoskeleton. Unlike conventional approaches that continuously stream raw inertial data to a microcontroller for classification, the proposed system executes activity recognition at the sensor level using the embedded Machine Learning Core (MLC) of the STMicroelectronics LSM6DSV16X IMU, allowing the host microcontroller to remain in a low-power state and read only the recognized activity label from IMU registers. While the system generalizes to multiple human activities, this paper focuses on three representative locomotion modes - stance, level walking, and stair ascent - using data collected from adult participants. A lightweight decision-tree model was configured and deployed for on-sensor execution using ST MEMS Studio, enabling continuous operation without custom machine learning code on the microcontroller. During operation, the IMU asserts an interrupt when motion or a new classification is detected; the microcontroller wakes, reads the MLC output registers, and forwards the inferred mode to the exoskeleton controller. This interrupt-driven, on-sensor inference architecture reduces computation and communication overhead while preserving battery energy and improving robustness in distinguishing level walking from stair ascent for torque-assist control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures. Sensor-level HAR using embedded IMU machine learning for wearable robotics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhentian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhentian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Friede","name":"Friede, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Mathes","name":"Mathes, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Identifying the potential of sample overlap in evidence synthesis of observational studies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62P10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sample overlap is a common issue in evidence synthesis in the field of medical research, particularly when integrating findings from observational studies utilizing existing databases such as registries. Due to the general inaccessibility of unique identifiers for each observation, addressing sample overlap has been a complex problem, potentially biasing evidence synthesis outcomes and undermining their credibility. We developed a method to construct indicators for the degree of sample overlap in evidence synthesis of studies based on existing data. Our method is rooted in set theory and is based on the coding of the ranges of several well selected sample characteristics, offers a practical solution by focusing on making inference based on sample characteristics rather than on individual participant data. Useful information, such as the overlap-free sample set with the largest sample size in an evidence synthesis, can be derived from this method. We applied our model to several real-world evidence syntheses, demonstrating its effectiveness and flexibility. Our findings highlight the growing importance of addressing sample overlap in evidence synthesis, especially with the increasing relevance of secondary use of data, an area currently under-explored in research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages,17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:34Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Polson","name":"Polson, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vadim","familyName":"Sokolov","name":"Sokolov, Vadim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative Bayesian Computation as a Scalable Alternative to Gaussian Process Surrogates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:29:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gaussian process (GP) surrogates are the default tool for emulating expensive computer experiments, but cubic cost, stationarity assumptions, and Gaussian predictive distributions limit their reach. We propose Generative Bayesian Computation (GBC) via Implicit Quantile Networks (IQNs) as a surrogate framework that targets all three limitations. GBC learns the full conditional quantile function from input--output pairs; at test time, a single forward pass per quantile level produces draws from the predictive distribution.\n Across fourteen benchmarks we compare GBC to four GP-based methods. GBC improves CRPS by 11--26\\% on piecewise jump-process benchmarks, by 14\\% on a ten-dimensional Friedman function, and scales linearly to 90,000 training points where dense-covariance GPs are infeasible. A boundary-augmented variant matches or outperforms Modular Jump GPs on two-dimensional jump datasets (up to 46\\% CRPS improvement). In active learning, a randomized-prior IQN ensemble achieves nearly three times lower RMSE than deep GP active learning on Rocket LGBB. Overall, GBC records a favorable point estimate in 12 of 14 comparisons. GPs retain an edge on smooth surfaces where their smoothness prior provides effective regularization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21406","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21406","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21406","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asim","familyName":"Unmesh","name":"Unmesh, Asim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaki","familyName":"Ramesh","name":"Ramesh, Kaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Ramani","name":"Ramani, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Vision-Language Models for Open-Vocabulary Zero-Shot Action Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:23:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Temporal Action Segmentation (TAS) requires dividing videos into action segments, yet the vast space of activities and alternative breakdowns makes collecting comprehensive datasets infeasible. Existing methods remain limited to closed vocabularies and fixed label sets. In this work, we explore the largely unexplored problem of Open-Vocabulary Zero-Shot Temporal Action Segmentation (OVTAS) by leveraging the strong zero-shot capabilities of Vision-Language Models (VLMs). We introduce a training-free pipeline that follows a segmentation-by-classification design: Frame-Action Embedding Similarity (FAES) matches video frames to candidate action labels, and Similarity-Matrix Temporal Segmentation (SMTS) enforces temporal consistency. Beyond proposing OVTAS, we present a systematic study across 14 diverse VLMs, providing the first broad analysis of their suitability for open-vocabulary action segmentation. Experiments on standard benchmarks show that OVTAS achieves strong results without task-specific supervision, underscoring the potential of VLMs for structured temporal understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21406","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael C","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Michael C","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alen","familyName":"Zacharia","name":"Zacharia, Alen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An information-based model selection criterion for data-driven model discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:45:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data-driven model discovery (DDMD) algorithms are powerful tools for extracting interpretable symbolic models from data. However, identifying the model that best balances goodness-of-fit and sparsity is often a laborious process requiring user fine-tuning, is prone to overfitting, and results may significantly vary depending on model initialization and specific training procedure. Here, we present a sparse regression algorithm that automatically and adaptively generates candidate models, and uses a novel sample-length-scaling logarithmic information criterion (SLIC) to identify the best model from these candidates. We demonstrate that SLIC greatly outperforms other popular information criteria in extracting the correct model from the data of several nonlinear ordinary and partial differential equations. Then, we demonstrate SLIC's ability to discover interpretable models from experimental datasets in fluid dynamics and nanotechnology that generate new testable predictions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21387","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21387","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21387","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigory","familyName":"Koroteev","name":"Koroteev, Grigory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Natural Qubit Algebra: clarification of the Clifford boundary and new non-embeddability theorem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:30:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Natural Qubit Algebra (NQA), a compact real operator calculus for qubit systems based on a $2\\times2$ block alphabet $\\{I,X,Z,W\\}\\subset\\mathrm{Mat}(2,\\mathbb{R})$ and tensor-word representations. The resulting multiplication law induces a canonical $(\\mathbb{Z}_2)^{2m}$-grading with a bicharacter that controls commutation signs, placing the framework naturally within the theory of color-graded and Clifford-type algebras.\n Within this language, we provide: (i) an explicit real Clifford normal form for two-qubit operators via the identification $\\mathrm{Mat}(4,\\mathbb{R})\\cong\\mathrm{Cl}(2,2;\\mathbb{R})$; (ii) a purely algebraic reformulation of the Bell--CHSH scenario, where the quantum violation is expressed as a spectral non-embeddability of a noncommutative spinor algebra into any commutative Kolmogorov algebra; and (iii) compact factored representations of the Bernstein--Vazirani and Grover phase oracles, showing that both Clifford and non-Clifford examples can admit similarly structured symbolic descriptions.\n We clarify that Grover's iterate remains outside the Clifford group due to its continuous spectral rotation, consistent with the Gottesman--Knill theorem, while retaining a compact tensor-block form in NQA. The framework isolates spectral, algebraic, and syntactic aspects of operator structure, providing a graded operator language compatible with standard quantum mechanics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21387","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Gogolev","name":"Gogolev, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Sickert","name":"Sickert, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Denzler","name":"Denzler, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Subtokens: A Rich Character Embedding for Low-resource and Morphologically Complex Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.5.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tokenization and sub-tokenization based models like word2vec, BERT and the GPTs are the state-of-the-art in natural language processing. Typically, these approaches have limitations with respect to their input representation. They fail to fully capture orthographic similarities and morphological variations, especially in highly inflected and under-resource languages. To mitigate this problem, we propose to computes word vectors directly from character strings, integrating both semantic and syntactic information. We denote this transformer-based approach Rich Character Embeddings (RCE). Furthermore, we propose a hybrid model that combines transformer and convolutional mechanisms. Both vector representations can be used as a drop-in replacement for dictionary- and subtoken-based word embeddings in existing model architectures. It has the potential to improve performance for both large context-based language models like BERT and small models like word2vec for under-resourced and morphologically rich languages. We evaluate our approach on various tasks like the SWAG, declension prediction for inflected languages, metaphor and chiasmus detection for various languages. Our experiments show that it outperforms traditional token-based approaches on limited data using OddOneOut and TopK metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 content pages, 2 figures, 8 tables, one example textbox"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21374","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21374","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21374","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Ghaffarzadeh-Esfahani","name":"Ghaffarzadeh-Esfahani, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nahid","familyName":"Yousefian","name":"Yousefian, Nahid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ebrahim","familyName":"Heidari-Farsani","name":"Heidari-Farsani, Ebrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Akbar","familyName":"Omidvarian","name":"Omidvarian, Ali Akbar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr","familyName":"Ghahraei","name":"Ghahraei, Sepehr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atena","familyName":"Farangi","name":"Farangi, Atena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"AmirBahador","familyName":"Boroumand","name":"Boroumand, AmirBahador","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Language Models for Privacy-Preserving Clinical Information Extraction in Low-Resource Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extracting clinical information from medical transcripts in low-resource languages remains a significant challenge in healthcare natural language processing (NLP). This study evaluates a two-step pipeline combining Aya-expanse-8B as a Persian-to-English translation model with five open-source small language models (SLMs) -- Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, and Gemma-3-1B-it -- for binary extraction of 13 clinical features from 1,221 anonymized Persian transcripts collected at a cancer palliative care call center. Using a few-shot prompting strategy without fine-tuning, models were assessed on macro-averaged F1-score, Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), sensitivity, and specificity to account for class imbalance. Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct achieved the highest overall performance (median macro-F1: 0.899; MCC: 0.797), while Gemma-3-1B-it showed the weakest results. Larger models (7B--8B parameters) consistently outperformed smaller counterparts in sensitivity and MCC. A bilingual analysis of Aya-expanse-8B revealed that translating Persian transcripts to English improved sensitivity, reduced missing outputs, and boosted metrics robust to class imbalance, though at the cost of slightly lower specificity and precision. Feature-level results showed reliable extraction of physiological symptoms across most models, whereas psychological complaints, administrative requests, and complex somatic features remained challenging. These findings establish a practical, privacy-preserving blueprint for deploying open-source SLMs in multilingual clinical NLP settings with limited infrastructure and annotation resources, and highlight the importance of jointly optimizing model scale and input language strategy for sensitive healthcare applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 3 figures, 2 supplementary files"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21374","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameer","familyName":"Ambekar","name":"Ambekar, Sameer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Nasirigerdeh","name":"Nasirigerdeh, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter J.","familyName":"Schuffler","name":"Schuffler, Peter J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lina","familyName":"Felsner","name":"Felsner, Lina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel M.","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Daniel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia A.","familyName":"Schnabel","name":"Schnabel, Julia A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Mean is the Mirage: Entropy-Adaptive Model Merging under Heterogeneous Domain Shifts in Medical Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:06:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model merging under unseen test-time distribution shifts often renders naive strategies, such as mean averaging unreliable. This challenge is especially acute in medical imaging, where models are fine-tuned locally at clinics on private data, producing domain-specific models that differ by scanner, protocol, and population. When deployed at an unseen clinical site, test cases arrive in unlabeled, non-i.i.d. batches, and the model must adapt immediately without labels. In this work, we introduce an entropy-adaptive, fully online model-merging method that yields a batch-specific merged model via only forward passes, effectively leveraging target information. We further demonstrate why mean merging is prone to failure and misaligned under heterogeneous domain shifts. Next, we mitigate encoder classifier mismatch by decoupling the encoder and classification head, merging with separate merging coefficients. We extensively evaluate our method with state-of-the-art baselines using two backbones across nine medical and natural-domain generalization image classification datasets, showing consistent gains across standard evaluation and challenging scenarios. These performance gains are achieved while retaining single-model inference at test-time, thereby demonstrating the effectiveness of our method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Surya","familyName":"Duvvuri","name":"Duvvuri, Sai Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanakya","familyName":"Ekbote","name":"Ekbote, Chanakya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Rachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devvrit","familyName":"Khatri","name":"Khatri, Devvrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Brandfonbrener","name":"Brandfonbrener, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjit","familyName":"Dhillon","name":"Dhillon, Inderjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manzil","familyName":"Zaheer","name":"Zaheer, Manzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interleaved Head Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:05:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-Head Attention (MHA) is the core computational primitive underlying modern Large Language Models (LLMs). However, MHA suffers from a fundamental linear scaling limitation: $H$ attention heads produce exactly $H$ independent attention matrices, with no communication between heads during attention computation. This becomes problematic for multi-step reasoning, where correct answers depend on aggregating evidence from multiple parts of the context and composing latent token-to-token relations over a chain of intermediate inferences. To address this, we propose Interleaved Head Attention (IHA), which enables cross-head mixing by constructing $P$ pseudo-heads per head (typically $P=H$), where each pseudo query/key/value is a learned linear combination of all $H$ original queries, keys and values respectively. Interactions between pseudo-query and pseudo-key heads induce up to $P^2$ attention patterns per head with modest parameter overhead $\\mathcal{O}(H^2P)$. We provide theory showing improved efficiency in terms of number of parameters on the synthetic Polynomial task (IHA uses $Θ(\\sqrt{k}n^2)$ parameters vs. $Θ(kn^2)$ for MHA) and on the synthetic order-sensitive CPM-3 task (IHA uses $\\lceil\\sqrt{N_{\\max}}\\rceil$ heads vs. $N_{\\max}$ for MHA). On real-world benchmarks, IHA improves Multi-Key retrieval on RULER by 10-20% (4k-16k) and, after fine-tuning for reasoning on OpenThoughts, improves GSM8K by 5.8% and MATH-500 by 2.8% (Majority Vote) over full attention."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charafeddine","familyName":"Mouzouni","name":"Mouzouni, Charafeddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Black-Box Reliability Certification for AI Agents via Self-Consistency Sampling and Conformal Calibration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:03:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a black-box AI system and a task, at what confidence level can a practitioner trust the system's output? We answer with a reliability level -- a single number per system-task pair, derived from self-consistency sampling and conformal calibration, that serves as a black-box deployment gate with exact, finite-sample, distribution-free guarantees. Self-consistency sampling reduces uncertainty exponentially; conformal calibration guarantees correctness within 1/(n+1) of the target level, regardless of the system's errors -- made transparently visible through larger answer sets for harder questions. Weaker models earn lower reliability levels (not accuracy -- see Definition 2.4): GPT-4.1 earns 94.6% on GSM8K and 96.8% on TruthfulQA, while GPT-4.1-nano earns 89.8% on GSM8K and 66.5% on MMLU. We validate across five benchmarks, five models from three families, and both synthetic and real data. Conditional coverage on solvable items exceeds 0.93 across all configurations; sequential stopping reduces API costs by around 50%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 11 figures, 10 tables, including appendices"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Pantiukhin","name":"Pantiukhin, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Kuznetsov","name":"Kuznetsov, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Shapkin","name":"Shapkin, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonia Anna","familyName":"Jost","name":"Jost, Antonia Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolay","familyName":"Koldunov","name":"Koldunov, Nikolay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Hierarchical Multi-Agent System for Autonomous Discovery in Geoscientific Data Archives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:37:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid accumulation of Earth science data has created a significant scalability challenge; while repositories like PANGAEA host vast collections of datasets, citation metrics indicate that a substantial portion remains underutilized, limiting data reusability. Here we present PANGAEA-GPT, a hierarchical multi-agent framework designed for autonomous data discovery and analysis. Unlike standard Large Language Model (LLM) wrappers, our architecture implements a centralized Supervisor-Worker topology with strict data-type-aware routing, sandboxed deterministic code execution, and self-correction via execution feedback, enabling agents to diagnose and resolve runtime errors. Through use-case scenarios spanning physical oceanography and ecology, we demonstrate the system's capacity to execute complex, multi-step workflows with minimal human intervention. This framework provides a methodology for querying and analyzing heterogeneous repository data through coordinated agent workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables, supplementary material included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Mengxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek V.","familyName":"Datla","name":"Datla, Vivek V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alignment-Weighted DPO: A principled reasoning approach to improve safety alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in alignment techniques such as Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) have improved the safety of large language models (LLMs). However, these LLMs remain vulnerable to jailbreak attacks that disguise harmful intent through indirect or deceptive phrasing. Using causal intervention, we empirically demonstrate that this vulnerability stems from shallow alignment mechanisms that lack deep reasoning, often rejecting harmful prompts without truly understanding why they are harmful. To mitigate this vulnerability, we propose enhancing alignment through reasoning-aware post-training. We construct and release a novel Chain-of-Thought (CoT) fine-tuning dataset that includes both utility-oriented and safety-critical prompts with step-by-step rationales. Fine-tuning on this dataset encourages models to produce principled refusals grounded in reasoning, outperforming standard SFT baselines. Furthermore, inspired by failure patterns in CoT fine-tuning, we introduce Alignment-Weighted DPO, which targets the most problematic parts of an output by assigning different preference weights to the reasoning and final-answer segments. This produces finer-grained, targeted updates than vanilla DPO and improves robustness to diverse jailbreak strategies. Extensive experiments across multiple safety and utility benchmarks show that our method consistently improves alignment robustness while maintaining overall model utility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21345","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21345","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21345","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhui","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Minhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yongheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David S.","familyName":"Lalush","name":"Lalush, David S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason P","familyName":"Mihalik","name":"Mihalik, Jason P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pew-Thian","familyName":"Yap","name":"Yap, Pew-Thian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RelA-Diffusion: Relativistic Adversarial Diffusion for Multi-Tracer PET Synthesis from Multi-Sequence MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:30:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-tracer positron emission tomography (PET) provides critical insights into diverse neuropathological processes such as tau accumulation, neuroinflammation, and $β$-amyloid deposition in the brain, making it indispensable for comprehensive neurological assessment. However, routine acquisition of multi-tracer PET is limited by high costs, radiation exposure, and restricted tracer availability. Recent efforts have explored deep learning approaches for synthesizing PET images from structural MRI. While some methods rely solely on T1-weighted MRI, others incorporate additional sequences such as T2-FLAIR to improve pathological sensitivity. However, existing methods often struggle to capture fine-grained anatomical and pathological details, resulting in artifacts and unrealistic outputs. To this end, we propose RelA-Diffusion, a Relativistic Adversarial Diffusion framework for multi-tracer PET synthesis from multi-sequence MRI. By leveraging both T1-weighted and T2-FLAIR scans as complementary inputs, RelA-Diffusion captures richer structural information to guide image generation. To improve synthesis fidelity, we introduce a gradient-penalized relativistic adversarial loss to the intermediate clean predictions of the diffusion model. This loss compares real and generated images in a relative manner, encouraging the synthesis of more realistic local structures. Both the relativistic formulation and the gradient penalty contribute to stabilizing the training, while adversarial feedback at each diffusion timestep enables consistent refinement throughout the generation process. Extensive experiments on two datasets demonstrate that RelA-Diffusion outperforms existing methods in both visual fidelity and quantitative metrics, highlighting its potential for accurate synthesis of multi-tracer PET."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21345","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo","familyName":"Ryu","name":"Ryu, Hyunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas W.","familyName":"Mitchel","name":"Mitchel, Thomas W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Sitzmann","name":"Sitzmann, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling View Synthesis Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:18:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Geometry-free view synthesis transformers have recently achieved state-of-the-art performance in Novel View Synthesis (NVS), outperforming traditional approaches that rely on explicit geometry modeling. Yet the factors governing their scaling with compute remain unclear. We present a systematic study of scaling laws for view synthesis transformers and derive design principles for training compute-optimal NVS models. Contrary to prior findings, we show that encoder-decoder architectures can be compute-optimal; we trace earlier negative results to suboptimal architectural choices and comparisons across unequal training compute budgets. Across several compute levels, we demonstrate that our encoder-decoder architecture, which we call the Scalable View Synthesis Model (SVSM), scales as effectively as decoder-only models, achieves a superior performance-compute Pareto frontier, and surpasses the previous state-of-the-art on real-world NVS benchmarks with substantially reduced training compute."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://www.evn.kim/research/svsm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21327","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21327","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21327","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elbert","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Elbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia","familyName":"Dwork","name":"Dwork, Cynthia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lunjia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Lunjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reid","familyName":"McIlroy-Young","name":"McIlroy-Young, Reid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equitable Evaluation via Elicitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Individuals with similar qualifications and skills may vary in their demeanor, or outward manner: some tend toward self-promotion while others are modest to the point of omitting crucial information. Comparing the self-descriptions of equally qualified job-seekers with different self-presentation styles is therefore problematic.\n We build an interactive AI for skill elicitation that provides accurate determination of skills while simultaneously allowing individuals to speak in their own voice. Such a system can be deployed, for example, when a new user joins a professional networking platform, or when matching employees to needs during a company reorganization. To obtain sufficient training data, we train an LLM to act as synthetic humans.\n Elicitation mitigates endogenous bias arising from individuals' own self-reports. To address systematic model bias we enforce a mathematically rigorous notion of equitability ensuring that the covariance between self-presentation manner and skill evaluation error is small."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21327","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21320","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21320","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21320","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre Can","familyName":"Acikgoz","name":"Acikgoz, Emre Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Hübotter","name":"Hübotter, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dilek","familyName":"Hakkani-Tür","name":"Hakkani-Tür, Dilek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gokhan","familyName":"Tur","name":"Tur, Gokhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tool-R0: Self-Evolving LLM Agents for Tool-Learning from Zero Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:41:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming the foundation for autonomous agents that can use tools to solve complex tasks. Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a common approach for injecting such agentic capabilities, but typically under tightly controlled training setups. It often depends on carefully constructed task-solution pairs and substantial human supervision, which creates a fundamental obstacle to open-ended self-evolution toward superintelligent systems. In this paper, we propose Tool-R0 framework for training general purpose tool-calling agents from scratch with self-play RL, under a zero-data assumption. Initialized from the same base LLM, Tool-R0 co-evolves a Generator and a Solver with complementary rewards: one proposes targeted challenging tasks at the other's competence frontier and the other learns to solve them with real-world tool calls. This creates a self-evolving cycle that requires no pre-existing tasks or datasets. Evaluation on different tool-use benchmarks show that Tool-R0 yields 92.5 relative improvement over the base model and surpasses fully supervised tool-calling baselines under the same setting. Our work further provides empirical insights into self-play LLM agents by analyzing co-evolution, curriculum dynamics, and scaling behavior."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21320","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:21Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21319","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21319","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21319","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marion","familyName":"Neumeier","name":"Neumeier, Marion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Roßberg","name":"Roßberg, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Botsch","name":"Botsch, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang","familyName":"Utschick","name":"Utschick, Wolfgang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-Aware Diffusion Model for Multimodal Highway Trajectory Prediction via DDIM Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:40:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate and uncertainty-aware trajectory prediction remains a core challenge for autonomous driving, driven by complex multi-agent interactions, diverse scene contexts and the inherently stochastic nature of future motion. Diffusion-based generative models have recently shown strong potential for capturing multimodal futures, yet existing approaches such as cVMD suffer from slow sampling, limited exploitation of generative diversity and brittle scenario encodings.\n This work introduces cVMDx, an enhanced diffusion-based trajectory prediction framework that improves efficiency, robustness and multimodal predictive capability. Through DDIM sampling, cVMDx achieves up to a 100x reduction in inference time, enabling practical multi-sample generation for uncertainty estimation. A fitted Gaussian Mixture Model further provides tractable multimodal predictions from the generated trajectories. In addition, a CVQ-VAE variant is evaluated for scenario encoding. Experiments on the publicly available highD dataset show that cVMDx achieves higher accuracy and significantly improved efficiency over cVMD, enabling fully stochastic, multimodal trajectory prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a conference paper in IEEE Intelligent Vehicles Symposium (IV) 2026, Detroit, MI, United States"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21319","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guancheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Guancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diji","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Diji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shared Nature, Unique Nurture: PRISM for Pluralistic Reasoning via In-context Structure Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:38:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are converging towards a singular Artificial Hivemind, where shared Nature (pre-training priors) result in a profound collapse of distributional diversity, limiting the distinct perspectives necessary for creative exploration and scientific discovery. To address this, we propose to equip models with inference-time Nurture (individualized epistemic trajectories) using Epistemic Evolution paradigm, progressing through explore, internalize, and express. We instantiate this via PRISM (Pluralistic Reasoning via In-context Structure Modeling), a model-agnostic system that augments LLM with dynamic On-the-fly Epistemic Graphs. On three creativity benchmarks, PRISM achieves state-of-the-art novelty and significantly expands distributional diversity. Moreover, we evaluate the real-world utility via a challenging rare-disease diagnosis benchmark. Results demonstrate that PRISM successfully uncovers correct long-tail diagnoses that standard LLM miss, confirming that its divergence stems from meaningful exploration rather than incoherent noise. Overall, this work establishes a new paradigm for Pluralistic AI, moving beyond monolithic consensus toward a diverse ecosystem of unique cognitive individuals capable of collective, multi-perspective discovery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Khalaf","name":"Khalaf, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena L.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Serena L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Halpern","name":"Halpern, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itai","familyName":"Shapira","name":"Shapira, Itai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Flavio du Pin","familyName":"Calmon","name":"Calmon, Flavio du Pin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel D.","familyName":"Procaccia","name":"Procaccia, Ariel D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust AI Evaluation through Maximal Lotteries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:01:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The standard way to evaluate language models on subjective tasks is through pairwise comparisons: an annotator chooses the \"better\" of two responses to a prompt. Leaderboards aggregate these comparisons into a single Bradley-Terry (BT) ranking, forcing heterogeneous preferences into a total order and violating basic social-choice desiderata. In contrast, social choice theory provides an alternative approach called maximal lotteries, which aggregates pairwise preferences without imposing any assumptions on their structure. However, we show that maximal lotteries are highly sensitive to preference heterogeneity and can favor models that severely underperform on specific tasks or user subpopulations. We introduce robust lotteries that optimize worst-case performance under plausible shifts in the preference data. On large-scale preference datasets, robust lotteries provide more reliable win rate guarantees across the annotator distribution and recover a stable set of top-performing models. By moving from rankings to pluralistic sets of winners, robust lotteries offer a principled step toward an ecosystem of complementary AI systems that serve the full spectrum of human preferences."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianneng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jianneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre V.","familyName":"Morozov","name":"Morozov, Alexandre V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural network optimization strategies and the topography of the loss landscape"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.1.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T17:49:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural networks are trained by optimizing multi-dimensional sets of fitting parameters on non-convex loss landscapes. Low-loss regions of the landscapes correspond to the parameter sets that perform well on the training data. A key issue in machine learning is the performance of trained neural networks on previously unseen test data. Here, we investigate neural network training by stochastic gradient descent (SGD) - a non-convex global optimization algorithm which relies only on the gradient of the objective function. We contrast SGD solutions with those obtained via a non-stochastic quasi-Newton method, which utilizes curvature information to determine step direction and Golden Section Search to choose step size. We use several computational tools to investigate neural network parameters obtained by these two optimization methods, including kernel Principal Component Analysis and a novel, general-purpose algorithm for finding low-height paths between pairs of points on loss or energy landscapes, FourierPathFinder. We find that the choice of the optimizer profoundly affects the nature of the resulting solutions. SGD solutions tend to be separated by lower barriers than quasi-Newton solutions, even if both sets of solutions are regularized by early stopping to ensure adequate performance on test data. When allowed to fit extensively on the training data, quasi-Newton solutions occupy deeper minima on the loss landscapes that are not reached by SGD. These solutions are less generalizable to the test data however. Overall, SGD explores smooth basins of attraction, while quasi-Newton optimization is capable of finding deeper, more isolated minima that are more spread out in the parameter space. Our findings help understand both the topography of the loss landscapes and the fundamental role of landscape exploration strategies in creating robust, transferrable neural network models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages in the main text + 5 pages in the supplement. 6 figures + 1 table in the main text, 4 figures and 1 table in the supplement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moe","familyName":"Putterman","name":"Putterman, Moe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehtaab","familyName":"Sawhney","name":"Sawhney, Mehtaab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Valiant","name":"Valiant, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On infinite sets with no $3$ on a line"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:54:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We give a construction of an infinite set of points $A$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ such that any subset $P\\subseteq A$ has a constant density subset $P'$ with no three points collinear and yet $A$ cannot be separated into finitely many subsets such that each subset has no three points collinear. This provides a new proof of a question of Erdős, Nešetřil, and Rödl. The construction was generated by an internal model at OpenAI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Zixian","name":"Zixian, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group Orthogonalized Policy Optimization:Group Policy Optimization as Orthogonal Projection in Hilbert Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T12:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Group Orthogonalized Policy Optimization (GOPO), a new alignment algorithm for large language models derived from the geometry of Hilbert function spaces. Instead of optimizing on the probability simplex and inheriting the exponential curvature of Kullback-Leibler divergence, GOPO lifts alignment into the Hilbert space L2(pi_k) of square-integrable functions with respect to the reference policy. Within this space, the simplex constraint reduces to a linear orthogonality condition = 0, defining a codimension-one subspace H0. Minimizing distance to an unconstrained target u_star yields the work-dissipation functional J(v) = - (mu / 2) ||v||^2, whose maximizer follows directly from the Hilbert projection theorem. Enforcing the boundary v \u0026gt;= -1 produces a bounded Hilbert projection that induces exact sparsity, assigning zero probability to catastrophically poor actions through a closed-form threshold. To connect this functional theory with practice, GOPO projects from infinite-dimensional L2(pi_k) to a finite empirical subspace induced by group sampling. Because group-normalized advantages sum to zero, the Lagrange multiplier enforcing probability conservation vanishes exactly, reducing the constrained projection to an unconstrained empirical loss. The resulting objective has constant Hessian curvature mu I, non-saturating linear gradients, and an intrinsic dead-zone mechanism without heuristic clipping. Experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks show that GOPO achieves competitive generalization while maintaining stable gradient dynamics and entropy preservation in regimes where clipping-based methods plateau."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo B.","familyName":"Grando","name":"Grando, Ricardo B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor A.","familyName":"Kich","name":"Kich, Victor A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alisson H.","familyName":"Kolling","name":"Kolling, Alisson H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junior C. D.","familyName":"Jesus","name":"Jesus, Junior C. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodrigo S.","familyName":"Guerra","name":"Guerra, Rodrigo S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo L. J.","familyName":"Drews-Jr","name":"Drews-Jr, Paulo L. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross domain Persistent Monitoring for Hybrid Aerial Underwater Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:56:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hybrid Unmanned Aerial Underwater Vehicles (HUAUVs) have emerged as platforms capable of operating in both aerial and underwater environments, enabling applications such as inspection, mapping, search, and rescue in challenging scenarios. However, the development of novel methodologies poses significant challenges due to the distinct dynamics and constraints of the air and water domains. In this work, we present persistent monitoring tasks for HUAUVs by combining Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) and Transfer Learning to enable cross-domain adaptability. Our approach employs a shared DRL architecture trained on Lidar sensor data (on air) and Sonar data (underwater), demonstrating the feasibility of a unified policy for both environments. We further show that the methodology presents promising results, taking into account the uncertainty of the environment and the dynamics of multiple mobile targets. The proposed framework lays the groundwork for scalable autonomous persistent monitoring solutions based on DRL for hybrid aerial-underwater vehicles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the Brazilian Conference on Robotics 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen G.","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Wen G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured Prompt Language: Declarative Context Management for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T17:03:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present SPL (Structured Prompt Language), a declarative SQL-inspired language that treats large language models as generative knowledge bases and their context windows as constrained resources. SPL provides explicit WITH BUDGET/LIMIT token management, an automatic query optimizer, EXPLAIN transparency analogous to SQL's EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and native integration of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and persistent memory in a single declarative framework. SPL-flow extends SPL into resilient agentic pipelines with a three-tier provider fallback strategy (Ollama -\u0026gt; OpenRouter -\u0026gt; self-healing retry) fully transparent to the .spl script. Five extensions demonstrate the paradigm's breadth: (1) Text2SPL (multilingual NL-\u0026gt;SPL translation); (2) Mixture-of-Models (MoM) routing that dispatches each PROMPT to a domain-specialist model at runtime; (3) Logical Chunking, an intelligent strategy for documents exceeding a single context window--expressed naturally through SPL's existing CTE syntax with no new constructs, decomposing a large query into a Map-Reduce pipeline that reduces attention cost from O(N^2) to O(N^2/k) and runs identically on cloud (parallel) or local hardware (sequential); (4) SPL-flow, a declarative agentic orchestration layer with resilient three-tier provider fallback; and (5) BENCHMARK for parallel multi-model comparison with automatic winner persistence. We provide a formal EBNF grammar, two pip-installable Python packages (spl-llm, spl-flow), and comparison against Prompty, DSPy, and LMQL. SPL reduces prompt boilerplate by 65% on average, surfaces a 68x cost spread across model tiers as a pre-execution signal, and runs the identical .spl script at $0.002 on OpenRouter or at zero marginal cost on a local Ollama instance--without modification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages, 6 figures, 14 tables, 15 code-listings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clemens","familyName":"Pohle","name":"Pohle, Clemens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgenticTyper: Automated Typing of Legacy Software Projects Using Agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.3; D.2.7; F.3.3; I.2.2; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:53:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Legacy JavaScript systems lack type safety, making maintenance risky. While TypeScript can help, manually adding types is expensive. Previous automated typing research focuses on type inference but rarely addresses type checking setup, definition generation, bug identification, or behavioral correctness at repository scale. We present AgenticTyper, a Large Language Model (LLM)-based agentic system that addresses these gaps through iterative error correction and behavior preservation via transpilation comparison. Evaluation on two proprietary repositories (81K LOC) shows that AgenticTyper resolves all 633 initial type errors in 20 minutes, reducing manual effort from one working day."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICSE 2026 Student Research Competition (SRC)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Roohi","name":"Roohi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad","familyName":"Shoja-Sani","name":"Shoja-Sani, Ahmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Stefanov","name":"Stefanov, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Constrained Neural Collision Operators for Variable Hard Sphere Surrogates and Ab Initio Angle Prediction in Direct Simulation Monte Carlo"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-15T03:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Direct Simulation Monte Carlo (DSMC) method is the gold standard for non-equilibrium rarefied gas dynamics, yet its computational cost can be prohibitive, especially for near-continuum regimes and high-fidelity \\emph{ab initio} potentials. This work develops a unified, physics-constrained neural-operator framework that accelerates DSMC while preserving physical invariants and stochasticity required for long-time kinetic simulations. First, we introduce a local neural collision kernel replacing the phenomenological Variable Hard Sphere (VHS) model. To overcome the variance suppression and artificial cooling inherent to purely deterministic regression surrogates, we augment inference with a physics-constrained stochastic layer. Controlled latent-noise injection restores thermal fluctuations, while cell-wise moment-matching strictly enforces momentum and kinetic-energy conservation. Remarkably, this operator exhibits zero-shot spatial and thermodynamic generalization: a model trained exclusively on 1D Couette flow accurately simulates a complex 2D lid-driven cavity, capturing high-order non-equilibrium moments without retraining.Second, to bypass the extreme cost of quantum-mechanical scattering, we develop a dedicated \\emph{ab initio} neural operator for the Jäger interaction potential. Trained via a \\emph{physics harvesting} strategy on large-scale collision pairs, it efficiently captures the high-energy scattering dynamics dominating hypersonic regimes. Validated on a Mach~10 rarefied argon flow over a cylinder, the framework reproduces transport behaviors and shock features with high fidelity, achieving an approximate 20\\% cost reduction relative to direct numerical integration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Migliarini","name":"Migliarini, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berat","familyName":"Ercevik","name":"Ercevik, Berat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwagbemike","familyName":"Olowe","name":"Olowe, Oluwagbemike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saira","familyName":"Fatima","name":"Fatima, Saira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh Anh","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Minh Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasu","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Vasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwinee","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Ashwinee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"@GrokSet: multi-party Human-LLM Interactions in Social Media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-11T12:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as active participants on public social media platforms, yet their behavior in these unconstrained social environments remains largely unstudied. Existing datasets, drawn primarily from private chat interfaces, lack the multi-party dynamics and public visibility crucial for understanding real-world performance. To address this gap, we introduce @GrokSet, a large-scale dataset of over 1 million tweets involving the @Grok LLM on X. Our analysis reveals a distinct functional shift: rather than serving as a general assistant, the LLM is frequently invoked as an authoritative arbiter in high-stakes, polarizing political debates. However, we observe a persistent engagement gap: despite this visibility, the model functions as a low-status utility, receiving significantly less social validation (likes, replies) than human peers. Finally, we find that this adversarial context exposes shallow alignment: users bypass safety filters not through complex jailbreaks, but through simple persona adoption and tone mirroring. We release @GrokSet as a critical resource for studying the intersection of AI agents and societal discourse."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramchand","familyName":"Kumaresan","name":"Kumaresan, Ramchand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACAR: Adaptive Complexity Routing for Multi-Model Ensembles with Auditable Decision Traces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-06T23:27:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ACAR (Adaptive Complexity and Attribution Routing), a measurement framework for studying multi-model orchestration under auditable conditions. ACAR uses self-consistency variance (sigma) computed from N=3 probe samples to route tasks across single-model, two-model, and three-model execution modes. The system is implemented on top of TEAMLLM, a deterministic execution substrate with immutable artifacts and complete decision traces. We evaluate ACAR on 1,510 tasks spanning four benchmarks: MathArena, Reasoning Gym, LiveCodeBench, and SuperGPQA, using Claude Sonnet 4, GPT-4o, and Gemini 2.0 Flash, producing more than 7,550 auditable runs. Results show that sigma-based routing achieves 55.6 percent accuracy, exceeding the two-model baseline of 54.4 percent while avoiding full ensembling on 54.2 percent of tasks. The routing mechanism is model-agnostic and requires no learned components. We also document negative results. First, retrieval augmentation reduced accuracy by 3.4 percentage points, as median retrieval similarity was only 0.167, demonstrating that experience injection without semantic alignment introduces noise rather than grounding. Second, when models agree on incorrect answers (sigma equals zero), no downstream ensemble can recover; this agreement-but-wrong failure mode is intrinsic to self-consistency and bounds achievable accuracy at approximately eight percentage points below full ensembling. Third, attribution estimates based on proxy signals such as response similarity and entropy showed weak correlation with ground-truth leave-one-out values, indicating that practical attribution requires explicit counterfactual computation. This work documents which assumptions fail in practice and provides falsifiable baselines for future research on routing, retrieval, and multi-model attribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures. Measurement framework for adaptive multi-model routing with auditable execution traces"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irwin","familyName":"King","name":"King, Irwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRACE: Trajectory-Aware Comprehensive Evaluation for Deep Research Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-05T13:28:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evaluation of Deep Research Agents is a critical challenge, as conventional outcome-based metrics fail to capture the nuances of their complex reasoning. Current evaluation faces two primary challenges: 1) a reliance on singular metrics like Pass@1, creating a \"high-score illusion\" that ignores the quality, efficiency, and soundness of the reasoning process; and 2) the failure of static benchmarks to quantify crucial attributes like robustness and latent capability. To address these gaps, we introduce TRACE (Trajectory-Aware Comprehensive Evaluation), a framework that holistically assesses the entire problem-solving trajectory. To counter the \"high-score illusion\", we propose a Hierarchical Trajectory Utility Function that quantifies process efficiency and cognitive quality, including evidence grounding, alongside accuracy. To measure deeper attributes, TRACE introduces a Scaffolded Capability Assessment protocol, quantifying an agent's latent ability by determining the minimum guidance needed for success. Our contributions include the TRACE framework, its novel metrics, and the accompanying DeepResearch-Bench with controllable complexity. Experiments show TRACE delivers a granular ranking that uncovers critical trade-offs between agent accuracy, efficiency, and robustness entirely missed by singular metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21227","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21227","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21227","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Caiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Menglin","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Menglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Madrigal","name":"Madrigal, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Mallick","name":"Mallick, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Kessler","name":"Kessler, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Ruehle","name":"Ruehle, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saravan","familyName":"Rajmohan","name":"Rajmohan, Saravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Budget-Aware Agentic Routing via Boundary-Guided Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-04T07:39:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) evolve into autonomous agents that execute long-horizon workflows, invoking a high-capability model at every step becomes economically unsustainable. While model routing is effective for single-turn queries, agentic routing is a sequential, path-dependent problem: early mistakes compound, feedback is often at the end of the episode, and deployments often demand strict per-task spending limits. We propose Budget-Aware Agentic Routing, which selects between a cheap and an expensive model at each step to optimize the cost--success frontier and to operate under strict per-task budgets. We propose Boundary-Guided Training, which leverages two boundary policies (always-small vs.\\ always-large) to build a difficulty taxonomy and to anchor learning under sparse rewards. Our approach warms start with boundary-guided SFT data synthesis via stratified sampling of cost-efficient trajectories, then applies Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization (BoPO), combining boundary-relative rewards with a reference-guided advantage to avoid degenerate cheap-failure solutions. Experiment results show that our method improves the efficiency frontier, matching strong routing baselines at substantially lower cost while demonstrating generalization to strict inference-time budget constraints. Overall, our work establishes a foundational framework for agentic routing, shifting the paradigm from static model selection to dynamic, budget-aware sequential decision-making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21227","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ezieddin","familyName":"Elmahjub","name":"Elmahjub, Ezieddin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junaid","familyName":"Qadir","name":"Qadir, Junaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah","familyName":"Mushtaq","name":"Mushtaq, Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafay","familyName":"Naeem","name":"Naeem, Rafay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Ghaznavi","name":"Ghaznavi, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waleed","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Waleed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IslamicLegalBench: Evaluating LLMs Knowledge and Reasoning of Islamic Law Across 1,200 Years of Islamic Pluralist Legal Traditions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T10:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As millions of Muslims turn to LLMs like GPT, Claude, and DeepSeek for religious guidance, a critical question arises: Can these AI systems reliably reason about Islamic law? We introduce IslamicLegalBench, the first benchmark evaluating LLMs across seven schools of Islamic jurisprudence, with 718 instances covering 13 tasks of varying complexity. Evaluation of nine state-of-the-art models reveals major limitations: the best model achieves only 68% correctness with 21% hallucination, while several models fall below 35% correctness and exceed 55% hallucination. Few-shot prompting provides minimal gains, improving only 2 of 9 models by \u0026gt;1%. Moderate-complexity tasks requiring exact knowledge show the highest errors, whereas high-complexity tasks display apparent competence through semantic reasoning. False premise detection indicates risky sycophancy, with 6 of 9 models accepting misleading assumptions at rates above 40%. These results highlight that prompt-based methods cannot compensate for missing foundational knowledge. IslamicLegalBench offers the first systematic framework to evaluate Islamic legal reasoning in AI, revealing critical gaps in tools increasingly relied on for spiritual guidance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript has been submitted for review to Artificial Intelligence \\\u0026amp; Law"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Hamdan","name":"Hamdan, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Dentamaro","name":"Dentamaro, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Pirlo","name":"Pirlo, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Cheriet","name":"Cheriet, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Architecture-Agnostic Curriculum Learning for Document Understanding: Empirical Evidence from Text-Only and Multimodal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T10:09:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate whether progressive data scheduling -- a curriculum learning strategy that incrementally increases training data exposure (33\\%$\\rightarrow$67\\%$\\rightarrow$100\\%) -- yields consistent efficiency gains across architecturally distinct document understanding models. By evaluating BERT (text-only, 110M parameters) and LayoutLMv3 (multimodal, 126M parameters) on the FUNSD and CORD benchmarks, we establish that this schedule reduces wall-clock training time by approximately 33\\%, commensurate with the reduction from 6.67 to 10.0 effective epoch-equivalents of data. To isolate curriculum effects from compute reduction, we introduce matched-compute baselines (Standard-7) that control for total gradient updates. On the FUNSD dataset, the curriculum significantly outperforms the matched-compute baseline for BERT ($Δ$F1 = +0.023, $p=0.022$, $d_z=3.83$), constituting evidence for a genuine scheduling benefit in capacity-constrained models. In contrast, no analogous benefit is observed for LayoutLMv3 ($p=0.621$), whose multimodal representations provide sufficient inductive bias. On the CORD dataset, all conditions converge to equivalent F1 scores ($\\geq$0.947) irrespective of scheduling, indicating a performance ceiling. Schedule ablations comparing progressive, two-phase, reverse, and random pacing confirm that the efficiency gain derives from reduced data volume rather than ordering. Taken together, these findings demonstrate that progressive scheduling is a reliable compute-reduction strategy across model families, with curriculum-specific benefits contingent on the interaction between model capacity and task complexity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuetao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinzhou","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make Every Draft Count: Hidden State based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T08:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding has emerged as a pivotal technique to accelerate LLM inference by employing a lightweight draft model to generate candidate tokens that are subsequently verified by the target model in parallel. However, while this paradigm successfully increases the arithmetic intensity of memory-bound inference, it causes significant compute inefficiency: the majority of draft tokens fail verification and are discarded, resulting in waste of computation. Motivated by the goal of recollecting this wasted computation, we propose a novel system that transforms discarded drafts into reusable tokens. Our key insight is to perform auto-regressive prediction at the hidden states level and postpone the integrating token information after the hidden states generation, so the draft hidden states are not contaminated by incorrect tokens, enabling hidden state reuse. To implement such a system, first we introduce a draft model architecture based on auto-regressive hidden states, which preserves richer semantics than token-based drafters to facilitate draft repurposing. Second, we design an efficient token information injection mechanism that leverages our specialized draft model to construct high-quality draft token trees and enables resampling tokens from verification failures. Third, we eliminate the overhead hidden in our design to further maximize hardware utilization. We conducted extensive evaluations against various baselines, demonstrating up to a 3.3x speedup against standard speculative decoding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riya","familyName":"Adsul","name":"Adsul, Riya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balachandra Devarangadi","familyName":"Sunil","name":"Sunil, Balachandra Devarangadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Nalawade","name":"Nalawade, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudharshan","familyName":"Govindan","name":"Govindan, Sudharshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Task-Aware LoRA Adapter Composition via Similarity Retrieval in Vector Databases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-01T22:20:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter efficient fine tuning methods like LoRA have enabled task specific adaptation of large language models, but efficiently composing multiple specialized adapters for unseen tasks remains challenging. We present a novel framework for dynamic LoRA adapter composition that leverages similarity retrieval in vector databases to enable zero-shot generalization across diverse NLP tasks. Our approach constructs a task-aware vector database by embedding training examples from 22 datasets spanning commonsense reasoning, question answering, natural language inference, and sentiment analysis. At inference time, we retrieve the most similar training examples, compute task similarity distributions via nucleus sampling, and dynamically merge relevant LoRA adapters using retrieval weighted fusion strategies. We evaluated four merging methods Linear, Concatenation, TIES, and Magnitude Prune demonstrating that our dataset centric retrieval approach often matches or exceeds the performance of individually fine-tuned task-specific adapters. Notably, Linear merging achieves 70.95% on PIQA and 77.62% on RTE, substantially outperforming single-task baselines (46% and 52%, respectively). Our framework requires no additional retriever training, operates with frozen embeddings, and enables efficient, interpretable adapter composition. These results suggest that retrieval based dynamic merging offers a promising direction for scalable, parameter-efficient multitask learning without requiring full model retraining for each new task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21221","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeju","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Context Compilation: Distilling Long Context into Compact Portable Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T08:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient long-context LLM deployment is stalled by a dichotomy between amortized compression, which struggles with out-of-distribution generalization, and Test-Time Training, which incurs prohibitive synthetic data costs and requires modifying model weights, creating stateful parameters that complicate concurrent serving. We propose Latent Context Compilation, a framework that fundamentally shifts context processing from adaptation to compilation. By utilizing a disposable LoRA module as a compiler, we distill long contexts into compact buffer tokens -- stateless, portable memory artifacts that are plug-and-play compatible with frozen base models. Crucially, we introduce a self-aligned optimization strategy that eliminates the need for synthetic context-relevant QA pairs. By regularizing context reconstruction task with context-agnostic random queries, we force compressed tokens to reside within the model's existing instruction-following manifold. Experiments with Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that Latent Context Compilation preserves fine-grained details and reasoning capabilities where prior methods falter, effectively decoupling memory density from model parameters even at a 16x compression ratio."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21221","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21220","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21220","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21220","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhadip","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Subhadip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Field-Theoretic Memory for AI Agents: Continuous Dynamics for Context Preservation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T04:33:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a memory system for AI agents that treats stored information as continuous fields governed by partial differential equations rather than discrete entries in a database. The approach draws from classical field theory: memories diffuse through semantic space, decay thermodynamically based on importance, and interact through field coupling in multi-agent scenarios. We evaluate the system on two established long-context benchmarks: LoCoMo (ACL 2024) with 300-turn conversations across 35 sessions, and LongMemEval (ICLR 2025) testing multi-session reasoning over 500+ turns. On LongMemEval, the field-theoretic approach achieves significant improvements: +116% F1 on multi-session reasoning (p\u0026lt;0.01, d= 3.06), +43.8% on temporal reasoning (p\u0026lt;0.001, d= 9.21), and +27.8% retrieval recall on knowledge updates (p\u0026lt;0.001, d= 5.00). Multi-agent experiments show near-perfect collective intelligence (\u0026gt;99.8%) through field coupling. Code is available at github.com/rotalabs/rotalabs-fieldmem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 6 figures. Code: https://github.com/rotalabs/rotalabs-fieldmem"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21220","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21217","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21217","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21217","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S M Ruhul","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, S M Ruhul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rifa","familyName":"Ferzana","name":"Ferzana, Rifa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Applied Sociolinguistic AI for Community Development (ASA-CD): A New Scientific Paradigm for Linguistically-Grounded Social Intervention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-30T23:12:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper establishes Applied Sociolinguistic AI for Community Development (ASA-CD) as a novel scientific paradigm for addressing community challenges through linguistically grounded, AI-enabled intervention. ASA-CD introduces three key contributions: (1) linguistic biomarkers as computational indicators of discursive fragmentation; (2) development-aligned natural language processing (NLP), an AI optimisation paradigm prioritising collective outcomes; and (3) a standardised five-phase protocol for discursive intervention. A proof-of-concept study, incorporating real-world and synthetic corpora, demonstrates systematic associations between exclusionary language and negative sentiment and simulates intervention-based improvements. ASA-CD provides a unified methodological, ethical and empirical framework for scalable, value-aligned AI in the service of community empowerment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables; simulation-based study introducing the ASA-CD framework"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21217","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhyar Rzgar K","familyName":"Rostam","name":"Rostam, Zhyar Rzgar K","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gábor","familyName":"Kertész","name":"Kertész, Gábor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EQ-5D Classification Using Biomedical Entity-Enriched Pre-trained Language Models and Multiple Instance Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-30T20:10:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The EQ-5D (EuroQol 5-Dimensions) is a standardized instrument for the evaluation of health-related quality of life. In health economics, systematic literature reviews (SLRs) depend on the correct identification of publications that use the EQ-5D, but manual screening of large volumes of scientific literature is time-consuming, error-prone, and inconsistent. In this study, we investigate fine-tuning of general-purpose (BERT) and domain-specific (SciBERT, BioBERT) pre-trained language models (PLMs), enriched with biomedical entity information extracted through scispaCy models for each statement, to improve EQ-5D detection from abstracts. We conduct nine experimental setups, including combining three scispaCy models with three PLMs, and evaluate their performance at both the sentence and study levels. Furthermore, we explore a Multiple Instance Learning (MIL) approach with attention pooling to aggregate sentence-level information into study-level predictions, where each abstract is represented as a bag of enriched sentences (by scispaCy). The findings indicate consistent improvements in F1-scores (reaching 0.82) and nearly perfect recall at the study-level, significantly exceeding classical bag-of-words baselines and recently reported PLM baselines. These results show that entity enrichment significantly improves domain adaptation and model generalization, enabling more accurate automated screening in systematic reviews."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:47Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21215","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21215","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21215","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Runyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonggang","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yonggang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inference-time Alignment via Sparse Junction Steering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-30T08:40:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Token-level steering has emerged as a pivotal approach for inference-time alignment, enabling fine grained control over large language models by modulating their output distributions without parameter updates. While effective, existing methods rely on dense intervention at every decoding step. This persistent manipulation not only incurs substantial computational overhead but also risks compromising generation quality by excessively drifting from the model's intrinsic distribution. In this work, we show that dense intervention is unnecessary and propose Sparse Inference time Alignment (SIA), which performs sparse junction steering by intervening only at critical decision points along the generation trajectory. Our key insight is that high entropy junctions mark pivotal decision points in the generation trajectory and are particularly susceptible to misalignment, indicating the need to introduce alignment related reward signals at these points. Extensive experiments across different model families and alignment objectives show that steering only 20% to 80% of tokens achieves superior alignment-efficiency trade offs. For strong base models such as Qwen3, intervening on as few as 20% of tokens matches or even surpasses heavily post-trained instruct models. This sparsity enables stronger guidance while better preserving the model's native distribution, integrates seamlessly with search based methods such as Best-of-N, and reduces computational cost by up to 6x."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21215","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohadeseh","familyName":"Sheikhqoraei","name":"Sheikhqoraei, Mohadeseh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zainabolhoda","familyName":"Heshmati","name":"Heshmati, Zainabolhoda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeinab","familyName":"Rajabi","name":"Rajabi, Zeinab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leila","familyName":"Rabiei","name":"Rabiei, Leila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Effective Multi-Domain Rumor Detection in Social Networks Using Domain-Gated Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-28T16:28:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social media platforms have become key channels for spreading and tracking rumors due to their widespread accessibility and ease of information sharing. Rumors can continuously emerge across diverse domains and topics, often with the intent to mislead society for personal or commercial gain. Therefore, developing methods that can accurately detect rumors at early stages is crucial to mitigating their negative impact. While existing approaches often specialize in single-domain detection, their performance degrades when applied to new domains due to shifts in data distribution, such as lexical patterns and propagation dynamics. To bridge this gap, this study introduces PerFact, a large-scale multi-domain rumor dataset comprising 8,034 annotated posts from the X platform, annotated into two primary categories: rumor (including true, false, and unverified rumors) and non-rumor. Annotator agreement, measured via Fleiss' Kappa ($κ= 0.74$), ensures high-quality labels.\n This research further proposes an effective multi-domain rumor detection model that employs a domain gate to dynamically aggregate multiple feature representations extracted through a Mixture-of-Experts method. Each expert combines CNN and BiLSTM networks to capture local syntactic features and long-range contextual dependencies. By leveraging both textual content and publisher information, the proposed model classifies posts into rumor and non-rumor categories with high accuracy. Evaluations demonstrate state-of-the-art performance, achieving an F1-score of 79.86\\% and an accuracy of 79.98\\% in multi-domain settings.\n Keywords: Rumor Detection, Multi-Domain, Natural Language Processing, Social Networks, Mixture-of-Experts Model"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takato","familyName":"Yasuno","name":"Yasuno, Takato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disaster Question Answering with LoRA Efficiency and Accurate End Position"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; K.4.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-28T01:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural disasters such as earthquakes, torrential rainfall, floods, and volcanic eruptions occur with extremely low frequency and affect limited geographic areas. When individuals face disaster situations, they often experience confusion and lack the domain-specific knowledge and experience necessary to determine appropriate responses and actions. While disaster information is continuously updated, even when utilizing RAG search and large language models for inquiries, obtaining relevant domain knowledge about natural disasters and experiences similar to one's specific situation is not guaranteed. When hallucinations are included in disaster question answering, artificial misinformation may spread and exacerbate confusion. This work introduces a disaster-focused question answering system based on Japanese disaster situations and response experiences. Utilizing the cl-tohoku/bert-base-japanese-v3 + Bi-LSTM + Enhanced Position Heads architecture with LoRA efficiency optimization, we achieved 70.4\\% End Position accuracy with only 5.7\\% of the total parameters (6.7M/117M). Experimental results demonstrate that the combination of Japanese BERT-base optimization and Bi-LSTM contextual understanding achieves accuracy levels suitable for real disaster response scenarios, attaining a 0.885 Span F1 score. Future challenges include: establishing natural disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A benchmark datasets, fine-tuning foundation models with disaster knowledge, developing lightweight and power-efficient edge AI Disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A applications for situations with insufficient power and communication during disasters, and addressing disaster knowledge base updates and continual learning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":306,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2602%2A%20AND%20registered%3A%5B2026-02-26T01%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202026-02-27T01%3A00%3A00Z%5D%20AND%20(language%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20MoE%20OR%20inference%20OR%20training%20OR%20reinforcement%20OR%20distillation)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}